      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Ito</head>
        <p>Ito</p>
        <p>• Nothing doing! That's about the position in loco, football circles. After Warrington's thu.e defeats in seven days comes a blank date, so they won't lose this afternoon!</p>
        <p>Why there should be no fixture on the Saturday before Christmas is a thing that warts an explabation. I t seems to me that a go d gate has Leen lost, but maybe it is a dispensation of Providence after all.</p>
        <p>The Christmas programme is none too gaudy either, 'Leigh at home is so old standing dish which requires spice. So much for Christmas Day. On Boxing-Day, the Wirepullers travel to Oldham, where they will probably get more spice than they want. On Saturday, the 28th, Leeds are due at Wilderspool, and the chances are about even that they follow the example of HuddErsfield and Wakefield and take away the points. Then comes the year-end, and some of us won't be sorry.</p>
        <p>Perchance 'Warrington will improve in 1913, though there are no stars on the horizon to lead the weary follower on his way. Last week, in discussing the Saturday matchee. the " Daily News " remarked casually that Broughton Bangets were at home to " the feeble Warrington team." That struck me as being about "the limit." " Ti:e feeble Warrington team." Well ! well ! "It's coming to surnmat " !</p>
        <p>Naturally, there was not a big crowd to witness the contest between the Bangers and the feeble ones. And those who stayed away probably had the best after- noon. It was a feeble sort of match, and even the winners did not strike one as football giants by any means. Warrington were stubborn, and only allowed the Broughton men to score twice. For themselves they- were nilletl. and barring accidents, they would still be nilled of they had ben playing yet.</p>
        <p>The chief deficiency of the Wirepullers seems to be pace. Since they met Huddersfield they have gone slower. There were several cccasions on Saturday when a speedy threequarter might have gone through the defence, but neither Clare nor .Iclntyre could cover the ground fast enough. Brown and Daintith were an improvement at half back, and the youngster pleased everybody by his nippiness and persistence. He wasn't content with merely passing the ball ; i.e followed it all the time and revealed exceptional tackling powers for one of his physique. Alec Brown may yet fill a big gap.</p>
        <p>Dickenson got into the wars, and has joined Brooks in the knee hospital. His fellow centre. Traater,did a lot of good work on the defence, but he seems to have !ost his attacking power. Like the others he has gone lower, hangs back, and does too much punting. It seems to me that our only hope is in Renwick, who is down for a trial with the "A" team to-day. The skipper has been badly missed, and if Ito cannot pull the team together the outlook will be dismal in the extreme. Let us hope that he will soon find the form which distinguished him at the close of last season.</p>
        <p>Broughton had a big pull forward on Saturday, and emphasised the fact that, good as the Warrington acrummsgers are, they are short of weight. In mid- field play the deficiency i not so apparent, hut when the " scrums" are formed mar either goal-line, you have only to take note on which side the ball comes out to see what chances Warrington are missing. A pack that can get the ball rear the line uces a long way towards winning the match. AI e there no " founds " to be had in Yorkshire or Cumberland?</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For. Acat</p>
        <p>Per-</p>
        <p>_ P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. ent'gP. Hud'fleld l4 14 0 0... 44 75 316...16 19 89...1(0 80 Wakefield Tl 4 10 2 2... 31 51 215...20 32 136... 78 57 Runalet 13 10 3 0... 37 53 233...26 19 109... 7692 Hull K. 16 14 10 3 1... 47 50 244...30 33 159... 7560 Wigan l2 0 3 0... 39 55 243...17 22 100... 7530 Broughton 10 6 2 2... 21 29 129...11 II 64... 7000 oewslury 13 9 4 0... 33 64 253...25 25 125... 69.23 Salford l2 8 4 0... 18 '22 102...10 18 74... 6606 Oldham IL 6 4 1... 28 34 158...22 16 92... 5909 Widnes ll 6 4 1... 19 30 123...20 23 109... 59119 Swinton 13 7 5 1... 24 31 141...10 19 77... 57•69 Bradford l3 6 6 1... 21 20 102...16 21 104... 50 00 Whdale 11 11 5 6 0... 92 32 140...16 23 101... 4545 Halifax 13 5 7 1... -17 30 124...25 35 155... 42'30 Hull l3 5 7 1... 15 20 90...24 38 162... 42.30 Leeds.. St Helens 13 5 7 L.. 22 20 131...25 31 143... 4230 Batley l2 5 7 0... 25 23 119...19 25 113... 4166 Warrington 14 5 8 1... 11 16 70...21 28 126... 3928 York l3 5 8 0... 19 20 128...27 38 168... 38.46 Leigh 12 4 8 0.- 9 17 09...15 25 105... 33 33 Runcorn l5 5 10 0... 22 27 125...32 53 223... 23 33 Bramley -14 3 11 0... 17 20 91...38 67•277... 21,12 Barrow l9 `") 9 1... 9 14 60...28 30 104... 2083. Reighley lO 1 9 0... 11 10 52...2a 38 166... 10 00 Coventry l3 012 1... 16 20 92...57 09 411... 3'84</p>
        <p>To-day the " A " team will probably bare matters all their own way in a friendly with Prest%%ich. Two or three local juniors are to have a trial, and Renwick's presence should ensure a welcoming crowd. Please note that the young St. Helens wing, Manchester, is off the map : why he ever had a trial puzzles me. He didn't appear able to run, and I think a wing threequarter's paces might be tested before he is thrown into combination football. The " A "team were lucky to heat the Rangers "A " and carry a passenger. A great deal was due to Critlchley's fine goil kicking.</p>
        <p>The selected thirteen for to-day is Caldwell, full back ; Wehsby, Hudson, Renwick and Davies, three- quarters; A Brown and Douglas, half backs ; Carless, Bevan, Critchley, Harrner,Naylor and Arnold forwards.</p>
        <p>since the team was selected Warrington have madP arrangements to give a trial to-day to " Wax " Williams, the Halifax wing threequarter. At his best Williams was a very speedy and effective player, and he might be of use to Warrington.</p>
        <p>Lancashire football is at a low ebb just now, and the County Championship has deservedly gone to Yorkshire, who beat the Palatines at Oldham on Monday by 20 points to 8. Only five or six years ago Lancashire football was supreme, and for years there were no backs in Yorkshire who could compare with Fish, Wilson, T in S Williams and the two Lees of Oldham. The bust is on the other leg now, and for the quintessence of scientific back play we have to ga to Wagstaffe and his merry men. Lancashire's (and incidentally Warrington's) turn will come again.</p>
        <p>It is a long time since Yorkshire won on Lancashire soil, and I happen to remember the occasion. The match was played at New Barnes, Salford, and Tom Broadley and F W Cooper made backs of the Lanca- shire team, which included Hoskins and Williams at three-quarter, and It Batey (Widnes) at full buck. The Tykes were only beginning then to abandon the old style for the new—which they have perfected to-day.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. The fixtures are as follows :7._ _</p>
        <p>Leeds 0 Halifax Widnes Huddersfleld Keighl.ey Batley Coventry — Dewsbury — Wigan St Helens 27 Rochdale Hornets Barrow l6 Salford Hull Kingston R Swinton Leigh -</p>
