      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>Warrington Lose at Broughton.</head>
        <p>Warrington Lose at Broughton.</p>
        <p>The return match between Warrington and Broughton Rangers was played at Broughton on Saturday. Both teams were as selected, but there was only an attendance of about 2,000 and the game was of an uninteresting character. ‘Varrington gave a trial at halt-back to A Brown and Daintitb, and both men gave a good account of themselves. The teams were :</p>
        <p>-Warrington—Jolley, back ; Clare, Dickenson, Tranter and Mclntyre,threequarter-backs ; Daintith and Brown half-backs ; U Tbotuas, R Thomas, Cheaters, Skelhorne, Fearnley and Cox, forwards. Broughton Rangers—Danson, back; Scott, Barnes, Barlow and Craven, threequarter-backs : Jones and Tighe, half-hacks; Winskill, Cheetham, Gorry, Hurst, Roberts and J L Clarupitr, forviards. Referee, Mr B Ennion, Wigan ; touch-judges, Messrs, R Wailing (Widnes) and W Hart (Oldham). Toe Rangers set the ball in motion and Mclntyre made a powerlul response to the opening kick. Warrington attacked, and the two halves got nicely away from the scrummage, but the movement broke down among the threequarters. The home forwards used their weight to some purpose and Warrington were forced back to the line. Tighe broke away from a scrum and passed to Clampitt who scored easily, Barlow failing with a goal. , Tranter Daintith and Cox responded with a smart run to the other end, but could not pass Denson, whose clearances were a feature. The Warrington backs combined grandly in the Rangers half, but speed was lacking and Denson forced Mclntyre into touch. A penalty to the Raneers enabled Barlow to add a goal. Warrington still struggled hard and Clare pulled Cheat- ham down near the line. The position was an excellent one for attack, however, and the Rangers securing possession a series of neat transfers sent bcott over the line. No goal resulted. Half time : Rangers S points, Warrington nil. The second half saw Warrington put op a plucky fight. Brown was untiring behind the pack and irequently troubled the home defence, while Mclntyre was again all but over after good passing. Dickenson was injured and had to retire. Thomas missed a couple of penalty shots at goal, but Warrington fully held their own, and there was no more scoring. Final : Broughton Rangers, 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) ; Warring- ton, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>THE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRING " A " v BROUGHTON " A."---Played at Wilcerspool before 2,000 spectators. Both aides were well representEd, the visitors including several first- tearders.Warrington opened in vigorous style and it was not long before a forward rush left the Rangers full back beaten, Critchley scoring near the posta and also adding a goal. Broughton improved and Davidson made great efforts but the borne tackling was very sute.A smart bit of work by Nicholas and Jordan took play to the visiting line again and Davies slipped over at the corner in a general scramble for the tall. Critchley added another fine goal, Half-time : Warrington • A," 10 points ; Broughton Rangers, nil. In the second half the Rangers made good use of the wind, and after Davidson had kicked a penalty goal, the forwards profited by a weak clearance on the home line, and Clampitt scored, for Davidson to improve. Keen plea followed, and the result was in doubt until Jordan followed up it long kick, and touched down tinder the full back's feet. The Rangers' custodian was caught napping, and when Critchley added another goal from long range the locals were in a safe position. Final :—Werrington " A," 3 goals, 3 tries (15 points) ; Broughton Rangers, 2 goals, 1 try (7 points).</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RYLANDS' RECREA LION v. ASHTON. These teams tried conclusions at Ashton on Saturday' on a heavy ground before 2,000 spectators. The first half, considering the state of the ground, produced some good football, hylands' doing most of the press- ing. They could not break through the home defence,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OUR IMPS SPORTS CARTOON.</head>
        <p>OUR IMPS SPORTS CARTOON.</p>
        <p>IMPS AT WINTER SPORT.</p>
        <p>however, the only score being a dropped goal by A Booth. Half-time arrived with the score two points in favour of the " Whet-nen." Resuming, Ashton were soon pressing the " Wiremen," but Brarnhall relieved. Rylands forwards, ably led by A Stretford, although giving weight away, ..ere often masters of 'the pack and continued their progress towards their opponents' goal, and from a penalty for Obstruction Potter landed a goal. From a miskick by Rylands, Etherington picked up smartly, and, racing along in fine style until challenged by Bramhall, passed inside, for Williams to score. The goal kick failed.. From a pack formed on the line Leah attempted to kick, hut tie ball was charged down by Civil, a bo, dropping on the ball, scorld Ashton's second try, from which he also kicked the goal, making the score eight points to four in favour of Ashton. TO-DAY'S TEAM'.</p>
        <p>_ RYLANDS RECS. V. SALFORD ST. BARTS.—At Salford. Full bans, F Horrocks ; threequarter backs, J Griffiths, W Leah, A Horrocks, and J Barton ; half-backs, J. Gallop, and J Warren ; forwards, A Stretford, J Brand- wood. F Mason, H Morris, A Booth and B Slater. Reserve J Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>Upon the resurnntion the game was of a more even nature. Birmingham (Latchford)• cleared when sur- rounded. At the other end Shakeshaft had hard lines after a fine individual effort. Uive-and-take play followed. Latchford added a further p.A01., whilst Altrincham this half scored twice. Final : Latchford, four goals ; Altrincham, two goals, Harrowhy Wallasey Rovers Sandhach Ramblers Bollingten Lostock Gralsm 4 Bowdon Vale I Latchford 4 Altrincham</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ASsOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASsOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. FIRST DIYISIOI4. RESULTS TO SATURDAY. Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. Fo-r-A-g;st Pte Lostces Oral= 7... 7... 0... 0... 25... 5... 14 1..y01m 7. 4 1. 2... 13... 12... 10 Alobbarley 9.. 4... 3... 2... 14... 11... 10 Broad heat h Bowdon Vale Oughtrington Park 6... 3... 1,.. 2... 8... 5... 8 Barntou Villa Latchford St Vincent's 9 2 7. 0.. 9.. 2... 4 A Itrincham Lads 8 0. . 5. 3 . 11... 19... 3 Oughtringtou Arnat 8... 1... 6... 1... 14... 25... 3</p>
        <p>BROADIIEATII V. ST. VINCENT'S. At Navigation-road on Saturday. Final : Brcadheath, two; St. Vincent's nil.</p>
        <p>MORBERLEY V OUGHTRINGTON AMATEURS. At Mobberley on Saturday. In the opening stages both goalkeepers were tested kiit were safe. After a spell of midfield play, a good effort by Woodmck was frustrated by Rouse, who cleared magnificently when hard pressed. Jones, on the visitors' left, put in several nice runs and centres, but the home cetence prevailed. After an even first half the interval arrived with the score-sheet blank. Matters ruled much the same in the second half, the chief feature being some capital half- back play by Wood and Owen on the Amateurs' side— they repeatedly checked the home forwards, The result, a goalless draw, was a very fitting ending.</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. RESULTS TO SLTURDAY. P. W. L. D. for agst Pts Weaver Refining Co 7... 4... 0... 3... 17... 5... 11 Stockton Heath Latchford Recs Padgate Amateurs Cadishead Wesleyan 6. 3... 1... 2... 13... 9... 8 Latchford Reserve 6... 1... 3... 2._ 9... 9... 4 Latchford Britannia 5... 2... 3... 0... 8... 14... 4 Warrington OLB WEAVER REFINING CO. V PADGATE AMATEURS. These teams met at Acton Bridge on Saturday, it being expected that the m.,tch would have had an important hearing upon the destination of this season's championship cup. The visitors immediately attacked but were repulsed, and th. home team opened their account about fifteen minutes from the start. The visitors were a long time getting used to the ground, being guilty of many mistakes. The Refiners shot at every conceivable opportunity and again had the satis- faction of scoring. At hull-time the Refiners led by two goals to nil. Restarting, the Amateurs still kept to the short passion gains, which wasnot at all suitable to the ground. The home team displaying splendid stamina, obtained two morn goals, and rata out deserving winners. Result : Weaver Refining Co., four goals ; Padgate Amateurs, nil.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH X' WARRINGTON C.L.B. At Stockton Heath. This return engagement pro- duced the best exhibition given by the Warrington C.L.H. this s•-ason. At their first meeting the Heath won 5-0. The Brigade contested every inch of the ground, their defence being particularly good. At the interval Stockton Heath led by two goals to nil. In the second portion the game continued to be well fought out, hut both defences held firm, and whew time wai called Stocktoh Heath had gained the verdict by two goals to nil.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RECREATION v CADISHEAD WESLEYANS. At Latchford. The second meeting of these , two clubs again resulted in a close and well-fought game. At their previous meeting the lA'esleyans won by 3-2. They once more emphasized their superiority, and although the game took place at Latchfor.d they gained both pOints as on the previous occasion. Final Cadishead Wesleyaus, two goals; Latchford Recreation, One gun'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. FOURTH ROUND.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. FOURTH ROUND.</p>
        <p>- LATCHFORD V ALTRINCHAM. At Lltchford. The home team att.icked strongly, and Siddall opened their account with a good drive. Encouraged with their success, the home team forged ahead, and when Shakeshaft added a second point it was fully deserved. Nowithstanding 11011a8 capital defensive tactics by Watkins and Richmond, the visitors' backs, Latchford maintained the upper band• and at the interval led by three goals to nil.</p>
        <p>111•111 M...-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</head>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>WIDNES ST PAUL'S v. GREAT SANKEY.—PIayed at Widnes on Saturday. From the outset the visitors pressed. E Forster, receiving, hit the post with a fast shot, but T Cook gave them the lead from the rebound. IThe locals broke away on the left, but B J Kelly 'cleared his lines. E Darlington raced away on the right. and gave to T Whitfield, who gave T Warne no chance with a terrific shot. The visitors kept their opponents penned in their own half, and would surely have scored had not tho home hack handed the ball out. F. Forster took the ensuing kick, and made no mistake with a fast rising shot. Sankey forwards were in great form; and T Cook added a fourth after a rush in the goalmonth. W Booth put in a splendid centre, which E Darlington just failed to convert. The latter made amends a few minntes later by giving Sankey their fifth goal. Interval : Great Sankey, 5 ; Widnes St Paul's, 0 Resuming, the locals tried hard to get ahead, but E Glover sent them to the rightabout with a huge Lunt. Fine passing ena bled the visitors to get possession in the home half, and after E Darlington had missed the post by inches, T Cook put Sankey further ahead. Parkinson, the Saints' "centre half, put his forwards in possesion, but they were well watched by Glover, Millington ..nd Payne. The visitors forced a corner, which was nicely placed. The ball went out to W Boo' h, who scored a seventh from a difficult angle. In the last minute E Darlington centred for E Forster to score a splendid goal. Result : Great San key, 8 ; Widnes St Paul's, nil.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. FAIRFIELD UNITED V NAYLOR'S. Fairfield United, one of our leading junior clubs played a very clever game against Naylor's team on Saturday last when the latter team were beaten by 5 goals to 3. Fairfield owe their success to the fine shooting and dribbling of both Appleton and Simkin. The goals were divided between Appleton and Sirukin each securing 2, the remaining goal being put in by one of the opposing backs. For Naylore, Collins scored all thri e.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S TEAMS, WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE, GREAT SANKEY V' WIDNES RECREATION.— At Penketh Recreation. To-day Great Sankey Team, Davenport, goal ; B J Kelly, E Glover, full backs ; E Glover. F Millington, H Payne, half-backs ; E Darling- ton, T Whitfield, T Cook, E Forater,W Booth,for wards. Reserves: R Clatwortloy, H Ray. Kick-off 230 p.m. • HOCKEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SANKEY LADIES v. FARN WORTH LADIES.</head>
        <p>SANKEY LADIES v. FARN WORTH LADIES.</p>
        <p>Sankey Ladies entertained the strong Farnworth team on Saturday and were well beaten by 7 goals to 1. The forward play of the visiting ladies was exceed- ingly good, and the scorers were : Misses Gray (2), Joynson (2), Cozier, Hutchinson and Martindale. The scorer for Sankey was Miss Dodds. LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven lest 24 to the Y.M.C.A. last week and on the afternoon's play the visitors deserved to win. They are an enthusiastic side and were very Pleated at their first success over a Latchford eleven. Latchford opened the game well and a centre from the left was finely converted by Pea-sou. The visitors then attacked and an equaliser came from the right wing followed by what proved to be the winning goal, in the second half the born* team had a big share of the play but failed to get the ball in the net. The Y.M.C. A. left back and centre-half played strongly while for the home side Fletcher and Billington at baok were the best. The Second Elevens met at Manchester and Latchford won easily by 4-1. The first half saw the sides some- what evenly matched and the score was a goal each at the interval. After that the visitors got the upper hand and good all-round play brought three more goals to their score. The visitors defence was too good for the Y.NI.C.A. forwards. The goals for Latchford were scored by Minards (2), Hobin end Patch, ode each. The First Eleven v Mid-Cheshire at home this after- noon is Brubl, goal; W Fletcher C Billington, backs; A E Bradtbaw, J B Williioson, A Knieht, halves ; R A Dale, J E Pearson, W H Minards, E Banks, H it Colton, forwards, Bull 3 -AT 230 p.m. The Second Eleven have no match.</p>
        <p>£350 CtiEQUE FOR WIDNES INSTITUTE. At a meeting of the Church Council in connection with St. Mary's Church, Widnes, on Tuesday evening, the vicar (the Rev. Stephen Jones) intimated that Mr Harry S Timrnis, son of the late Mr H Sutton Timinis, a great benefactor of Church work, had sent a cheque or £350 to defray the existing debt on the Young Men's Institute in Hutchinson-street. The heavy debt had been caused through the enlargement of the premises, the provision or billiard tables, and fixtures to make the place acceptable to members. Recently a bazaar realised £215, out of which some £5O was granted to wipe off part of the debt, but, in view of Mr. Timmis's munificenco, that £5O bas been banded back to the Church Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>HEXT WEEK: THE IMPS' STEEPLECHASE.</head>
        <p>HEXT WEEK: THE IMPS' STEEPLECHASE.</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING ASSOCIATION. (N.A. CENTRE).</p>
        <p>The committee has decided to hold the annnal meet- ing on January 91b, 1913, when all prize monies will be paid out along with other business. . .</p>
        <p>r Mr T Roberts, proprietor of the Bay Horse Hotel, Winwick-street, has decided to hold an Angling Club at his house. All members w!w intend joining this society should hand in their names to Mr J Potter or write them down on the list at the Bay Horse. We ' need hardly say that the Bay Horse Club is affiliating to the Northern Anglers as it is pretty well known about town. There are rumours of another society falling in with the Northern anglers but up to the pre- sent DO official news is at hand.</p>
        <p>We hear that several members ar going to spend the Christmas holidays at Melvely and Chirk if the weather is at all favourable.</p>
        <p>GENERAL SECRETARY'S REPORT FOR SEASON 1912.</p>
        <p>"In presenting their annual report your Execu- tive Council have regretfully to announce a serious decrease in the membership, the total now amounting to 8,238 against 9,096 last yesr, a loss of 858 members. The unsettled state of the labour world is probably responsible for tne decrease, and may be considered as only a passing depression. The Liverpool secession, however, is a more serious matter for schich no ar!equate reason has been received. It has been rumoured that the Liverpool centre thinks itself strong enough to stand alone. At any rate no other explanation has been mooted. If this is really the motive, it is a very short-sighted one and contrary to the general maxim that " Union is strength." This great combination of angling societies —the Northern Anglers Association—has hitherto befn able to speak with united voice to railway companies, canal proprietors, landowners, Conservancy Boards and even to the Government Board of Agriculture,and Fisheries and it cannot be disputed that every concession in the way of cheap railway tares, cheap fishing privileges, improved fishery regulations, and reduced licence duties within the Northern area, are due to the initiation and energetic action of the Northern Anglers Association. Separated we are help- less and of no account; united we have been powerful. While expressing our regret that Liverpool is no longer with us in the great struggle for the benefit of anglers in general, we are in no way downhearted. The slump is over and brighter prospects are in sight.' A</p>
      </div>
