      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>POLICE DINNER.</head>
        <p>POLICE DINNER.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF MEDALLIONS AND CERTIFICATES FOR SWIMMING. On Thursday evening the members of the Borough Police Life Saving Class met at dinner at the Patten Arms Hotel. The chair was occupied by Mr Martin Nicholls, Chief Constable, and there were also present His Worship the Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph). Alderman Bolton, chairman of the Town Hall,Parks and Baths Committee, Mr A T Hallaway, deputy town clerk, Mr H Spicks, assistant town clerk, Mr A Barlow, baths superinteudent, Mr W Parsons, assistant magistrates' clerk, Mr J Ashton, instructor to the class, Chief- Inspector Williamson, Inspector Stuart and some twenty-five meinnels of the Force. An excellent dinner was purveyed by Mr Burleigh, and after full justice was done to the same the Chief Constable proposed the usual loyal toasts which were duly honoured. Mr NICHOLLS next explained the objects of the Royal Lite Saving Society, stating that the members of the Force had quite voluntarily taken up the work, formed a class, and put in a lot of practice at the baths the result being that four men had won the Royal Society's medallion and certificate and ten others the certificate. Mr Nicholls then asked the Mayor to present the awards as follows : Aledallion and certificate: Sergeant Woodland and P.C.'s Barr' Coombs and Gibson. Certificates : Chief Inspector William- son Sergeant Woollacott, Detective Craig end P.C.'s Corbett, Bowles, Mortara', Andrews, Skelton, Grubb and Green. An Instructor's certificate was also presented to Mr J Ashton.</p>
        <p>_ After the presentations the Mayor solid that he did not know any nody or men who had developed and improved to a greater extent than the Police Force in this country. He remembered the time when the Police Force were held up to good humoured criticism and ridicule. He thought the Police had so far developed and improved themselves mentally and physically that they were of use not only in the administration of justice, but in looking after the safety and welfare of the community in which they erestationed. In the regulation of street traffic they also rendered an important duty. Another feature which struck him wss the work of the Ambulance section. The mariner in which they discharged this duty was in his cpinion equal to the attention received in large hospitals. There were many other things in which the police had improved their position. With regard to life saving, their latest development, it was a department which reflected great credit on all concerned. He was pleased to hear from the Chief Constable that their action was quits volun- tary. 1 heir latest development was to improve their powers of helping others. If they continued, he had Do doubt they would become one of the most important institutions in the country. (Applause). Later the CHIEF CONSTABLE proposed the health of His Worship the Mayor which was enthusiastically drunk. The MA YOR in responding said it was very important that they should have a good and conscientious police force, who had many opportunities of serving the public, and it wan Jar everyone who hal any influence to further their (the police) interests in every way. The Chef Constable also proposed the health of Alder- man Bolton,alluding to the good service he had rendered the police es Chairman of the Baths Committee in obtaining for the life saving class the use of the baths for training. Aldermen Bolton in re.urning thanks said when he found they had desired facilities in order to practice swimming, he had been glad to endeavour to get such for them at the baths free, seeing they were public servants who desired to make themeelves more efficient.</p>
        <p>At the request of the Chief Constable thl Mayor, on behalf the life saving clas3 presented to Air J Ashton a silver cigarette case, suitably inscribed, as a token of recognition of his services in acting as instructor, in which capacity he had given his time and instruction entirely free, bringing the class to a high state of efficiency. . .</p>
        <p>- During the evening an enjoyable programme of songs and duets, 'sere rendered by Messrs J Willett, E Walker, and P Woollacott, and Sergeant Woodland and P.C.'s Gibson and Wood, Mr P Smith acting as accom- panist, whilst Mr II M Ashton contributed some humorous items in his mood inimitable style. At the cicse tha healtta of the Chief ConstaMe was proposed by Nlr A '1" HALLAWAY and heartily drunk, with musical honours, Mr Nrcriot.t.s responding. An enjoyable function closed with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
