        <p>The Examiner Saturday December 21, 1912.</p>
        <p>superior authority. That being so, had the Education Committee power to rescind it ? The CH AIRM AN answered that be had consulted with the Law Clerk on the point, and his opinion was that the matter was not at present within the purview of that committee, loccause the resolution had been adopted by and had become part of the pro- ceedings of the Education Authority (the Town Council) and that the proper course would be for the Eduction Authority, if it thought fit, to rescind the minute and send it back for consideration. In view of the remarks made at the last Council meeting he felt extremely reluctant to appear to wish to burk discus- sion on the question. There was no question at all about it, se far as th:ire might be any value in the motion if the committee did pass it, that except for its moral effect on the Education Committee, it would be absolutely inoperative. Councillor JENKINS : Then if we begin talking it is simply talking against time? The CIiNIKM AN remarked that it wis purely a gees- tion of policy as to whether the Education Committee thought it desirable in view of the discussion which hid already taken place in the Council, that all the facts should be Again discussed. A good deal had been slid by Councillor Pemberton about his being a busy num. Surely there were others who were busy also,and some thought it was not necessary to do business two or three times by pursuing a policy of carrying a res.lu- tion on 3 day and upsetting it the next. He was abso- lutely sick and weary of the methods which were adopte lie certain quarters in coensction with public busini sa. hstead of making progress it wve going a foot to-day and going back two feet to-morrow. Tbat was the policy that was sought to be inati3ur tted there, and as far be was concerned, as chairman of the com- mittee, he did not wish to encourage it. Cnuncillor AuEfiErt " to test th 3 feelings of the meeting" seconded the motion. Thee was a principle involved, and that was that in the distribution of public work every contract should be open to competition. Co incillor Dr MckeP., in Kipp)! tin; the motion, thought there was not exactly fair pl.), in regard to the letting out of the work. If they were going to advertise in one ins!anoe let them advertis , in all cases. The present methol cettainly savoured of knob- sticking. Why was not the fact pelniicly announced that it was tha in nation to nvrgo the three schools into Ono contract? If they des:reyed c mpetition they might gain for oaoe, bat they were g 'bog to lose in the long run. Mr Pa.titeLouou explained that ha was the proposer of the minue un her discussion at the last meeting a the Education Committee, He called special attention</p>
        <p>to it in the preserce of Councillor Pemberton, who never raid a %Turd. Mr Fairclough entered upon a detailed criticism of the attitude of Cc.uncillor Pemb2rton on educational matters,with special reference to the school site in Le-ely-line. A special sub-com- mittee spent a considerable time in lo 'king for a s lit- able site, but Councillor Pemberton " arried on '' to such al extent about the'r work that Alderman J Ames Evan; an i Couacillor Wilkinson retired in disgust. When the questi on was disc issed ia the Council, Coma- cill or Pemberton made a most insemp2r ite speech about the offer of Lord Nestor), the owner of the land. Whoa a deputatioa wont to inteivie v Mr Leslie, Lord Newton's agent, Mr Leslie tuned t ) a cou its of dis- cmsions in the Council. He read out Corncillor Penolonton's speeches and refused to have an)thing to do with him. (Lau4hter.) Ho then read out Alder- man B Atiou'a s,)eemes an! deck) id to have aily thing to do wi:h him. " Men Mr Leslis real out my speech in the Elocution Com nittee," said Mr Fairclough, "and he said,' i agree with every word of it.' I was the bnly mill he would talk to, except Mr Longdin and Mr Murray. Iff hid been in Councillor Peruberton's plac., I should have felt I wanted to creep under the table." (Laught-ir.) Mr Pemberton had at all times been criticising thorn an I constantly submitt*ng that they were paying too much money to architects, and if there had been auy knob-sticking with regard to arohitects in the town Councillor Pemberton was the greatest dalinq -eat. The reason they approached Messrs Wright and Ilaealyn was because tbey had done tho'r work excellently and because they were first in open competition. It was thereore thought that it would be advantageous to the ratepayers if the firm were allowed to carry out the three schools. When the matter was before the Education Committee Councillor Pemberton sat there like a dummy without saying a word, but when he saw the opp ortunity of making an exhibition beiore the whole town he brought the matter forward. He supposed it was for notoriety. It could tot be for au3 thing else ; it could not he that he was doing it for the good of the riitep lers. Councillor EIENSIIALL, said he was one of the noble band who, in the first instance, along with Couneillor Pemberton, tried to keep the bridge in the interests of economy and failed. He was not allowed to help to save £1,500, by the appointment of a permanent architect, so when he saw a chance of saving £lO9 he went for it. He agreed with the premises of these who supported the motion, but dkagreed with their penclusione. LIE there were 50 competitions, loc.l architects would not compete under the existing con- ditions. Consequently the arrangement sas not an injustice to any local arohitect. Mr JaitataN asked if it was not .a .fact that no</p>
        <p>ber of the Royal Institute of British Architects; v7cUaid have been allowed to compete on the 31 per cent terms? Tha CHAT ftgaN ; It is a fact. Mr JARMAN said the scheduled terms of the institute were five per cent, on plans and 2 per cent, on bills of quantities. lie- took it that if, at the meeting of the Sites and Build ings Committee, Councill Jr Pemberton had wished to satisfy himself that there was nothing under•hani, he could have called for the letter from the architects to be read The fact that the two singlu bleole schools would be identical with one of the block of the Oakwcod Avenue school would entitle the committee to a rebate, as there would be practically no new plans and quanti- ties to get out. Thera was, therefore, no fear or knob- sticking, while, on the other hand, nothing could have bean gained by open competition, Or tslcKfac asked why wig not a clause inserted in the advertisement for the Oak wood-avenua Schools, saying that possibly the architects would be asked to provide the designs for two other schools ? The CHAIRMAN replied that no such proceeding was contemplated at the time. C.auncillor PEMBERTON replied to the discussien. Having expressed a 4 :übt as to whether the same pleas would be equally adaptable to both sites' he referred to the positioa which might arise if the Board of Education rejecled the plsns. The CriatesmaN; We will discuss that when it arises. I must ask you to confine your remarks to the motion. I have already given 303 considerable latitude. Councillor PEMBERTON: You allusvrel Mr Fairclough latitude to go on talking for a quarter of an hour mak- ings personal attack on me—a point which had nothing to do with the discussion,</p>
        <p>The Cuarnm.tN You'have had over half an hour, and your pint has nothing to oda with the dleussion. Councillor PfMIRTON: I don't agree with you. The CITAIRMAN : It is not likely that you would. Counoili4 ThrtmogrtToN : Oh, yes it. is. I hare very often agreed with you. For a man to get up like Mr Fairolough did and make a personal attack on me with regord to an interview with L9rd Newton's agent is no vgumeot in answer to the question I have laid befoi e yen,The CHAIRMAN: 1 am not disputing your right to reply to that at ail. What I was calling your attention to was in referenco to. the Board of Edscation not</p>
        <p>approving the plans, and that a certain situation might arise if they did not. I said we will discuss that when it arises. If you wida to reply to Mr Fairclougn's remarks, do so. Pewittor P4/1113ERTON said ha did not feel at all unconafortable bef,re Mr Leslie, whom he thought was very nice. (Laughter.) Us thought he was going to give him (Counedlor Pemberton) a great big kn ,ck,but be met it all right. " I had some wrong information on OLIO point," explained () auncillor Pemberton," and I apologised. If I make a mistake I hope I shall ' always do the right thing, like anybody else ought to do. If we had been firmer we should have got better terms."</p>
        <p>Mr WALKER wished to point out that the architects in carr3ing out that their contract, were not under- cutting fellow architects. They were acting on standard terms.</p>
        <p>_ Cot=lllor PEM BERTON said if that were ao,it made the position worse. If they were receiving standard rates, theymust be getting five per cent.lt had repeatedly been stated that the committee were effecting is saving of £4OO, but it now .transpired that there was no raving at all. _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : What Mr Walker said was that the architects were not undercutting other architects If you paid close attention to what Mr Jarman said you would have seen the true explanation. Councillor PEMBERTON said that in spite of what Mr Jarman had said svarate quantities would have to he prepared unle3s the thr,e schools were included in one contract.</p>
        <p>Further comments by Councillor Pemberton drew from Alderman SMETHURST the protest that he was delivering the same speech. Councillor PEMBERTON said he was answeriog Mr Fairclough when he inferred that Messrs Wright and Hamlvo built the best school. Messrs Wright, Ciirnett and Wright built that school and it was only fair that thy should receive credit for it. He was arguing the case of the twenty local archtects who originally competed. lhe CtiartuiteN, in winding up the debate, said it was unfortunate that the Chairman of the Sites and Build- ings Committee had not been present at either of the meetings at which the question had been debated. lie (the chairman) defended the minute at the Council meeting,out of loyalty to the Sites end Buildings Com- mittee, although he might add that the decision of that committee commended itself to his judgment. At the are time he would much have preferred that the difficulty and unpleasantness of contesting the point with Councillor Pemberton had devolved on somebody else's shoulders. Councillor Pemberton bad made use of some figures in the most reckless manner possible. lie had asserted that £38,500 worth of work was being</p>
