      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SCHOOLBOYS IN BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLBOYS IN BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>FAILURE OF MODERN METHODS OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Presiding at the meeting of the Lancashire Educe- I tion Committee on Monday, Sir Henry Hibbert said he came into contact with a large number of commercial men, and rarely came across one who said boys were turned out to-day as good as formerly. Boys entering offices were not as good in reading, writing and arithmetic as formerly to the merchant or manufacturer who employed them, and therefore there was a nectssity for inquiry in order to see whether the statements made by the commercial men were accurate or otherwise. A resolution was brought forward frankly acknowledging, in common with teachers and educa- tionists, that the results in the three main essentials of instruction in voluntary schools had materially deteriorated of late years, and believing most strongly that the greatest obstacle to true educational progress in the country was the multiplicity of subjects required to be taught to children under 14 years of age. Alderman Taylor said the old system of education was tested by immediate results, whila the results of the new system were seen rather in the future. He did not desire to blink at the fact that there was deterioration in the immediate results of the new system. Mr Sharpies, of the National Union of Teachers, said the new system was net yet properly introduced in some schools. Teachers were working new methods vrrr well. From actual experience be asserted that a careful use of the modifications of the new system, with a careful adherence to the old, was infinitely better than the old system itself. The subject was referred back for further considera- tion.</p>
        <p>WHITAKER'S PEERAGE (1313)- The distinctive features of " Whitaker's Peerage, Baronetage, Knightage and Companionage " are :—(lj Its price,3s net, (2) Its alphabetical contents. (3) Its circulation. The price makes it the cheapest ; its alphabet—one alphabeticalliat of everyone with a title or decoration—makes it the handiest; and its circula- tion, which exceeds that of all other peerages combined shows that it is the most popular of all such books.</p>
