      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NO COMPETITION,</head>
        <p>NO COMPETITION,</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE AND ARCHITECTS FOR COUNCIL SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>The question of alloting architectural work without competition gave rise to another discussion at the meet- ing of the Warrington Education Committee on Mon- day evening. Councillor Pemberton introduced a motion to rescind a decision previously arrived at by the Education Committee as follows :—"A letter, dated the 7th November, 1912, was read from the architects for the Oekwooi-avenue Council School in answer tothe committee's ingeiresin connection with the prepa-ation by ?ern of the pl ins and qu tutiti es for and the super- vision of the erection of the two one-department schools to be provided in Oliver-stre, t and Parr-street in addition to the scheol in Oak% ood avenue. and it was resolved that the architects for the aikwood- avenue Council &amp;drool b 3 also appointed the architects for the cchools to be erected in Odyer-street and Parr- street upon the terms sat forth in their letter." In view of the fact that the minute had been con- firmed by the Town Councieas the Editcation Authority r fortnight ago, the CHAIRMAN said he was advised that es on if Councillor Pernberton's resolution were accepted it would be inoperative, but he allowed the discussion to proceed, with the result that the com• mittee adhered to its original attitude, by an over- whelming majority. Councillor Henry Roberts (chairman) presided, and there were also present :—The May le Aldermin , Smethurst, Councillors Archer, Birtles, Henshall, Jenkins, Lindley, Dr McKee, Osb elle, Pemberton, and Wilkinson, the Rev G C Briggs, the Rev Father Wilson, Mrs C Jackson Holmes, Messrs C S Burgess, 11 T Fairclough, J Jarman, 11 leingdon, C E ParSer, E Plinston, F 11 Roberts, C II Ueswerte, J Upson and G Walker, Mr J Moore Murray (director), and Mr R B Roberts (assistant director). At the outset the CHAIRMAN informecithe committee that he had received the foil owing letter: —"Warrington, 16th December, 1912 Po the eihairmaa of the Educa- tion or Sites and Buildings Committee, Warrington. Dear Sir,—The report of the discussion at the Town Council meeting of the enct December, on the minutes of the Education Committee will, we believe, have created in the minds of those who re ad the proceedings al impression as regards the attitude of those members of the architectural profession practising in Warrington which is centrary to facts. We are sure that. it is not the intention or wish of the E luevi m Com nittee that any false impression should be spread abroadeied there- fore desire that you should give Neal publ city t ) this letter as to the statements made at the Town Council meeting. The main comment to a hich we, the under- signed, take exception is that as only one firm of Warrington architects suhmitted plane in compete; i met was to be inferred therefrom that the remaining a-chitects did uos desire t comp Oe far the Oakwood-avenus echools. It will ha within the recollection of tl e Education Committee th it at the time the competition for the Evelyn-street Schaols was first mcoted a representation was sent front all the 1 scat architects, reemesting the Education Cornelia( e to make their conditions of cerepetition comp'y wi. b the requirements of the Rossi Institute of Bietish Architects. The Education Committee, for some reason, did not see their way to follow these conditions, and thereupon the Council of the Rosal Ins it,u'e of British Architects issued a notice, stating that unless the conditions were altered, they (the Council) could not allow their members to take part in this competi- tion, and subsequently issued a further similar notice in respect of the Oakwood-avenue Schools. Had it not been for this request from the Architectural Council, it was the intention and wish of the arshitects adising in Warrington to submit designs in c mpetition for these schools; and as a matter of fact, individual members of the profession in Warrington are at present competing for school work, where the 'conditions of competition are as approved by the Royal Institute of British Architects, thus indicating their a illingness to compete where conditions are satisfactory. (Signed) SI P Silcook, F.R.1.8.A., Seger Owen, F,R.1.8.A., Robert ' Garnett, Albert Warburton." The CHAIRMAN raid he prcposed to refer the letter to the Sites and Buildings Committee. Members would notice that the real reason why there was not more competition for the Oakwood-avenue Schools was a reason very different from the one generally alleged. Ceuncillor PEMBERToNdn sut mitting his proposition. said some members of the Education Committee and the Town Council seemed to have the idea that he was a sort of public detective in regard to municipal a ffa*rs and that unless he moved in every matter or initiated the movement be had no right to open his mouth. Ile failed to follow that line of thought, because he was a busy man and be never pretended that he could watch everything. Moreover every member of the committee had an equal right to keep an eye on its doings Recalling the conditions of the pat appoint- ments of school architets, be mentioned that as the architects' fees for one school amounted to over £BOO and act', was a prospect cf the ccmmittee having to build eight or nine schools, be suggested that 3e per cent, commitaion would be adequate payment. That was condemned as knotoeticking. Next there was a pro- posal to appoint their own arohitectealmost unanimously recommended by a sub-committee, who theught it would be advantageous in every way and would effect a saving of £1,500. That propcsal was rejected by the Education Committee and they were placed in the old position of resorting to open competition. Efforts had been made on several occasions to save the ratepayers' money, brit time whole committee had been ngainst those efforts, When the Oakwood-avenu 3 Scheele cime to be bniltthere was no question of pricee he terms advertised being what,for want of a better word,rsiiffit ba described as the trade union rate of wages-5 per cent. Messrs Wright and Hamlyn won the competition, but there weie still certain conditions to be carried out, because the competition dietinctly said the plans must work cut at not more than £lO iris per heed, and until those cm- ditions had teen fulfilled why was there, indecent haste to make them tho architects for two other schools ? It was gaits contrary to the usnal practice to epproach architeee,as the committee bad d sue in the present instsuce. The question of terms bad been thrashed out with much bitterness awl the committee were so determined not to have any knob-sicking that it was thought that point was sett'ed for ever. And yet here they were sanctioning the approach of architects to reduce their terms. Continuing, Councillor Pemberton said Le clnsidered it improper that the terms were not set forth on the agenda poper, as members who gave tepir time in the Public service had a right to full information on any question that arose, That point had been fought out before, but there bad been many lapses from the generally Accepted rule that all the business should be detailed on the agenda paper. The Chairman stated at the Town Council eseetleg that the terms were being withheld out of coneideratiop for the ipterests of the profession, but he (Councillor Pemberbee) teoeght the ' other architects would be only too pleased to know the terms on which the work was being done. In his opinisn, it was knobsticking in the worst sense to approach the firm who had designed one sshool and say: "If you are given two other john, without tiny corn- paw. se how much will you take off ? " He wanted in the first instance tO maJe the commission ' three end a half per cent. and to give all lopsleeohitects the epportunity of competing, but the committee approached one favoured firm for a reduction of terms. That firm was being commissioned to carry out £38,1100 worth of work, and only £14,000 worth had been 1 wen in open competition. The firm were finishing the Belton School extension, worth £6,000. There was no competition for that. The appointed architects were Messrs Wright, Garnett and Wright, but 1 since they won in open competition the partnertheo of the firm was altered, end it came abcut that Messrs Wright and Hamlyn gat the work. They were appointed in open competition as arohitects of the Oakwood- avenue School, which would poet phout £14,000, and now they were to be given the work of (varying out single block schools at Oliver-street and Pare-street at cost of about £lO,OOO. In due course other blocks.' would have to be added to those schools and the position wouiel arise—" Well, you see, Messrs Wright and Hamlyn have does the infant school ; yen =cot very well ask anybody else tp come on.' That would mean an additional £8,510. Teens was 41,500 worth of work,in connection with which all the other architects, who had so many friends a rhea time ago,had no chance 'whatever. Was there any sense of fair play in that ? He was not making charges against anybody.tie was not alleging for a moment that the committee bad deliber- ately, and with premeditation, done anything wrong. He did not allege so, because many people made mistakes—he had made mistakes, hut had always been ready to acknowledge them—and it might have heen that they had not seen that point of view and thought that they could effect some saving, lo9t in public life it was necessary to look at what was fair and right to the whole of the profession and the ratepayers. In the first instance 110 architects from all over the country competed and 73 had their fees returned because the society to which they belonged insisted that one of the conditions should he the appointment of nn assessor. His experience in those matters new showed him that it was necessary to have an assessor and he would use every endeavour to bave one appointed on the next occasion If the Board of Education came to the conclusion that Messrs Wright and Hamlyn's plans were the finest ever created, and that in future they must be the standard for all schools, he would admit that be had been wrong in his attitude. But if the Board did not take that view, why had not the other architects been given a chance? It was worthy of note that every competition arranged by the committee had been won by a different firm. Mr UPSON painted out that the minute bad been approved by the Town Council, which was their</p>
      </div>
