        <head>WHEN BUYING FURNITURE</head>
        <p>WHEN BUYING FURNITURE</p>
        <p>Or ANYTHING FOR THE HOUSE have a look round MARKET STREET, WARRINGTON; the Shop lately occupied by HUDSON &amp; DUTTON, Ironmongers, is now SUTTON'S FURNITURE SALEROOMS, With a Complete Stock of BEDROOM, DINING-ROOM &amp; KITCHEN FURNITURE, BEDSTEADS &amp; BEDDING, CURBS &amp; FENDERS, &amp;c. Give us a call, inspect our Reliable Goods, note the prices, and the order is ours SUTTON'S, Opposite FRUIT MARKET WARRINGTON, DELIVERIES FREE.</p>
        <p>given out without competition, and made up that sum by quoting £6,000 for the Bolton School extension. That extension ass given to the architects who were carrying out the original work at a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee, at which Councillor Pemberton was present, and %las confirmed by the Education Committee, and afterwards by the Town Council at a meeting at which Coun- cillor Pem ertoa was r lso present. For all practical purposes he was entitled to rule out absolutely the question of the &amp;Icon School extension from the discussion, lie went on to say thtt £24,000 worth of work was given without competition. That again was not true. competition was invited end was secured for the Oakwood-avenue schools, which would cost asout £14,000 or £15,000. Twenty achitects competed. It was most unfair to quote figures in a connection that was absolutely false to the true facts. The plans selected were chosen on their merits, and the terms of Commission were 5 per cent for agricultural work and 21 per cent. for quantities. What on earth was the use of asking twenty architects, nineteen of whom bad their plans rejected on merits, to compete for the other two schools? The reason why the Warrington members of the R.1.8.A. did not compete was seen in their letter. They declined not because the committee did not offer proper commission,but because the committee would net consent to have en assessor to adjudicate on the plans. The responsibility for their absolute exclusion rested prim rrily, and almost entirely, on the shoulders of Councillor Pemberton who protested against the appointment of an assessor. Councillor PaMBERTON said he always thought that the majority ruled, but the Chairman' last observation was very flattering to him. The CHAIRMAN said that the history of Councillor Pemberton's association with the question of the appointment of architects did not entitle him to very much gratitude from the members of the R.1.8.A. in Warrington. Having justified th . non disclosure of terms by pointing out that the Corporation adopted a similar course in the case of oertain large contracts, the Chairman made a pretest against the " shameful waste,of public time" occasioned by the reintroduction of the'question and hoped the committee would support him'in an endeavour to carry on the proceedings of the , committee with something like business acumen and due consideration. Councillor Pemberton's attitude was pursued at the loss of the time, patience and temper of other members. Three voted for the motion, Councillors Pemberton, Archer and Dr McKee, and nineteen voted against. The Mayor, Councillor Osborne, Mr F R Roberts and Mr Unsworth did not vote,</p>
        <p>BAZAAR SURPRISE. GAMING AND LOTTERY PROSECUTION.</p>
        <p>_ A cave of considerable interest was decided at Dudley on Monday, when the Vicar of Dudley and the church- wardens and secretaries connected with a bazaar, held for the renovation of the church, were charged with using gaming tedes and having lotteries at the bazaar. The gaming was alleged to have taken place at spinning jenny tables where books, a leg of mutton, and a pork pie were amongst the prizes. The alleged lotteries took the form of raffling the various articles. The bench found that the place was unlawfully need under the Gaming Act, but as it ccas not proved that four of the defendants had been connected with the management the cases against them were dismissed. The Vicar bud one of the secretaries, however, were ordered to pay costs. In the charges under the Lottery Act a similar decision was arrived at, the Vicar and one of the secretaries being ordered to pay the costs. The bench warned the public that other cases would not be so leniently dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>MINERS AND DRINK.</head>
        <p>MINERS AND DRINK.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur MArkham, M.P., speaking at Cardiff on Saturday night, said that from a number of inquiries which he had made in connection with eighteen collieries with which he was associated, he found that 50 per cent, of men the drank more than they ought to do, 25 per cept, regularly drank 0 excess, and only, , about 15 per cent, saved up for a rainy day. In the 1 South Wales 'returns the maximum of those who saved for further contingencies was 20 or 25 per cent. There was less drinking in the Sirhowy Valley, Monmouth- shire, than in the Midlands and the North of England.</p>
