      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>•1</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Christmas at the Antipodes.—Christmas in Australia is very different in climatic conditions from what it is here, but it has the genuine holiday spirit and is honoured by the observance of many old customs as in the Mother Country. Ninety-five people out of every hundred are British born or of British descent, and it is therefore no wonder that they cling to old traditions, and John Bull's way of doing things. Although no Yule log burns on the hearth, beef, turkey, plum-pudding and mince pies, are invariably present at the Australian Christmas dinner' which is usually a mid day meal. Servants are hard to come by in Australia and harder to keep, so that even people of means have a good deal to do in the house and late dinners are avoided. Holly and mistletoe do not flourish in Australia, but churches shops, and dwellings, are lavishly decorated with a pro- fusion of summer vegetation and there are tiae visits of S inta Clause to the children. The shade temperature may be anything in the nineties, or possibly over a I oundred, suggesting surf-bathing at the seaside, rather than winter -sports. All sorts of tummer out-door recreations are indulged in, and although it is hot, the Amosphere is dry, and there is everywhere an energy, dertness, and j oyousness, begotten of bright sunshine and cloudless skies.</p>
        <p>Revived Peerage.—Women may bask in the reflected ;tory of their husbands' honours, but those who win listinction for themselves in any public field are not given ornamental handles to their names, and it would oe something of an innovation for a woman's name to appear in the New Years honours list. It was in 1871 that, for the first time in cur history, a peerage was conferred on a woman—Miss Ar gels Burdett-Coutts— hut the " new departure " which this bestowal seemed to promise never got beyond the start. It now appears chat Miss Burdett Coutts was all the time co-heiress to the ancitnt barony of Latymer, which, after being in abeyance for over Oleo centuries, has been revived in the person of Mr Francis Coutte, whose mother was the elder sister of the late Baroness Burdett Coutts. The fourth Lord Latyroer, and the last to bear the title, died in 1577 and Mr Coutts successfully claimed his descent from one of his four daughters. The Baroness Burdett-Coutts, who was the greatest woman philanthropist of Queen Victoria's reign died in 1906. Sweated Female Labour.—That the National Anti- Sweating League has still a great work before it, is shown by the Home Office inquiry into the conditions of employment in the making-up trades. Since Thomas Hood wrote "The song of the shirt "-seventy years ago, organisation and collective bargaining have vastly improved the condition of the working classes as a whole, but exceptions are to be found in many of the industries which employ cheap female labour. There are still tens of thousands of women and girls, who work long hours for the barest pittance. A skilled worker making chemises at 9d per dozen, earns less than one penny an hour, whilst few people who earn good money can realise what it means to link together 765 hooks and eyes, stitch them on a card, and do all this w 0.711 for a penny. Yet such instances as these, and many others, can be found quoted from official documents,and among them are the making of blouses at lid. each, button- holes at 6d. a gross, artificial flowers at one penny per dozen sprays, Bible and Prayer Book folding at lid. per thousand sheets, in which employments the skilled band working 12 to 14 hours daily, may earn 55. to 61, per week. The Trade Boards Act was mainly designed to put down sweating in the making up trades, but so far it seems tohave done little to help this struggling mass of toilers, composed very largely of women and girls, usually classed as_outworkers. _ . _</p>
        <p>Contrasts in Dress.—ln the world of fashion, as elsewhere, striking contrasts are frequently seen. For instance, in the matter of reception and evening dresses, skirts may be short or long; materials may be so heavy as to suggest coverings for furniture,—to which purpose the designs are well suited—or the most flimsy and fragile imaginable. Some colours are most garish, others are soft and restful, and delicate in tint. Veiling is still a feature of evening dress, and is employed even in the case of embroideries and lace. Slashings are much used, especially on dresses of the freakish type. A Riot of Colour.—Evening dresses that have been, and are being prepared for Christmas festivities, may be described as a riot of the most daring and original yet lovely colours. Curiously enough, the most brilliant when well chosen, and arranged judiciously, 80 as not to approach the face too nearly, are eminently becoming at night, under the glow of artificial light, to both blondes and brunettes. A brocade, literally ablaze with colour that evens year ago would have been pronounced impossible, is this season a great success. But very little of the brocade is used on the corsage, which may consist chiefly of gold or white lace veiled—as alto the brocade—with palest pink chiffon, flesh coloured tulle, or some other tint suited to the wearer's colouring and complexion. Flesh pink is very largely employed this winter as veiling,and is extremely becoming. With black aril white, still so modish, only the latter can however be used. The New Woollen Coats.—Every season woollen coats improve, and there is every probability that like the blouse, they will he always with us. In self-colours they are most desirable gamenta; and nothing could be more charming than thuse mixed shades, which give the effect of being shot. In mercerised cotton— unproporly designated mercerised silk—these coats are not quite so satisfactory, for they are heavy, and do not retain their colour as well as in wool, nor are they so warm. In silk they are very handsome and wear moderately well. Some colouts are combined very effectively with black, others with white. Very pretty caps, quaint modifications of the Neapolitan cap, are to be had to match the coats in plain colours or shot effects. For morning wear these caps are very useful, moreover as they are worn well down on ,the head, no hat pins are required to fasten them. Beware of the Snetztr.—Yawning, we are told, is healthy, and like laughter it is contagious. Why, we hardly know, but it is easy to understand that the extra effort in emptying and filling the lungs with fresh air helps to purify the blood. Nor is it harmful to other people—like sneezing—which has recently been the subject of a special investigation by the bacteriological exports of the New York Health Department. They fled that not only common colds are distributed by expiratory explosions, but that measles, scarlet fever, mumpv, whooping cough, and otuer diseases are spread by sneezing.A pers:nsuffering from a cold will discharge the active germs to a distance of five or six feet by the mere act of talking, and sneezing once or twice in a tramcar or railway carriage may convey a cold to the rest of the passengers. Ventilation is the only practical safeguard and that is often most difficult to secure when it is most needed. The person with a violent mold is the one who wants the windows closed, and nothing short of going about in a smoke helmet would seem to offer any real security against the risk of infec- tion at this time of year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>PORCO TIN MINES.</head>
        <p>PORCO TIN MINES.</p>
        <p>Within only two months of issuing the prospectus/ a full confirmation of its statements have been cabled home by Mr Malcolm Roberts of the Aramayo Franck() Mines—the superintending engineer. Shipment of plant is taking place on his advice, the mines have been taken over for the Company, and the staff is on the property. There are 22 mines of combined area 900 acres, with seven tin lodes developed, showing 45,000 tons of ore, sample assayed to contain 2,000 tons of extractable tin oxide. The prospectus estimat(s were based upon the price of tin at £l5O per ton, the price to-day is about .C 226 per ton, and Mr Roberts says the plant on its way will treat 20 tons a day more than was estimated. Share certificates are ready and a settlement is being applied for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>unty,</head>
        <p>unty,</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, Decembe r 21, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>"An invaluable woman that," said my friend, Colonel Chudleigh, as his house- keeper, Mrs. Ransom, bade us good-night after receiving the Colonel's instructions with regard to certain arrangements for the following day. "I don't know what I should have done without her."</p>
        <p>_ Mrs. Ransom was, certainly an ideal housekeeper, a gentle, grey-haired woman who said she was fifty. One would imagine that without the blue glasses which she always wore she would look a good deal younger. Her figure was slim, her move- ments- were graceful, and her step alert. The scar of a burn extending from the corner of her mouth up a portion of the right cheek made it difficult to judge what she might have looked like before an acci- dent disfigured her. The Colonel's housekeeper had come to him at a time when his two little girls were deprived of a mother's care, and when the shadow of a terrible tragedy rested over Dower House, Colonel Chudleigh's romantio old-world home situated in a pine wood about three miles' -distance from Sevenoaks.</p>
        <p>It was in the library of the Dower House that we were sitting late on the night of the 24th of December when the Colonel referred so gratefully to Mrs. Ransom's excellent qualities. I had arrived that evening on a visit to my friend and client. He had invited me to spend Christmas with him, and, being a bachelor with no ties of my own, I had gladly accepted the invitation. My father had been (ho Chudleighs' family solicitor, and when he retired and I took his place the management of their legal affairs passed into my hands. The Colonel was fifty-two, and I was twenty years his junior when our profes- sional acquaintance ripened into a personal friendship. It was the tragedy that brought us more closely together. When Colonel Chudleigh, at the age of forty-four, came home from India to take possession of his late father's estates, lie came to Dower House a childless widower..</p>
        <p>A handsome, well-preserved man of charming manners and considerable wealth, he had no sooner taken up his residence in his Kentish home than his neighbours de- cided that it would not be long before he gave the Dower House a mistress. Their conjecture was realised, but not quite in the way they had anticipated. The Colonel, after a stay in town during the London season, fell in love with Miss Phyllis Frayne, a charming young actress who had made a great success in a much- talked-of comedy at a West-End theatre. The Colonel, before the marriage, had taken me into his confidence. I was present i at the ceremony, which took place n Lon- don, and I saw the happy pair off when they left for the honeymoon in Switzerland. Phyllis Frayno was twenty-five and tho Colonel was forty-seven when they became man and wife, and I had every reason to believe that the union would be a happy one.</p>
        <p>The young actress had told her ardent wooer all alxiut herself. Her father, a cap- tain in the Merchant Service, had died, leaving her mother in straitened circum- stances. It was necessary for her and for her brother, who was a year her junior, to earn their living. She thought she had dramatic talent, and. had obtained an en- gagement to play small parts with a travel- ling company. Her mother had died soon afterwards, and when she came to London and made her first real success she had no one to think about but herself. Her brother was a clerk in a provincial bank in the north of England, and so far as she knew he was her only relative. The Colonel brought his bride back to the Dower House, and for five years he was supremely happy. Mrs. Chudleigh was de- voted to her husband and to her children, two little girls, and no one could have fore- seen the sudden disaster which was destined to darken this happy English home. The Colonel while hunting caught a severe chill. He took to his bed, and the doctor was summoned.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Chudleigh at once installed herself her husband's nurse. She prepared every- thing he was ordered with her own hands. She spent nearly the whole day by his bed- side, and at night she had a bed made up in a room which opened into the Colonel's.</p>
        <p>But the Colonel, in spite of the devoted attention of his wife, grew worse instead of better. The local doctor was a little mysti- fied, and decided if there was no improve- ment to summon a London physician. It. was on the sixth day after the Colonel had taken to his bed that I was summoned by telegram to visit the doctor at his pri- vate residence.</p>
        <p>Wondering what-it might mean, and not a little alarmed, I went down at once.</p>
        <p>The doctor told me au extraordinary story. Early that morning he had paid a visit to his patient, and was alone with the sick man.</p>
        <p>Standing on a small table by the bedside was a glass of barley water. Something about its appearance caused the doctor to pick it up, take it to the window, and look at it.</p>
        <p>From what he saw a terrible suspicion flashed across his mind. He poured it into an empty medicine bottle which was on the mantel shelf, corked it, put it in his pocket, and brought it away. • "The ordinary rough test to which I have been able to subject that barley water," said the doctor to me, "has confirmed my Nuspicions. Someone has put a small quan- tity of arsenic into it."</p>
        <p>'Good heavens!" I gasped. "It is im- possible—who would do such a thing?" "Listen. Mrs. Chudleigh prepared that barley water herself. I ascertained that she took it to her husband's room the last thing at night. Then, as is her custom, she bolted the door of his bedroom. This door is loose, it seems, and on windy nights it rattles unless bolted.</p>
        <p>"Half that barley water was given to the Colonel during the night. She must be the poisoner." "I am certain," I exclaimed, "that what- ever has happened, Mrs. Chudleigh is inno- cent. There can be no possible motive." The doctor shook his head. "Do you know what the people about here would say if they knew what I know?" "They would agree with me !" "No; there has been scandalous gossip for the last fortnight. I have heard it from two of my patients whose servants have talked. On three occasions lately Mrs. Chudleigh has been seen in the evening in earnest conversation with a well-dressed, good-looking young fellow on the outskirts of the pine wood by the path that leads to the Dower House. That is the motive that gossips about here would supply." "I refuse to believe it—l cannot believe it?" I said. "In any case I ask you to remain in the town until after the specialist has been. Will you come here this evening at six?" At six o'clock that evening I returned. Evidence of poison had been found not only in the barley water, but the glass in which medicine had been administered. That night I accompanied the doctor to the Dower House. He told Mrs. Chudleigh she must not enter her husband's room again. A trained nurse was in charge. The distressed wife protested, and then the doctor blurted out the truth. "Madam, your husband is being poisoned, and no one but yourself has had access to his medicine or the drink in which the poison hae been</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>VllNOMllllMBsissiatosimsts</head>
        <p>VllNOMllllMBsissiatosimsts</p>
        <p>administered. To-morrow the police will vestigate the matter." Then he left us together. "I believe you are innocent," I said, "bat to-morrow you may be arrested." "Arrested—l--but why—why should poison the man I lova—the father of my children?" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I must tell you the truth," I said, "for you will have to face it. Who is the young man you have been seen to meet at night by the pine woods?" With a cry of dismay Phyllis Chudleigh buried her face in her hands.</p>
        <p>Presently she looked up. "I am inno- cent,' she said, "but I trust not be taken. The shame of that would kill my husband. Leave me. I will find a way out and save him and save myself from the sorrow and shame that this charge made publicly against me would mean." I tried to argue with her, but she would not listen. "Go," she said; "I will do the only thing that is possible." I left the Dower House and went to an hotel in Sevenoaks for the night. The next morning I went to see the doctor. The Colonel was already better, but Mrs. Chudleigh had disappeared. She had left the house at night. She had taken a late train to London—that had been ascertained —but beyond that nothing had been heard of her. Then I understood her last words to use. I told the doctor that the guilty woman, terrified at what lay before her, had gone away with the intention of committing sui- cide.</p>
        <p>No charge was made. The doctor, after Mrs. Chudleigh's disappearance, felt it his duty to see the Colonel and tell him every- thing and let him decide. The blow was terrible. But gradually the Colonel recovered something of his old strength, and he had his children to live for. Six months after Mrs. Chndleigh's dis- appearance he advertised for a housekeeper. lie wanted to go abroad and leave his little girls in charge of someone who could be trusted. Lady --, a friend of his in Lon- don, the wife of a brother officer, wrote to him strongly recommending a widow whom she had known for many years, and Mrs. Ransom came. The children took to her at once, and then the Colonel went abroad, satisfied that his little ones would be cared for and tended in his absence. _ _ _</p>
        <p>That was the tragedy I recalled as I sat that night in the library of the Dower House in which I was a Christmas guest.</p>
        <p>The tragedy was even more deeply im- pressed upon my mind when, at midnight, I found myself alone in the guest chamber. The room set apart for guests was the one in which the Colonel had lain and been brought almost to death's door by the hand of his wife. _ .</p>
        <p>„. Mrs. Ransom had made the room as bright and cheerful for me as she could. A log fire was burning on the hearth, and on the table by the bedside there was a spirit stand and glasses and hot water, and all the ingredients for a seasonable nightcap. 1 mixed myself a glass of hot spirits, sipped the toddy, put it on the little table by the bedside, undressed and got between the sheete. In the dim firelight the room looked a little weird and ghostly. It was a room panelled in dark oak—one of the oldest rooms in the Dower House. I had unpacked any clothes and hung some of them on the hangers in a cupboard which fitted into the wall. _ _</p>
        <p>Gradually I fell asleep. How long I had been asleep I do not know, when a strange sound in the room awoke me. The fire was low, but as I nervously turned my eyes in the direction of the sound I distinctly saw a woman's figure. Too terrified to move, I held my breath and lay still. I saw the figure advance. It was that of a woman in a long white gown. It came nearer and nearer to my bed. By the side of my bed it paused for a moment. Then it extended its hand towards the little. table. _</p>
        <p>The figure took a glass and with the other hand appeared to be putting some- thing into it. Then the figure put the glass gently down, and turned its back to me. Terrified as I was, I raised my head to watch its retreat- ing movements. I saw it open the door of the cupboard in which I had hung my clothes, enter it, pull the door to gently after it. Instantly my terror left me. I sprang out of bed and threw the cupboard door open just in time to see the back on which my clothes were hanging gently sliding to. I thrust my arm through and forced it back again. A white figure was in front of me. I seized it, and the woman started and shrieked. Then I understood she had been walking in her sleep. My grasp had rou.9ed her to consciousness. Before me, trembling -with terror, stood a woman of about thirty in her nightdress. I was in a bedroom. A lamp with a shade on it was burning on the chest of drawers. I seized the lamp and held it up to see the woman's features. She flung herself upon me and dashed the lamp from my hand. It fell to the ground, the blazing oil ran across the floor, and in a moment it had caught the curtains and they began to blaze. The flames ran up and spread. In a moment the Dower House with its old :wooden beams would be on fire. I loosed my grip of the woman, dashed into my own room and unlocked the door, and rushed out into the passage crying "Fire!" Fire!" _ _</p>
        <p>_ Ins moment I heard the sound of move- ment. The Colonel came rushing out of his room. "The room inside is on fire," I cried. "It May spread." The Colonel shouted for the servants, and, half-dressed, they hurried to the landing. "Quick," said the Colonel. "Fill the buckets and turn on the hose. The fire is in the guest chamber." "No," I shouted, "it is in the room beyond. The one the cupboard in the guest chamber opens into." "The cupboard," cried the Colonel. "I don't understand!" I grasped his arm and dragged him with me; the servants followed. I pointed to the cupboard door. I thrust the panel back as far as it would go. "I never knew of this place," cried the Colonel. "What does it meant" The eight of the blazing room beyond made everyone forget the sudden discovery of the secret panel. The servants went in with water and did their best to eatinguiah the flames. "It may sprea," said the Colonel. "Wo must rouse every Dody. I will go to the chil- dren. Where is Mrs. Ransom? She is not here." _ _</p>
        <p>"I will wake her," I said, "if one of the maids will go with me and show me her room. You must stay and direct your people." The maid took me to the floor above and pointed to a room. I turned the handle of the door. It was locked. I knocked loudly. "The house is on fire," I cried. "Come out at once. The whole place may be ablaze directly." I put ray shoulder to the door and burst it open. Mrs. Ransom, half dressed, was standing in the room. When I burst in she uttered a cry of ' terror. I started back in astonishment. A grey wig was lying on the, dressing-table by a pair of blue glasses.</p>
        <p>..._</p>
        <p>The woman who stood trembling before me was a fair-haired woman of thirty. She had no burn upon her beautiful face. It was Phyllis Chudleigh. In an instant she saw that I had recog- nised her. .</p>
        <p>"Aty husband—my children !" she cried. "Are they safe—are they safer</p>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>The Mystery of Dower House was a mys- tery no longer. The woman who was car- ried out of the burning rooru in a dead faint was one of the upper housemaids, Mary Quayle. In her terror at the discovery she confessed the truth. Unknown to any- one she had had a lover in the village, a young man employed' on the estate. He had been detected in stealing valuable pro- perty. The Colonel had prosecuted him, and he had been sent to prison for two years. The girl threatened revenge upon the Colonel. She had quite accidentally discovered that a cupboard in her. bedroom opened int 6 the Colonel's room by a sliding panel. It had been put there perhaps two hundred years previously for some purpose, and in course of years forgotten. When the Colonel was lying ill Mary Quayle conceived the idea of poisoning him. She managed to get some arsenic from a farm, and at night, when the Colonel was asleep and his wife was lying down in the adjoin- ing room, she had crept through and put arsenic into the drink by the bedside. On the night that I slept in the guest chamber it is probable that the room, being occupied again, brought the tragedy to her i mind, and n her sleep, dreaming of it, she rose and acted her crime over again. But her midnight revelation was almost forgotten in the joy with which the Colonel discovered that the wife he had mourned as dead was alive and beneath his roof, and had watched over him and her, children all the time.</p>
        <p>had always believed in, her innocence. When Phyllis Chudleigh wrote to her and implored her to help her to go to the Dower House disguised and fill the situ- ation that had been advertised, Lady had consented, and the reference was a pious fraud. To her husband and myself Phyllis Chud- leigh revealed the circumstances that had induced her to disappear rather than to face arrest and seek to prove her innocence. The young man she had been meeting clandestinely was her brother. He had got into bad company, and his accounts were wrong at the bank. He was on his holiday leave, and was meeting his sister in this way instead of coming to the Dower House, be- cause he was afraid that his defalcation might at any moment be discovered and a warrant issued for his arrest.</p>
        <p>For Mrs. Chudleigh to have stated in a public court that it was her brother she was meeting secretly must have meant that the avowal would have brought his delinquen- cies to light. He would have been arrested. Her husband would have been shamed and humiliated by the publication of the fact that his wife was the sister of a, felon. After her flight her brother had succeeded in obtaining enough money from a friend to replace the sum he had appropriated, but her position remained the same. She believed that her flight had convinced everyone who knew of the poisoning that she was a guilty woman. If she attempted to clear her name, inquiries would be made, with the result that her brother might be ruined, and even then the suspicion of having attempted her husband's death might still rest upon her. And so she held her peace, and when the opportunity came she exercised the art of the theatre which was hers and returned, disguised, to her home and her dear ones. That Christmas Day at the Dower House was indeed a festival of family reunion. In the joy of a iVife restored to her husband's love, of a mother restored to her children, all else was forgotten. The only thought that troubled the happy party that Christmas Day was how the sud- den return of' the mistress of the Dower Rouse was to be explained to curious friend and neighbours. "If you will be advised by me, as the family solicitor," I said, when the Colonel put the question as we sat round the Yule log on Christmas night, "you won't explain at all. If necessity arises, refer your neigh- bours to me, and I will tell them the story of the Guest Chamber, and what happened in it on Christmas Eve."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>CHRISTMAS AT THE POST OFFICE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS AT THE POST OFFICE.</p>
        <p>LOCAL ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>On the 23rd and 24th December the final clearance for the night mail at the head office will be 9.30 p.m. On the 23rd and 24th December the town letter boxes will be cleared finally at 630 p.m. for the night mail, and for local delivery at 9.30 p.m. on the 23rd and 8 p.m. on the 24th. These hours also apply to all town offices except that local parcels will be received up to 8 p.m. It should be borne in mind, therefore, to avoid dis- appointment, that the head office in Springfield-street is the only place where letters may be posted after 6.30 p.m. for the night mails of the 23rd and 24th, and that the letter box at the head office will close at 9.30 p.m. on both dates. FRODSIIAM, HELSBY AND LYMM.</p>
        <p>On the 24th December the last collection for the night mails will be made at 7 p.m. from the sub-office, after which time no letters or parcels except those for local delivery will be dealt with on that date. LY The usual week-day supplementary clearance from the sub-office letter box and the railway station wall box at 9 p.m. will not be made on December 24th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WHAT WOMEN OUGHT TO</head>
        <p>WHAT WOMEN OUGHT TO</p>
        <p>KNOW.</p>
        <p>Every woman ought to know how to overcome those ailments which come to her between girlhood and middle age. Because she is a woman she is likely to know the frightful pains in the back and sides, the throbbing headache and a sense of lost power in the limbs. These symptoms should not be disregarded. The pains, if neglected, will play havoc with the strongest constitution, and rob the sweetest face of beauty. All women should know that Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound is a harmless herbal remedy. Taken according to directions, it affords speedy relief It is a woman's medicine for women's ailments, and acts in harmony with Nature, restoring and preserving health. A Bradford lady writes:—" I am taking the second bottle of Lydia E. Pinkbam's Vegetable Compound. It has done me a great deal of good. I am sorry I did not know of the medicine before." The Laay Manager, The Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead-road, London, NW., will send quite free a private book to women only, and she will supply the medicine direct at 2s. 9d. a bottle, or 48. W. for a very large bottle. Any chemist sells it at these prices. - _</p>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY AND INDUSTRIALISM. Mr. B. S. Towaroe, of Warrington, prospective Unionist candidate for the Middleton Division, in opening a bazaar in the Middleton Parish Church Schools on Saturday afternoon, drew a contrast between • two creeds held widely to-day—the one Christian, tbe other frankly and aggressively materialistic. No Christian, he said, could /Andy the vital social problems of the day and not feel a desire to improve the indnstrial conditions of his fellow-countrymen. There- fore, Christians roust be in touch with life, and learn to think more of hours of labour, rates of wages. house room, and other questions, but they must also look beyond these things and see that, in Bishop Westcott's words," the remedy of the industrial situation must be spiritual."</p>
        <p>.-..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>£12,000 BUILDING SCHEME.</head>
        <p>£12,000 BUILDING SCHEME.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS IMPORTANT PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>'• A DISGRACE TO LANCASHIRE.'</p>
        <p>A acheme for the erection of a Wolk of buildings for the accommodation of imbecile and epileptic patients, at a cost of nearly £12,000 was before the Board of Guardians at its meeting on Wednesday. Mr E L Bent presided and there were also present :—Mrs Simpson, Mrs Collingwood, Kers T It Matthews and A M Mitchell, Messrs A .1 Jolley (deputy-chairman), J Billinge, W Gill, G Mather, A Brooks, W C Brooks, W Furness, P. Lee,J A Rushworth, J A Hughes, G A Allen, W Flanagan, T Ritchie, J W Knowles, A Bottomley (clerk), It 1 Partridge (assistant clerk), W J Brown (master), C B Lawrence (superintendent of Cottage Homes.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>OLD OFFICIAL'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>OLD OFFICIAL'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN informed the Board of the death of a very old official who recently retired on superainus. tion—Mr Fairhurst, late porter at the Cottage Homes. He said Mr Fairhurst always excelled in his work and was mast courteous and kind.</p>
        <p>A vote et sympathy with Mrs Fairhurst was passed, the members stard,ng. ENTERTAINING VISITORS. At a meeting of the House Committee the Master reported with reference to the return of relatives and friends who had stayed over-night on the premises since he took up duty, and which was presented ant read at a meeting of the committee on the 29th ult. He stated that he had again searched the porter's books and found that two or three entries had not been taken into account, but in two instances this had been occasioned by omissions in the porter's book. He desired to inform the committee that it was no easy task to pre- pare the return and a very easy thing for him to miss a name, no matter how careful he might be, as he had to wade through over 6,000 entries to get the information required.</p>
        <p>urther details of the visits were submitted, but no comment was made upon them at the Board meeting.</p>
        <p>TRADE BOOM AND MORE RELIEF.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee had passed a-Te;olution calling the attention of the Newton Relief Committee to tbe increase in the out-relief granted in that district. Mr Jolley mentioned that the increase was about 25 per cent. in a comparatively short period, which was rather startling. Mr MATHER : With trade looming as it is, what can be the cause of this epidemic of poveity ? Mr KNOWLES said there was employment for every- body in the Newton District—in fact they could not get sufficient men to do the work. BIG BUILDING SCHEME. The Duildiog Committee had before them plans sub- mitted by Messrs W and S Owen, for the proposed building for the accommodation of male and lt male imbecile and epileptic patieats, as approved by the Board in July, together with alternative plans pie Fared in accordance with the suggeations of officers of the Local Government Board. The committee considered that although the new scheme provided accommodation for 170 patients, as against 160, and was to a certain extent better adapted than the original scheme, for the accommodation of other classes of patients (in the event of the building ultimately not being required for imbecile purposes), it would ho more economical, both from a financial and administrative point of view, to erect a building in accordance with the original plena. Accommodation for imbecile and epileptic oases was bound to be provided in Workhouses and would be required for mtny years. If the time did arrive when those cises had to be transferred to the care of another authority, the committee were satiEfled that, at a corn• paritively small cost,the buildings could be adapted for the accommodation of other classes. As stated by the architect,the new scheme would involve an expenditure of £11,662, as against £l2,666—the amount required if a building were erected in accordance with the original plans. In addition to that sum must be adied as amount which would be required for the erection of new receiving wards, also a sum of at least .£5,600 for the erection of a new board room, offices, Zko. In the o riginal scheme accommodation was provided for all classes of imbecile and epileptic patients in one build- ing,and the committee felt assured that better adminis- tration would be obtained in that way than by having them housed in separate buildings. They therefore recommeeded the Guardians to again submit the original plans for the approval of the Local Govern- ment Board.</p>
        <p>_ Mr MATHER moved and Mr W C Brooks seconded the approval of the riisutes. In reply to the Rev A M Mitchell, it was stated that the new buildings were to be erected on the plot of land facing the board room at the Workhouse, and aijoining the railway. The Rev A M MITCHELL thought the suggestion of the Local Government Board, whereby the Board room and adjacent buildings would be utilized, was a very good one. It was, however, ultra uires to erect any rew buildings for that purpose at the present time. They ought to await developments. A lot of money was going to be spent on sanatoria which would presently be practically</p>
        <p>_ THROWN ON TO THE SCRAP REAP, so that there would be plenty of accommodation avail- able. It would be a grievous mistake to spend so much money when they did not quite know where they were. It, should also be borne in mind that the present Board would shortly go out of office. The CHAIRMAN said he had no particular objection to buildings of that kind being ereoted, but be con- sidered the site was unsuitable. lie suggested that the question should be referred back for further con- sideration.</p>
        <p>Mrs COLLING WOOD endorsed the Chairman's view al to the unsuitability of the site. Buildings erected in that position would,in the event of their being discarded for their original purpose, be useless for the treat- ment of phthisical cases. Mr MATHER : We shall not need any after January. Mrs COLLINGWOOD : It is about the worse site in the whole place for the erection of such a building. Ihe Rev A M MITCHELL said the Board would have to provide offices in the town sooner or later. If they were really mad enough to go in for either of the schemes at present, he thought the larger one merited more favour than the other. If they had been on the look-out for the worst site they could provide,the coinmittee could not have made a more appropriate choice. Mr A BROOKS differed from the decision of the con" mittee, and expressed the opinicn that the plans had hardly received that careful consideration that WAS necessary before deciding upon the expenditure of such a large snm. He regarded the larger scheme as by far the more preferable. For the extra £6,000 they would bare that much better accommodation for ciao. pur- poses. Were they going to allow the old offices to suffice for all time ? That aspect of the question impressed him as being very urgent. He moved that toe scheme be reconsidered at a special meeting of the Building Committee. Mr FLANAGAN seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr FURNESS suggested that the Building Committee should introduce a modified scheme for the care of Imbeciles and epileptics, but at the same time make provision for adequate office and Board Room accommodation. The enormous expenditure at present proposed for the one building was altogether in excess of what was absolutely required. A strong argument against it was that in the opinion of the Local Cioxern- meat Board, it was not the better arrangement. Mr JOLLEY expressed the hope that members would not shirk their duties and responsibilities simplYl because an election was coming on in a few months-- The Board had definitely pledged itself to a scheme for the proper treatment of the lunatics in that institution and had appointed a committee to give effect to its; wishes. The question therefore narrowed itself down. tp the issue: Which scheme would they select? WU idle to suggest that they had not the support of the Local Government Board in trying to improve the. • • ow]dawns. The Local Government Board had beep pressing them for years to do something. The propor- tion of insane persons to he dealt with in Workhouses was higher in Lancashire than in other Fart of the country. It was ONE OF THE DISGRACEFUL FEATURES OF THEIR. WORK IN LANCASHIRE that there was not sufficient accommodation in the asylums for the insane, and consequently large numbers of people who ought to be in asylums were in Work- houses. The accommodation in the Warrington Workhouse for those classes of patients was the saw° now as it was ten years ago, while the numbers .or cases had nearly trebled. It was felt that the question of new offices might be allowed to Btand over for three or four years so as not to increase too heavily the Union's indebtedness. Interruptions by the Rev A M Mitchell led to Mr JOLLEY retorting warmly: I think Mr Mitchell has disgraced himself this morning and on more than one former occasion. The Rev A M MITCILItLL : What is the matter now 2 What</p>
        <p>ave _ Mr JOLLEY : If you won't interrupt I will finish my' 7 emarks, but if you persist in interrupting I will deal with your interruptions. I shall insist on my rights as a representative of the ratepayers. The Rev A M : You insist on your righta and won't allow other members to have theirs. On a vote being taken the proposal that the question be referred back to a special meeting of the Building Committee was carried by 0 votes to 15.</p>
        <p>I done?</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>your rlghte heirs. that the question - meeting of the Buildiug 4 Ovotea to 5. PPP—</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>The Li'xammer, Saturday, December 21 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>eilms e .Tr, rt. , Pzft*,,L HOPPING. -74'tk .</head>
        <p>eilms e .Tr, rt. , Pzft*,,L HOPPING. -74'tk .</p>
        <p>/YR!'</p>
        <p>I,</p>
        <p>on this occasion; it was one of most enjoyable features of the evening. Another charming duet "In spring- time " (N ewton) was given in sprightly, finished style by Miss Boyd and Mr Cheetham. Mr Foden Williams is well-known to Warringtonians as a clever and refined entertainer, and he enhanced his reputation by several original sketches, the best comprising modern "potted " versions of Shakespeare's plays and a capital skit on musical comedy. Mr H A Palmer's conjuring exhibitions, aptly described as "magic made merry," were a decided novelty, the stream of witty topical patter with which he accom- panied his tricks and his unconventional methods of utilizing the good offices of youthful assistants, all helping to keep the audience in rare good humour. Mr Palmer's was uncl mbtedly one of the best enter- tainments of its kind ever presented in Warlingtou. Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bac., played the pianoforte accompaniments with his customary skill and taste. During the evening Mr J Fulton-Smith read a letter from Colonel J Reid Walker, one of the managing directors, regretting his inability to join tub gathering</p>
        <p>ONTARIO'S STRATFORD-ON-AVON. An examination of the names of towns and cities in Ontario indicates in a striking manner how extensively that province has drawn on the world for its settlers, a condition of course common to Canada. For install° e we find among the thriving cities of Ontairo the names London, Paris and Berlin. All through the province of course there are old•established towns named after the mother town in the old land, in which it is evident that the original settlers have endeavoured to model the new settlement upon the old. Stratford, Ontario, is termed the classic city, and the visitor from Shakes- peare's land who comes to this Outwit' city would enjoy finding it on the River Avon, and finding, too, such names as Romeo Ward and Falstaff Ward, and civic names that recall the great dramatist. Again, London, Ontario, is on the Thames. It has its Covent Garden and its St. Paul's Cathedral. It is little more than a century since Colonel the Hon Thomas Talbot, the British officer formed the Talbot settlement in Ontario. There he burned the forest to form a farm. On part of that ground now stand the progressive cities of London and St Thomas.</p>
        <p>DON'T Loon OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER,which deepensto a nattirsdcolour ins, few days.Lockyer'S maintains a youthful anpearance.Large both everywhere. Post freel/6. Bedford Laboratory, Louder S.E.</p>
        <p>wanted a DAW intercepting depot they could raise the money quite easily. The speaker referred to the profits Made by the valous municipal undertakings and contended that municipal trading in Warrington had justified itself up to the hilt. He spoke strongly on the lac of foresight of the Corporation in allowing the proposal to construct a dock at Wilderspool to lapse and contended that in that matter the town bad "sold its birth-right for a Mess of pottage." While speaking hignly of the gallantry and willingness of the fire brigade, be st.bmitted that it would be more st.tizf.ctory to recrglzs .the police orce and make the fire brigade a depart- ment of it, thus etsuring that trained men would alwass be on the spot when the alarm was given. With regard to small holdings and allotments, he said the great difficulty gas to obtain suitable land at a reasonable price. Alluding to the work of the Bridges Committee, the Alderman said that after wrangling like Kilkenny cats for nearly twenty sears, the Council had tiv last agreed upon a bridge which would be about the widest, in regard to roadway, outside London. He believed that when completed it would be a credit to the town end to the engineer whom be was proud to know was a Warrington man. The Council bad built "THAT LITILE IRONMONGER'S BRIDGE"</p>
        <p>across the river at Howley. It was better than nothing, but if they had had municipal statesmen instead of municipal pedlars on the Council, they would have pro- vided a road bridge there, Comelttees which consisted of the whole of the members of the Council, incident- ally commented Alderman Bennett, were very often like a bear garden. He thodght the Education Committee was a much better-constituted authority than the Town Council. Only twenty-one members of it were ate members of the Council, the others being experts specially co-opted for their knowledge of Education. He often thought it, would be a good thing if they could have some measure of co-optation on the Town Council. In some respects he was a democrat to the finger tips ; but after all the mere rattling of the bailot box did not always bring the best men to the top anu democracy, judged by some of our municipal counoils, would be accounted a failure. Pending the time w,ben every intelligent citizen—loan and women— bad a vote and was able to 1143 it wisely, the principle of co-optation bad much to commend it. Tire differ- ence in the atmosphere of the two authorities was very marked indeed. One was orderly and decorous is its procedure; the ether was often exceedingly unruly—so much so that they all sometimes felt ashamed of it. Touching upon the duties of- the Old Age Pensions Committee, the Alder- man said it must he a source of satisfaction to every member to realize how many miserable, squalid, dull lives had been lit with new and unexpected sunshine throu2h the passing of that beneficent Act. (Applause.) Referring to the representation of the Council on the local Issuraoce Committee, the Alderman said that,with all its faults, the Act would do more to relieve human suaering :than any measure passed in our time. (Applause.) The tendency of modern legislation was to Impose increasing liabilities upon local authorities and the great problem of the future would be the financial problem. It was absurd that GREAT LANDLORDS WllO DREW ALMOST FABULOUS</p>
        <p>. GROUND RENTS Out of the towns, from improvements to which they bad contributed only in an infinitesimal degree, should abaplutely escape the burden of the rates which other j people had to bear. The fault he had to find with ' the last Budget was not that the unearned increment war to be taxed but that the proceeds were to be applied to the imperial exchequer and not to the local authorities. The income that arose from the growth of the town should be applied in relief of local expenditure. Having dealt With incqualiteS of rating, the Alderman put in a plea for a universal income tax— stiffly graduated—for imperial and loaal purposes. He extended his criticism of the party system to national affairs and suggested the possibility of applying tbe method of government by committees, from which politics could-he eliminated, to the work of Parliament. appealed to his hearers not to be deterred from oJnsidering proposals merely because they ware dilated Socialism. Lord Hugh Cecil had advised them to approach Socialism not in a bitter spirit of hostility but to treat each pro- posal on its merits. If . justice were done to existing interests there was no reason—theoretically at any rate—why almost any conceivable form of enter- prise should not be rhanaged by the municipality. rirtally the alderman exhorted his hearers to assist those who were trying to rear on the banks of the Mersey that "city beautiful" Which had been the dream of his life. (Applause.) Mr J GRIFFITHS, in proposidg a vote of thanks to Alderman lienuett,said the great work which t till laced the town with regard to street improvements would necessitate the tapping of ties') sources of income, and it was only fair that those individuals who reaped enormous revenues from land should be compelled to poutribute. (Applause). Mr G TOULMIN seconded. Ile said he had always understood that the word Conservative meant " one who would leave things alone " but Alderman Bennett would revolutionise Warrington if he had the chance. (Laughter and applause). The vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>. • • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MUNICIPAL FREE-LANCE.</head>
        <p>A MUNICIPAL FREE-LANCE.</p>
        <p>kLBERVIAN BENNETT AND POLITICS IN COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Crisp Comments on Local Affairs,</p>
        <p>Alderman Arthur Bennett, "inveterate Imperialist and tariff reformer and strong opponent of the present Government " as he described himself, gave a characteristically outspoken address before the mem- bers of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association on Tuesday evening. This enterprising body, in order to familiarise. itself with municipal problems, has formed a "Town Council " of its own and Alderman Bennett readily agreed to lend Ma encouragement to the innovation by giving an address on "The functions of a Town Council" Mr E LLOYD JONES, who presided, introduced the alderman as one of Warrington 'a noblest and best eons. Although he differed from them politically, tLey recognized that he was courteous in debate, convincing ,in argument, and always capable of taking opposition 'in the best spirit. (Applause.) Alderman BENNETT, R ho had a very cordial reception, said he had come to that meeting feeling that perhaps,to some extent, they sympathised with an ideal of his—an ideal which he had never hidden, to which to had always been faithful;and of the truth of which he was more fully convinced to-day than over—namely, that there was very little connection between municipal and imperial politics, that party politics in the adminis- tration of a town were a mistake, and it would be altogether to the good if they could devise some better and more suitable way of dealing with local affairs. Be had never been able to see any valid reason for running municipal elections on party lines except one ; and that was that the method tended to keep the party machinery in order. That, howeter, wrs nt ufflu'ent excuse, It was</p>
        <p>• A "SELFISH METHOD, for it was really exploitieg the energies of the people of the town and distracting them from the best modes of muuicipal service. What he said applied to both piffles, and it seemed a great pity that the best and highest interests of the town should often be trodden underfoot and wilfully overlooked merely to promote the efficiency of the party machines. Criticising the s 3 stern in some detail, the alderman said the principle of choosing candidates through the Ward Committees as at present constituted often resulted in the selection of men who were not the best qualified to carry out the higa work entrusted to them. Then again men were oppt.sed on party lines entirely irrespective of their merits as municipal administrators. Again and again in his experience men who had been of invaluable service in the Council had, for no reason whatever, except that they happened to be of the wrong , political colour, been opposed by men who had I often been cyphers and utterly useless fcir Conned work. Such Councillors bad been rejected and tbe town had suffered accordingly. The issues frequently raised 'on the platforms duriug municipal contests were utterly irrelevant and bad no bearing on municipal affairs. Ile had smiled to notice how the questions of Home Rule, religious education and the Ilse had been dragged into the contests and people were expected to wax enthusi- astic and work heart and soul to return a candidate on such questions which had absolutely nothing to do with the case. It was foolish—in fact he did not think</p>
        <p>ANYBODY OUTSIDE A LUNATIC ASYLUM would devise such a s)stem. But Carlyle had said of the people of this country that they were mostly fools end our method of electing Councillors gave colour to his contention. (Laughter.) Toe true line of demarca- tion in municipal work should be the fossils and the progressives. A curious fact was that, in regard to municipal affairs, he had known many</p>
        <p>HOPELESSLY ANTEDELUVIAN FOSSILS who had borne the honoured name of Liberal and many inveterate Tories who had been exzeedingly progressive and enthusiastic. In the actual woxk of the Council party colour hardly ever counted at all, the only excep- tions being the election of Mayor and Aldermen. Iu regard to the latter, politics had sometimes played an unwoithy part and they had elected aldermen from political motives rather than from considerations of suitability. If the honour went by seniority, it was absolutely fair to both parties and a man got the due reward of his service. It was notlan ideal method—the ideal would be to pick the best men whether their period of service was long or short, but it bad proved a good working rule. With regard to the Mayoralty, if they adopted the simple plan of each party electing the Mayor in turn, there would be no reason to introduce the cloven hoof of politics on that issue. It was</p>
        <p>LITTLE LESS THAN A. SCANDAL that so few of our leading citizens were prepared to assist in the government of the toe n, but be conceded that one of the reasons was the party lines on which elections were lought. Many men were not prepared to go round, hat in hold, begging for votes and spending :U0 or £4O, with the prospect that in three years they might be thrown out by an opponent who was utterly unworthy of the position. At the same time it showed a great want of public spirit that more prominent men were not pre- pared, even with the drawbacks of the present system, to give some time to the service of the town to which they owed so much. (Hear, hear.) The Alderman then passed under review the work of the various com- mittees of the Corporation. Alluding to the functions of the Watch Committee, he expressed satisfaction that the police were taking steps to bring to juitice those hair -brained motorists who ignored the safety of the public in order to promote their own selfish .pleasure. Referring to the tremendous importance which some people attached to the duties of the Finance Committee, the Alderman said that, boiled down to its plain essentials, nine-tenths of the work of that committee might be done by a third- rate clerk in an ordinary office, because it largely took the form of checking invoices and pay sheets and sign- ing them. He expressed regret at the adoption of the Government audit which might occasionally lead to injustice. While he would be the last man in the world to deny the necessity of the strictest audit, he thought the method required in a town like Warrington was a thorough commercial audit and not a little niggling, petty audit of the type they sometimes got from per- manent officials whose o .e idea seemed to be to find out some unimportant technicality which was not in order and thus put the Council to the trouble of the possi- bility of a surcharge. On the question of the rates, the Alderman said many of his fellow townsmen seemed to look at the whole universe through THE LITTLE PEEP-HOLE OF THE RATES, forgetful of the filet that rates were not the be all and end of all of existence. While he was in favour of economy when it was compatible with efficiency, he realized that high rates, good government and a pro- gressive town were better than low rates' bad government and an unprogressive town. (Hear, hear.) Having referred to the magnitude of Corporation expenditure, the alderman pointed out that in COOOSO- don with the rates the Finance Committee had, in spite of its other puerile duties, important functions to perform and its chairman (Councillor Wilkinson), had every reason to be proud of it. Continuing the alderman said he honestly believed that, with the additional powers conferred upon municipalities by the Housing and Town Planning Act, the work of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee was more important than that of any other committee, or in fact, of two or three other committees combined. In the past individual caprice had been allowed to run riot, with the result that we had towns with miserable narrow alleys, and dirty streets, wards like St John's without a single open space, nothing that was pleasing to the eye and much that was deleterious to health. Altogether, instead of having beautiful cities, we had CITIES OF DREADFUL NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Some of the irOWs"e-s- in Warrington would not compare with some of the pig sties he bad seen. flow any Christian nation, with a feeling of ordinary humanity, Could allow houszs to be huddled together in the way in which we saw them in many parts of Warrington, passed his comprehension. When he told them that one Councillor had suggested 30 houses per acre as an ideal standard, they would see what a great deal of leeway had to be made up. The Alderman commended the zeal which the Health Committee was showing in the demolition of slum property and said his only com- plaint against that body was that until recently it had been</p>
        <p>--- UTTERLEY APATHETIC WITH REGARD TO THE SMOKE NUISANCE. During the twenty years he had been in the Council, he did not think the floes inflicted for the emission of black smoke had amounted to £5, yet offences bad been going on from day to day under their very eyes. Smoke could be regulated by the introduction of scientific appliances, without hampering trade. Commsnting on the work of the Sanitary Works Committee the Alderman, while admitting the commercial success of the concern, characterisnd the pail system and the methods of disposing of the refuse as " barbarous, " remarking that he would not be satisfied until they got the water carriage system throughout the town, and a proper sewage farm. The Museum Committee, he continued, was the butt of every ribald joke in the Council—the one committee that was PERIODICALLY DELUGED WITII MUD by people who had not the ghost of an idea of its true duties and functions, and who were quite incapable ot seeing beyond their own noses. As a matter of fact, the committee ought to be honoured more thad any other, because it stood for what was best and highest in the life of the town. The Alderman deplored the fact that of the 100 000 books annually issued from the Library, 90 per cent were works of fiction, while the , demand for poetry represented only per ceut.As to art I if they wanted to buy a fine new picture, they had to get some rich friend to help them out, but it they</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FIYSTERIOUS RAILWAY TICKETS,</head>
        <p>FIYSTERIOUS RAILWAY TICKETS,</p>
        <p>Warrington Man Fined in London.</p>
        <p>At the Clerkenwell Police Court, on Saturday,Arthur Wilcox, 24, a tea merchant, of Forest-street,Warrington, was charged on remand with travelling from Rugby to Euston with intent to avoid payment of the fare (Cs 10d) and further with giving a false name and address.</p>
        <p>In evidence previously given,William Wordwellticket examiner on the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway, stated that on December 5 he found the accused in a third-class com- partment on the 10-40 a.m. train from Rugby. He pro- duced a ticket, upon which the date was written in ink with We words "press broken" in blue pencil. Wilcox explained that he had purchased the ticket at Latch- ford the night before and that the booking-clerk had said that the date press was broken and he would write the date in ink. The examiner added that other blank tickets and one marked" Press broken" had been found behind a carriage seat. Detective Inspector Robinson said that Willcox gave four names and addresses altogether' and eventually admitted that he wrote the date in ink on the ticket, and also the words in blue The accused explained at court that be found the ticket at Latchford, and thought he would use it. He offered to pay the fare, but the ticket examiner handed him over to the detective inspector. The station master of Latchford stated that the accused's father was an inspector on the line. The date press at Latchford had never been broken. Mr D'Eyncourt having said that he wanted more evidence as to how the tickets were stolen, John Thomas, a porter, of Latchford, near Warrington, was called on Saturday. Ile said that on December 4th he was acting as a booking-clerk. He knew the accused, and he did not issue a ticket to him on that occasion. The accused had often been to the office and had been left there temporarily. Cross-examined, the witness said that several weeks ago the office door was smashed but the tickets were not then missing. Peter Warren, the Latchford station master, said he checked the stock in November, and a number of tickets were missing, including the ticket bearing the number produced by the accused. Only a mackintosh was taken when the office was broken. Relief porters had the run of the office. The accused bore a good character as far as witners knew..</p>
        <p>Mr D'Eyncourt said it appeared to him to be a very bad fraud. He did not believe the accused found the ticket. A fine would be imposed of 40s. with three guineas costs. The magistrate declined to entertain an application for time to pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TRANS-CANADA MOTOR CAR TRIP.</head>
        <p>TRANS-CANADA MOTOR CAR TRIP.</p>
        <p>Despite roads that were almost impassable, the pioneer automobile tourists who set out to cross Canada from coast. to coast accomplished the trip in 49 d4ys. The tour was undertaken with the object of demonstrating how necessary a national highway is to the dominion. Every town through which the car passed pledged support to the movement. The trip was concluded at Victoria, where a banquet was given by the Automobile Association. Better roads for Canada is the aim of all connected with the movement.</p>
      </div>
