      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Pres</head>
        <p>Pres</p>
        <p>ints that Please</p>
        <p>WALKER'S BREWERY BAND</p>
        <p>CONCERT.</p>
        <p>For some years it has been the custom of the directors of Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd., to afford encouragement to the Brewery Band and an evening's pleasure to their NT orkpeople by holding an annual concert. There was tbe usual large and attentive audience in the Parr Hall on Monday evening, when a popular yet high-class programme was sub- imitted, and all were agreed 'hat the concert excelled in interest and variety any p, eviously arranged by the .firm. The band, under Mr. W. Winstanley's con- ductorship, continues to make good headway, and its 1 playing was characterised by crispness of attack and a nice quality of tone,in which the employment of wood wind instruments is no small factor. The overture " Crown Diamonds " (Auber), although allowing for greater contrasts of expression and detail than were actually realized, was a most painstaking and credit- able performance upon which all concerned are to be congratulated. The conductor gained individual distinction in the cornet solo "Maisie mine," very neatly played, and Mr J Hawes fully merited the unmistakeable appreciation with which his trombone solo" The death of Nelson" was received. The band accompaniments to these items were tastefully executed and carefully subordinated to the solo instruments. The vocal talent was of a high order. Miss Eileen Boyd, of London, who possesses a voice of excep- tionally fine range, gave a powerful yet unexaggerated interpretation of "The enchantress" (Hatton), and later she sang "Out on the rocks " (saintron), with a depth of feeling which created a great impression Mr Joseph Cheetham (tenor) delighted the audience with his artistic and soulful vocalization in "My Queen" (731unzenthal), "Somewhere a voice is ca ling" (Tate), and "For you alone" (Gheel), each being invested with marked expression. Mr Cuthbert Allan is a baritone with a voice of useful cilibre and compass and his piquant treatment of " The Drum Major "(Newton) and " Old Squire Bob " (Fox) caused those popular items to go with a delightful swing. The pathetic element in " Trooper Johnny Ludlow" (Tentp'e) Was also effectively brought out. We have rarely heard the duet" Watchman, what of the night? " (Serjeant) .so beautifully sung ashy Messrs Cheetham and Allan</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SHAKESPEARE THEATRE, LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE THEATRE, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Brilliant and Comical Pantomime Promised. On the afternoon of Boxing Day at the Shakespeare Theatre, Liverpool, Mr W W Kelly presents Mr Tom B Davis' fifth " Shakespeare " pantomime " Jack Horner" and it is pleasant to be able to state that there is splendid promise of a most brilliant and amusing " annual."</p>
        <p>The company is well described as "a record all-star cast." Composing a corps which is destined to develop that which competent authorities state is likely to prove the funniest pantomime ever seen in Fraser-street, there are many accomplished and celebrated people engaged. Particular interest centres in the engagement of Miss Maidie Scott, who is to appear as Charity, a foundling. Miss Scott thus makes her first appearance in a Liverpool pantomime. Iler successes elsewhere have made pantomime history in I the cities where she has figured. She is altogether a remarkable comedienne, and it is certain that she is destined to win a high place in the regard of the Liver- pool public. She is a curiously characteristic artiste with a particularly marked and charming per- sonality, great natural gifts, and exceptional aptitude. Miss Lily Morris is sure to prove a most vivacious, exponent of the title-role, and the welcome reward of persistent laughter is certain to accompany the doings of Mr Ernie Mayne, Mr Harry Brayne, Mr Charles Nortm (the gifted mimic), and Mr Fred Allendale. Miss Daisy May will appear as the Princess of lieartsond Miss Annie Heenan as the good Fairy. Special dances will be given by the four Nelson girls, and the principals are to be supported by a par- ticularly largs sad ..lergetio chorus-body. The music is by Messrs S P Swanton and J A Robert- son and the latter will conduct. The pantomime will be produced by Mr Fred W Warden, assisted by Mr Lewis Leslie, and the morning performinces will be as follows :—Thursday (Boxing Day), Friday, December 27th, and Saturday, December 28th at 2 p.m. Every Monday, Wednesday and Saturday tellowing at 2 p.m. during the next three weeks. From the week com- mencing January 20th each Wednesday and Saturday at 2 p.m, until further notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BRITISH COLUMBIA APPLES.</head>
        <p>BRITISH COLUMBIA APPLES.</p>
        <p>A large consignment of British Columbia apples., grown in the Okanagan district, has just, been received by the Agent General for British Columbia from hia Government for exhibition in this country. A portion of this fruit has been dispatched to the Manchester Winter Exhibition and the balance has been distributed in London and the leading cities of the United Kingdom. In the provinces these British Columbia. apples may be inspected by the public at the offices of the Canadian Government Emigration Agents at Liver- pool, York, Birmingham, Exeter, Glasgow, Aberdeen. Dublin, and Belfast ; at the office of the Grand Trunk railway at Glasgow ; and at the offices of the Canadian Northern Railway at Liverpool, Bristol, Birmingham. Plymouth and Glasgow. For its recent exhibit of apples at the Royal Horticultural Society's Hall, Westminster, the British Columbia Government was awarded a gold modal, whilst a Certificate of Merit, was awarded for the Winter ban ins apple.</p>
