      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>WE CORDIALLY WISH ALL OUR READERS A MERRY CHRISTMAS AND A HAPPY NEW YEAR.</p>
        <p>The strange case of Mr THE BEGINNING Bonar Law becomes OF THE END. exciting. There is one London newspaper -which pretends to suppose that his appear- ance at Ashton-under-Lyne was a sort of .apotheosis. It came out on Wednesday night (for it is an .evening paper) with an heroic cartoon which seems to have been inspired by a last century picture of the storming of Seringapatam. The Unionist. leader in the House of Commons, a gigantic figure, is advancing in front of an army .against the walls of a battered and crumbling fortress. He waves his sword over a vast plain dotted rah lines of soldiers and diversified by clouds of cannon smoke. A shell is bursting within about nine feet of his face; but of this threatening incident he is gloriously oblivious. Climbing up the steep, over fortifications that are tumbling down on the other heroes who follow in his rear, he bears aloft the flag of Imperial Unity, which, of course, is the Union Jack—somewhat in- correctly drawn. We accept this picture with cordial welcome as not merely a graphic, but an illuminative illustration of the present political situation. Nothing, it seems to us, could more completely display the sham sentiment, the pretended enthusiasm, the preposterous pretentiousness of those who still maintain the twin propositions that they alone are the defenders of the Empire, and</p>
        <p>. ,„</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>that the Empire can be maintained by no other means than their quack Tariff "Reform "proposals. We are about to see the end of a grea ' imposture. For years past the Conservative Party has been weeding out from among its representatives in Parliament the few men who were strong enough to stand up against the Chamberlain heresy. There are only two men now sitting on the Conservative benches —the brothers CECIL—who have maintained any sort of allegiance to Free Trade principles. Even they are only half-and- halfers, and one of them would not now have been in Parliament if the University of Oxford, the last refuge of reactionary principles in all other matters, had not taken him up when he was turned out of the representation of Greenwich because he would not say " Yes" on all occasions to his cousin, Mr ARTHUR BALF OUR. The Opposition in Parliament is deep-dyed in pledges to Tariff "Reform." Tariff Reform even according to Mr BALFOUR, is to be the first realised item of Conservative policy when the party led by Mr BONAR LAW and Lord LANSDOWNE comes back to power. And what is the condition of things to-day, after all these protestations that only Tariff "Reform "could help the people and serve the Empire? The proposal that is talked overtamong Conservative members of Parlia- I ment when they look into each others faces in the lobbies is whether Tariff " Reform " ought not to be dropped. It was said of the Roman augurs that they could not look into each other's faces without laughing. The case of Conservative members • of Parliament at this moment is far different. They can hardly look in each other's faces without tears. On this subject let us be frank with each other. Tariff" Reform" has been a conscious imposture from the first. There were, no doubt, some members of Par- liament who brought themselves to believe. There were many men outside who thought that it might be made a gooc'. thing for others as well as themselves. Almost all Conserva- tive candidates wrapped themselves round with the idea that the working man of the -cou- excl</p>
        <p>intry could be got to think that the change of commodities is a bad thing for himself. In the main Tariff" Reform" was taken up as a piece of good electioneering claptrap. We have no hesitation in saying that in the minds of most of its principal advocates it has had no other value. And now the truth is coming out, and the walls of the shoddy Tariff " Reform " fortress are falling about the ears of its defenders, as are those of the tumbling structure in the picture which was devoted to the Apotheosis Of Mr. BONAR LAW. What we have now to look for is the aban- donment of the cause in whose behalf the Duke of Westminster has been giving dinners at £l,OOO a head. Only one event, which we will not now name, is necessary as a signal for a most astounding debacle, which,shame- ful or shameless as it will be, will be the salvation of the country.</p>
        <p>In his trenchant and TRADE UNIONISTS well-reasoned reply to BEWARE! Lord Wolmer, M.P., which we publish in another column, Mr. Clement Edwards, M.P., justies to the hilt the serious charge that the Tory party is engaged in a conspiracy to smash Trade Unionism. If there is one fact which emerges more conspicuously than another from the recent proceedings in Grand Com- mittee on the Trade Unions Bill it is that the Tory representatives were working tooth and nail to re-saddle the trade unions v;ith those disabilities which the Government have made it their business to sweep away. Lord Wolmer and his colleagues have deliberately pursued a policy of obstruction designed not only to make the unions politically powerless but also to cripple their use- fulness in the purely industrial sphere. Lord Wolmer's own proposal to separate the benefit and strike funds, so as seriously to restrict the resources at the disposal of the workers in the event of a prolonged dispute, is a striking case in point. We should imagine that the youthful member for Newton has 'ere this regretted his temerity in throwing down the gauntlet to so doughty a champion of the workers as Mr. Clement Edwards, for the only effect has been to call further attention to the discreditable tactics which his Lordship and his allies have thought fit to adopt.</p>
        <p>NEW COUNTY JUSTICES. LOCAL APPOINTMEATS. The County Sealkeeper at Preston yesterday added the names of thirteen gentlemen to the Commission of the Peace of the County of Lancashire. The local appointments are : Mr. Joseph Bailey, 25, Wilson-Patten Street, Warrington, for many years connected with the works of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., a prominent Wesleyan. Mr. Joshua Ball, Southworth Hall, Croft, a leading agriculturist. Major G. R. Crosfield, Lodge Lane, Warrington, director of Joseph Cresfiield and Sons Ltd., a Territorial officer and a pioneer of the National Service League. Mr. J. Rymer Young, The Lindens, Grappenhall, Chemist. An ex-president of the Pharmaceutical Society. Mr. Wm. Paiiclough, Causeway Bridges, Burton- wood Mr. Wm. Thompson. Sankey Bill. Earlestown. Mr. Thos. Vicars, The Knoll, Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>Prince George Edward Alexander Edmund, fourth son of the King, completed his tenth year yesterday.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOWOZI</p>
        <p>Warrington's" Insurance Babies" will be born with a silver spoon in their mouths, thanks to Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>* - The question of the moment, politically, is: Will the Tory party throw over Tariff Reform? *4*</p>
        <p>Mr Bona.r Law has led them into the deepest morass of any they have encountered years, though their experiences of themany wilderness have been many and varied. He has " cemented" the ranks with gun- powder! . .</p>
        <p>* * Lord Larsdowne put the cat among the pigeons in November when he withdrew the Referendum pledge, as not being "a business proposition." The" Liverpool Courier" and other Conservative journals in the North at once told the public that this would not do for Lancashire and Yorkshire—without whose backing a Conservative ministry was impossible.</p>
        <p>Now, Mr. Bonar Law has upset all the remaining apples in the Tory cart by the maladroit proposal to consult the Colonies about food taxation in this country. This must be the new Imperialism or Colonial Preference." We are to invite the Colonies to confer with us and advise us about food taxes. Was there ever such a crazy proposal from a responsible party leader? Either it denotes second childhood, or we have arrived at the days of nursery politics.</p>
        <p>- The people of this c *ountry will not have food taxes at any price, and the " Times " and "Daily Mail" have already awakened to the fact, and virtually thrown Mr. Bonar Law over. The " Times" tells him plainly that he "must not attempt to shift the onus of food taxation on to a conference. of Ministers from overseas," while Colonial statesmen have already made clear their determination to have nothing to do with such a fatuous proposal. *</p>
        <p>The Tory party could not be in a worse plight, and no wonder its supporters all over the country are quietly discussing the advisability of dropping for ever Tariff Reform, which hangs round their necks like an "old man of the sea." Their leading newspapers are beginning to see that with- out a constructive policy embodying real social betterment they have not the slightest chance of securing the suffrages of an enlightened democracy. To go on on the bad old lines is confessedly hopeless. What the party requires is both a new leader and a new Policy. Mr. Chamberlain's legacy of Tariff Reform has merely buried them fathoms deep in national mistrust. *</p>
        <p>* * Mr. Crosfield and Mr. Parker both discuss in another column the impasse at which Toryism has arrived under the reckless leadership of Mr. Bonar Law, and their views are worthy of every reader's careful perusal. Mr. Crosfield is particularly vigorous in his condemnation of this party trafficking with the food of the people, and winds up with the following clarion call to his supporters: Let every Ptogressive then see ta it that the men who dare to threaten the humble homes of England with these evil and iniquitous taxes on throe things on a hich our people absolutely depend for health and comfort Are hissed and hooted off the boards. a a With the article in this issue our contri- butor A.M. brings to a close. the first section of his account of early Warrington Nonconformity. Many other branches of the subject remain to be treated, notably the very interesting Dissenting cause at Hill Cliffe, the Warrington and Penketh Quakers and the Presbyterian Church at Risley. The rise of the Warrington Methodists, Inde- pendents and Baptists has yet to be described before the subject is complete, and the Primitive and Quaker-Methodist histories have many interesting features. Our contributor would like to make the subject complete but fears that the interest of his readers may flag. An expression of opinion wquld be of value to us before the subject is resumed in the new year.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett filled the Junior Liberals with awe on Tuesday evening when he addressed them on "-The functions of a Town Council." They could not have selected a municipal representative capable of outlining Town Council work in more lurid colours and at the same time. giving,</p>
        <p>them an adequate idea of its multifarious character and importance. As a man with ideals, too, the Alderman could be relied upon to inspire his youthful hearers with progressive aspirations, and the fact that he made his lecture the vehicle for a few pet diatribes would probably do no harm at all. At the least he must have given his audience plenty of food for thought—and Junior Liberals are expected to think</p>
        <p>* * As a past Chancellor of the Exchequer, Alderman Bennett had, naturally, a valuable opinion to express on the duties of the Finance Committee. Here it is : " Boiled down to its plain essentials, nine- tenths of the work of that Committee might be done by a third-rate clerk in an ordinary office, because it largely took the form ef checking invoices and pay-sheets and signing them-" After this we must presume that the local Chancellor only works once a year—and then in the limelight—when he presents his annual budget speech and discloses deep secrets with the air of an oracle. It is fortunate that our Chancellor has no salary. * Though expressing himself a " strong opponent of the present Government," the Alderman had some nice things to say of the Old Age Pensions Act and the Insurance Act. The latter, he said, "would do more to relieve human suffering than any measure passed in our time." We wish the Alderman would express this same candid opinion on one of those platforms from which the Tory Member for the Borough finds so much that is uncomplimentary to say about the same Act. We are well aware that Torie, in their heart of hearts, do approve of National Insurance, but few of them are candid enough to give credit where credit is due—especially when the due is that of their political opponents. * It is high time we had a new Board of Guardians, or at least econsiderable change in the personnel. On Thursday the Rev. , A. M. Mitchell had a resolution before the ' Board which would authorise the payment of extra out-door relief at Christmas to each family within the Union—a practice adopted in many Unions up and down the country at the festive season The meeting was adjourned from the previous day under suspicious circumstances and when it met there was a bare quorum. No sooner did Mr Mitchell rise to move his resolution than Mr. G. Mather walked out of the room, leaving the meeting without a quorum and quite unable to do anything. No wonder Mrs Collingwood said "it was the meanest thing she had ever seen." As a</p>
        <p>result of Mr. Mather's peevish action, and the non-attendance of other mem- bers, the poor will have to do without their Christmas Box. Not only so, but members of eight other Unions in War- rington (chargeable to their own Unions) will also be deprived of the Christmas fare actu- ally authorised. Mr. Mather once described himself as 'boss of the show" when Chair- man of the Guardians. He requires the com— plete enlightenment which only the rate- payers of his own parish can give him at the next election. * *_</p>
        <p>It is reported that Colonel Hall Walker M.P., for Widnes, will retire at the end of the, present Parliament. The Liberals of the Division, at the instigation of the junior section, have invited Mr J B Martindale, of Manchester, to contest the next election. * * *</p>
        <p>Those fortunate people who get two turkeys given them this Christmas will do well to patronise the local Cold Stores and keep one of the birds nice and fresh for the New Year. People with game birds can do the other thing and obtain equal satisfaction. All tastes are catered for no w-a-days. * *</p>
        <p>According to the "Yorkshire Herald " (December 10th) a proposal from the Redcar branch : "That in view of a possible election the Tariff Reform League should give some clear definition on the lines they propose that the tariff should be levied "was discussed at a meeting of the Council for the North Yorkshire district of the tariff Reform League, and was "considered impracticable." It always is. • * * Town planning locally has advanced another stage, the Local Government Board having authorised the Town Council to pre- pare a definite scheme for their approval. It will be remembered that an inspector of the Board conducted a preliminary inquiry a short time ago, and this sanction is the out- come. The Corporation intend to include in their scheme all the unbuilt-upon land within the borough, and the next stage will probably be to confer with the landowners interested.</p>
        <p>1 a •••1.1%."0"%e 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LOCAL PARS,</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARS,</p>
        <p>Personal</p>
        <p>At an Examination held on December 16th at the University of Liverpool, Mr R Isherwood, son of Mr Edward Isherwood, Bewsey-road, succeeded in obtain- ing his Diploma of Membership of the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons. Musical Success. Nellie Woodhouse, of Padgate, succeeded in gaining honours certificate in the intermediate section at the recent London College of Music examination held in Liverpool. She is a pupil of Miss Atkiuson, A.L.C.M. 7, Fitzherbert-street, Warr ington. Miss A ilia Joan Greenall, pupil of Miss Mary Gornall, was successful in passing the primary division of the local school examination of the associated board of the R C.M. and R.A.M. recently held in Liverpool. Taxi-Cab Accident. _</p>
        <p>A Corporation scavenger, named Thomas Delooze, of 83, Wellington-street, was injured by a taxi-cab in Winwick-road at four o'clock on Monday afternoon. Whilst loading a tumbler it is supposed he was stepping out from the side of the cart, when tke splash-board of a taxi-cab caught him and knocked him down. The two back wheels of the car passed over the man, who was conveyed to the Infirmary. He was found to be suffering from a large cut on the back of the head and one on the forehead.</p>
        <p>ANTIIRACITE STOVES, clean, cheerful, smokeless, no flimeB.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND. 51, Bridge at. Warrington.—Advt. The late Mrs Delmage. The funeral of the late Mrs Delmage wife of Sergeant Major W Delmage, formerly stationed at Warrington, took place on Thursday of last week at the Chester Cemetery. The coffin was borne to the grave by Sergt - Major White, Quartermaster-Sergts. Tyler and Moran, and Sergt. Steel, of the Royal Army Pay Corps. The Rev 3 Phillips (chaplain of the cemetery) officiated both at the chapel and graveside. The chief mourners were: Sergeant Msjor W Delmage (husband), Miss Marion Delmage (daughter), Mrs Cain (sister), Mr and Mrs Chester (brother-in-law and sister), Alderman D L Hewitt (Deputy-Mayor Of Chester), Lieut. Baines (South Lancashire Regiment, Orford Barracks), Mr and Mrs Hodgson (Warrington), and Mr H H Smith. There were many beautiful floral tributes, including one from the Warrington Cycling Club, of whicti Mr Delmage was captain when he left Warrington. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse- market at.—Advt. Philomathic Society. - • The fortnightly meeting of this Society was held on Wednesday evening last at the Old Acadamy, Bridge Foot. The President (Mr Jas. Ball) was in the Chair, and there was a good attendance of members. The subject for the evening was a general discussion on the " Philomathizatiou of spare moments." Practic- ally all the members present took patt in the discussion, which was of a very interesting and instructive character; and many and varied were the methods revealed of how the philornathic spirit was cultivated in leisure hours. The charms and benefits of physical exercise, of conversation, reading, music, and the study of foreign languages were mentioned, and the value of a right use of spare time as tending to a development of character and pdrsonality was pointedlput by several of the speakers. The meeting terminated with a short illustrated address by Mr H H Taylor on the philoniathic side of lantern-slide collecting.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY.—Jcseph Rodgers &amp; Sons' celebrated Knives and Forks, &amp;c.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge. at., Warrington.—Advt. _ _</p>
        <p>Latchford Wesleyan Band of Hope. On Monday a tea and social was held in connection with the above Band of Hope. The schoolroom, which had been gaily decorated with flags and bunting, presented a very festive appearance and a Christmas tree on the platform, laden with about a hundred toys,proved a source of great interest to the children. At 6.30 nearly ninety-members sat down to a tea which was followed by games that caused much merriment. Next came the singing of some temperance hymns. The back part of the room had been thrown open to parents and friends who thoroughly appreciated the hearty singing of the childien. The event of the evening,in the'youngster's estimation, was the distribution of the toys which was done by Mr E Fogg dressed as Santa- Claus. As the children passed off the platform after receiving their present Mrs Strange presented each child with an orange, and the eveumg concluded with the oral pledge which is taken at the close of the usual weekly meetings. Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse. market st.-7-Advt. Education Committee and Church Schools.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening a meeting of ratepayers and parishioners, convened by the Rev E C E Crtrleton, vicar of Holy Trinity, was held in the Patten Hall, to consider "(1) the waste of public money, caused by the creation of vacant places in existing schools, through the over-provision of school accommodation by the Education Committee, and to (2) devise means in the interests of the ratepayers for protecting the older schools built by private benevolence without rate assistance." Mr John Hesketh presided over a small I attendance. The Rev EC E Carleton said anything like speculative building for school accommodation was undesirable, especially when ratepayers'. money was being spent. When the Evelyn-street schools were built, accommodation was provioed for 1,260 children. There must be some ndcalculation some- where, or otherwise there would not have been .100 or 600 vacant places created in the schools in the 'surrounding district, including such efficient schools as the Heathside, the Arpley-street, Holy Trinity and Sankey Council schools On the proposition by Mr E A While seconded by Cm. Broadhurst the following resolution was unanimously passed : "That this meeting pledges itself to do its utmost to support and preserve Holy Trinity Day School in its efficiency as an elementary school, that it may continue to be available for the educational needs of the district."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>Photo, Burrell.</p>
        <p>IN DUTCH DRESS. Back Row: Messrs. J. H. Kertland, W. R. Jackson, and G. H. Walker, Front Row: Doris Wilkes and Roy Jackson.</p>
        <p>Amongst many notable evenings Tuesday last will remain one of the brightest and most enjoyable in the recent history of the Warrington Photographic Society. It was an echo of the Ptiotographic Convention bold in Amsterdam 'ln glorious weather this summer. The three Warrington members who took part in that happy series of meetings gave us, in the broken " Dutch " of their Fatherland, an account of their adventures which were at once pressive and impressive. Dressed in Dutch costume with Master Roy Jackson and Miss Doris Wilkes taking the part of a little boy and girl from Holland, they kept the very large audience (over 50), in a ripple of merriment for the whole evening, while more than 200 excellent slides showed not only the points they wanted to illustrate'. but also tbe unstinted amount cf labour the three heroes must have devoted to their favourite club's entertainment. A dainty supper of " English pilsener " and cakes was served in an interval and the thanks at the end, though heartfelt and 1. ot merely formal, only showed how little those present could express their appreciation of a memorable evening. Three good flashlight portraits of the five who took part were afterwards obtained by Mr. Burrell.</p>
        <p>Plcturedrome.</p>
        <p>Important Sale of Ground Rents. Messrs John Mainwaring and Co., conducted ark important sale of ground rents in Warrington and Orford, amounting to 1,068 per annum, at the Blue Bell Inn, on Wednesday afternoon. Forty-two lots were offered and all were disposed of with the sleep- tion of the mo-t important —the works in Folly-lane, occupied by the Longfod Wire Co., Ltd., and the Lynton Wheel and Tyre Syndicate Ltd, the ground rent of which is :£.232 10s. The other lots were sold to the various lessees and others interested in the property, the total amount realized being £15,430. The prices represent between 221 and 24 years' pur- chase. Messrs Henry Greenall and Co. were tho solicitors to the vender.</p>
        <p>- On Monday and Tuesday the picture, "Tommy's Sister" will he shown. When Tom is ill, the girl retains his job for Lim—and does the work well." The Brand Blotter" and a special programmie are announced for Thursday (Bank Holiday), and on Friday and Saturday " Tke Blacksmith, " a great domestic drama. The proprietors hold the exclusive rights for Wart ington of this most powet ful play. Motor Offence. - •</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday, Robert Walter, motor driver, 10, Grove-street, Cobridge, Stoke-on-Trent, was summoned for driving a moor- car at a speed dangerous to the public on October 29th. Sergeant Wool lcott proved that on the day in question he saw the defendant driving a motor-lurry along Sankey-street at a rapid pace. Tbe car travelled at least 20 yards before defendant could pull up.— Defendant was fined in the costs 7s.</p>
        <p>The Boys and the Cartridges. The hearing was resumed at the County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Jitege Stanger, K.C., of the action brought by Reginald Goulding. an infant sueing by his father, Edmund Goulding, joiner, 13G, Oldham-street, Warrington, against John Maiuwaring, auctioneer, Warrington, to recover £lOO damages for the loss of an eye.—Mr Dehn, barrister (instructed by Messrs Diggle and Ogden, of Manchester) appeared for the plaintiffs, and Mr Robert McCleary, barrister (instructed by Messrs Arthur Browne and Co.), repre- sented the defendant.—The case for, the plaintiff was that certain cartridges were left lying about on vacant land near Oldham-street by a man named Harry Rowe, a contractor, for whose action Mr Mainwaring was responsible. One of the cartridges exploded and struck plaintiff in the eye, which was consequently lost. Operations were being conducted to blast out concrete beds, and the explosives were left about on the land under circumstances which it was contended were most negligent. Three boys found the cartridges which had been placed in a drain pipe.—For the defence Mr McCleary argued that the approximate- cause of the injury was not negligence on Rowe's part. but was due to a wanton act on the part of the children.—After hearing legal arguments from counsel, his Honour announced that he would give judgment at the Salford County Court.</p>
        <p>Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LmoH, Horse- . ,</p>
        <p>market st.—Advt. The Dramatic Society. In another column the Warrington Dramatic Society announce the production on January 113th, 17th and 18th, of the popular drama "Sunday." Few plays have been more warmly received locally than " Sun- day " on the occasion of professional visits, and the work of the local amateurs %lin be watched with interest. The cast will include many new players whose reputation is in the making and some old favourites. The performances will take place at the Royal Court Theatre, and the proceeds will, as usual go to local charities.</p>
        <p>Factory Act Prosecution. At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, James B Davies, baker, 6, Lord Nelson-street, was summoned for employing a young person beyond the legal laws contrary to the Factory Act. Mr J McCaghey (factory inspector), said the hours of employment in the work- shop were from 6a.m. to 6 p.m. On two occasions the boy worked all night .A fine of 108 and costs in the first case and 20s and costs in the other was imposed. Arthur Greenhalgb, packing case-maker, Thyme- street, was summoned for a similar offence in connec- tion with three of his employees. Mr McCaghey found three boys working I_l, quarter hours beyond the legal time.—A fine of 108 and costs was imposed in one case and defendant was ordered to pay the costs in the other two cases.</p>
        <p>Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse-</p>
        <p>market st.—Advt,</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Eccles.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers, particularly those connected with Wycliffe Congregational Church, will deeply regret to here of the death cf Mrs Eccles, wife of Mr Christopher Eccles, of 18, Canseway-avenue who passed away in the early hours of Monday. Although the deceased lady had been in failing health for two years or more, the end came unexpectedly. Although of a retiring disposition, Mrs Eccles was a very earnest and active church worker. As recently as November 2Sth she attended the meeting at Wycliffe Congrega- tional emit ch (of which Mr Eccles is secretary) when the call was sent to the new pastor. She did not feel sufficiently strong to venture t 9 the sale of work on the 12th inst., but sent her gibs and also her contribu- tion to the " Christmas cheer "fund, which is annu- ally dispensed at Wycliffe. By Sunday last she had become very much weaker, and her many friends were deeply grieved to hear of her death the follow- ing day. Two sons and a daughter are left with affectionate remembrance of a good mother. Deep ' sympathy is expressed for the family by the colleagues of Mr Eccles at the district offices of the London and North Western Railway at Arpley Station and by the large circle of friends he has made during his resid- ence in Warrington. The funeral took place at Damen on ThurEday. Before the body was removed from Warrington a short but iropressve service was conducted in the house by the Rev John Yonge. At Damen the last rites wera performed by the Rev 11H Snell, pastor at Duckworth-street Congregational Church, where Mr ar,d Mrs Eccles worshipped prior to taking up their tesidenos in Warrington. The mourners were chiefly members of the family. The goods manager at Arp7ey Station was represented by Mr Parkinson. A large number of floral tributes from Warrington included wreaths from Miss Hough, Mr and Mrs Ziba 'Armitge, Mrs and the Misses Robinson, Mr and Mrs T Winskill, The Office Staff of the London and North-Western Railway, Mr Eccles' Sunday school class, Deacons at Wycliffe Congrega- tional Church, Teachers at Wycliffe Sunday School, and the Thursday Afternoon Working Party. LAMPS, Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves.—J.S. GARLAND Bridge at. Worringtee.—Advt. Death of Mr. Joseph Besvdck. _ _</p>
        <p>Ask for Whitbread's Famous Ales and Stouts in screw-bottles.—Sole agent, PETER LEIGH, Horse- market at.—Advt. Holy Trinity Young Men's Association. A whist drive and dance proxoted by the members of this association took place on Thursday evening of , last week in the Patten Hall, Winmarleigh-street. The whist drive commenced at 7.45 and proved very enjoyable, and at ten o'clock refreshments (kindly arranged for by the Misses 11 Leigh, J Fergusson, Gill, L Walsh, Mrs R Walsh,and others) were partaken of at the whist tables. The whist prizes were pre- sented by Mrs Carleton, the winners being: Mrs H Bibby, Miss Gittens, and Miss N Taylor,Mr W Barnes, Miss L Walsh (playing as gentleman), and Mr A Hodgson. The room was then cleared, and dancing was indulged in until two a.m. The M.C.'s were Messrs It Walsh and S Worthington. Excellent music was supplied by Messrs Broadhurst(piano) and A Bennett (violin). The proceeds, after paying expenses, will be devoted to the reduction ottbe debt incurred thrcugh extensive alterations to the elul.: room,</p>
        <p>FOR MODERN FIREPT,ACES and Cooking Ranges cora bining economy with simplicity try J. S. GARLAND Ironmonger, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>- - - Blackburn Philanthropic Friendly Society. The Warrington staff, under the superintendency of Mr Joseph Fairhurst, held theit annual dinner and social evening at the Winnaarleigh Cafe en Thursday last. A very enjoyable evening was spent. Messrs George Whalley and Joseph Westwell represented the Board of Management. 'rhey vitro supported by Mr J W Dickinson, (chief inspector),Mr Joseph Fairhurst (superintendent), Mr G A. Leach (assistant superinten- dent), and the Medical Referees, Drs W Valentine, W Latham (Earlestown), and J S Manson (Warrington). A. telegram was read from Mr R Peel ',secretary), apologising for his absence. Mr Peel also sent a con- gratulatory telegram to the Warrington District for the good progress they had made. The following agents took part in the evening's programme :—Messrs S H Smith, J W Marsden, J Smith, T H Birkenhead, S Dancalf, J Pilling, and F H Allison. rho concert programme included the following artistes :—Mr Sam Aukland (humorist), Miss Seddon Earlestown, Miss Wright, High Legh, Mr L,T Rigby. The accompanist was Mr J \V Foden. Bank-street Mission Cricket Club Social A social gathering in connection with the Bank- street Mission (Ragged School) Cricket Club was held on Saturday at Atkinson's Cafe, the occasion being the presentation of a bat and ball to the hest batter and bowler of the season by the President of the club, Mr John Dodds. Tvvenry-three sat down to tea, followed by an entertainment given by members and friends from the Ragged School. A. piano solo was given by Mi. Ellison, followed by a song by Mr A Bennett. Mr Dodds, who tock the chair, said that when he accepted the invitation of their secretary. Mr W Hall to become president of the club, he promised a bat and ball to the best batter and bowler, and that was his reason for being with them. When reading , the analysis of the batting averages he noticed that Mr G Carless had won, to use a racing expression, , only by " half a head." if Mr II Wright would accept it he would also g.t e tint a bat, as he had so nearly I come out on top. He then risesented the bat to the winner, Mr G Carless, and a ball to Mr J Savage. Suitable replies having been made an excellent enter- tainment was gone through by Messrs J Nurse,Ellison, F Sedgeley, R Ashworth, H Wright, H Nurse, II Savage and A Bennett. Several humorous anecdotes ; were also given by Mr Dodds. A vote of thanks was, on the motion of Mr E Fear, eeconded by Mr H Wright, passed to Mr Dodds for making the presentation.</p>
        <p>The news was received with general regret of the death of Mr Joseph Beswick,tobacconist,Hersemarket- street, which took place somewhat suddenly at Holwood, Latchford, at about six o'cicck on Tuesday evening. For some time the deceased who was fifty-six years of age, had suflered from asthma but so l recently as Wednesday of last week he was attending to his business as usual, leaving the shop at 10 o'clock at night. By his urbanity and courtesy he had gained a large circle of friends—in fact he was one of Warrington's most respected tradesmen and he will be greatly missed. He was a member of the 1250 Lodge of Freemasons and held the office of almoner. He and his family had long been identified with Wycliffe Congregational Church. In politics Mr Beswick was a Conservatic e, but, -although he was a member of the Conservative Club in Sankey-street and the Junior Conservative Club in Orford-lane, he never took an active part in public affairs. He leaves two children to mourn his loss. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery- yesterday afternoon amidst many manifestations of sorrow and regret. The officiating clergyman was the , Rev W 13racecamp. The chief mourners were Messrs Joseph Beswick (son), William Helmick (brother), 1 James Houghton'(brother-in-law), John Beswick(uncle) Arthur Molyneux (brother-in-law) James Houghton, jr, C E Houghton, H B Houghton (nephews), William Mounfleld (cousin), James Clare (uncle), J Waters, W flainsworth, G N Vernon, C Newton, 'J Geerghegau, William Cherry. Drs Jago and Peacocke preceded the hearse. A large number of Masouic brethren were also present. Many beautiful wreaths were sent, including those from "The Junior Conservative Club," "The Directors of the Delbarwicic Pavilions Ltd," and "The Managers and staff of tbe Delbarwick Pavilions Ltd."</p>
