      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1912,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1912,</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR AND THE REV. W. T. JAYE. Sir,—l desire to thank Mr. Jaye for his courteous response to what I recognize on reflection to be a somewhat sharp and unkind criticism of a letter no intended to be offensive. I much regret to have stated my views without more personal consideration for the position and character of the writer.—l am. Sir, yours FREDERIC W. WILLIS. ThP Rectory, Warrington, December 18th, 1912.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL INSPECTION OF SCHOOL CHILDREN Sir,—NVith reference to the letter in your las Saturday's issue, if " Scrutatoz " cares to disclose his identity publicly or to write to me privately I shall be happy to give him the intimation he requires. Ile may, at all events, rest assured that the Local Authority does not engage in any work which is ultia vires.—Yours faithfully, 'TOLMAN KINGDON, Chairman, Medical Inspection Sub-ComMittee.</p>
        <p>-true-sued man will arise capable of commanding the respect of all right thinking persons. When that time arrives he will not lack followers ardent and eager to thrust these swaggering Falstails hack into the hideous gloom whence they ought never have been allowed to emerge. A NONCONFORMIST.</p>
        <p>, , : 1..7 s is r to the ~, -,14., t , ~ii,il _ . EIITOR.t.' i k -1 ''. ~- 1: •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD AND INDUSTRIAL "SPEEDING-UP."</head>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD AND INDUSTRIAL "SPEEDING-UP."</p>
        <p>Sir,— In amplification of your report of my speech at the Trades Council's social evening last hursday, allow me to point out that after saying that there was no justification for continual and excessive speeding up, and that employera should be on their guard against that, 1 referred to the remarkable extent to which employers of labour throughout the industrial world had failed to realise how far reasonable hours of labour could be adopted in place of excessively long hours without economic or cc mmercial loss. In not a few cases shorter hours, during which men are brisk and alert and in good trim for their work, have meant positive commercial gain. I will not trespass on your space by repeating what I said as to those other cases where shorter hour s du involve increased cost of production, and the efficacy of the International Association for Labour Legislation in dealing with such cases and overcoming the difficulty they present by means of International co-operation. My remarks under that bead I see you already have been good enough to report in last Saturday's paper.—Yours etc., A. H. CROSFIELD. 3-1, Hyde Park Gate, London, S.W. December 19th, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>UNCERTIFIED DEATHS AND PREMATURE BURIAL.</head>
        <p>UNCERTIFIED DEATHS AND PREMATURE BURIAL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Referring to a sudden death reported in the " Warrington Examiner "of 7th inst, may I venture to say that the association for the Prevention of Premature Burial has for many years past called public attention to the appalling number of persons who are annually buried without a medical certificate of death or any scientific evidence that they were really, and not merely apparently, dead? During the fourteen years 1897 to 1910 inclusive, the deaths in which the causes were not medically certified in England and Wales only, amounted to no fewer than 132,767. In alluding to the difficulties attending the diagnosis of apparent and real death, the late Professor Huxley, E.R.S., said :—" The evidence of ordinary observers on such a point as this is absolutely worth- less. And even medical evidence,unless the physician is a person of unusual knowledge and skill, may have little more value." That being so, who can presume to tell how many unfortunate persons,whose deaths are not scientifically velified, go down to the grave alive, but, as in certain forms of suspended animation, quite unable to give the slightest sign and manifestation of life? It is, truly, much too painful and horrifying to contemplate. To those of your readers who realise the present-day dangets of premature burial, and desire to assist in their removal. I should be pleased to send literature on the subject on receiving an envelope, stamped and addressed.—Thanking you for your kindness, I am, sir, your obedient servant, JAS. R. WILLIAMSON. 100, Chedington-road, Upper Edmonton, London, N. Dec. 12th, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>REV. E. C. E. CARLETON'S</head>
        <p>REV. E. C. E. CARLETON'S</p>
        <p>"MISCALCULATIONS."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the course of a characteristic attack on the Education Committee at a meeting in the Patten Hall Mr Carleton is reported to have said " there must he some miscalculation somewhere " ; apparently this "miscalculation " was made by himself whilst prepar- ing his speech ; at least, that is the most. charitable explanation of his misleading statements. As dis- proving his contention that the Local Education Authority has provided school places unnecessarily and extravagantly, it may suffice to point out that, even now, out of 45 elementary school departments in the Borough, 27 have more scholars on their registers than there is recognised accommodation for. Twelve months ago, of course, the conditions were worse; for the opening of the Evelyn-street school has given some relief. At the schools specifically named by Mr. Carleton there were in November, 1911, scholars enrolled in excess of recognised accommodation as follows : At Arpley-street, mixed, 165; at Heathside, girls, 38; at Holy Trinity, mixed, IS. Anyone caring to go further into the matter can easily verify the above figures and get many more which will prove that we, in Warrington, are still a long way from an ideal state of affairs in regard to school accommodation. I venture to think that Mr Carleton's energies would be better directed and more in accord with the spirit of the Gospel of which he is a minister, if he tried to help instead of to hinder those who are endeavouring to secure for every child—even for the poorest—the opportunity of being taught in rooms well lighted, warmed and ventilated, iu premises thoroughly clean and sanitary. _How far some of our local schools fall short of such a standard is evident to the eyes and nose of any one who visits them; and I am confident that the Education Committee is rightly giving effect to the wishes of most parents, and of most ratepayers also, in steadily striving to mend or end these unsatis- factory conditions.—Yours faithfully, ALF. J 3OLLEY. Dec. 19th 1912. 34, Crcsfield-street,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>COMPULSORY SERVICE And the Nonconformist Conscience.</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY SERVICE And the Nonconformist Conscience.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, G. Dale, is apparently unable to understand the Nonconformist conscience and even you Mr Editor are content to playfully poke a burnt sties at this poor bit of jelly. And yet strange as it may seem it still exists. Although there does not as yet appear any modern Elijah to sorrowfully confess himself as the sole remaining prophet of the Lord there is every reason to believe that there is still left in England a remnant of obscure souls who have not bowed the knee to the latest Baal. Nonconformists cannot rid their minds of the warning that " thou shalt have no other Gods before Me," and that their Master is declared to be the " Prince of Peace," Who, although He confessed that " evil must come into the world," added the solemn warning, "Woe unto the man by whom it shall come." They are not unmind- ful of Shakespeare's admonition, "How oft the sight of means to do ill deeds make ill deeds done." Ani- mated by the latent spirit of the three Hebrew youths recorded in the 3rd chapter of Daniel, who, when it was demanded of them why they refused to bow before the golden image upraised on Dare's plains, declared that not only would they not do so but even if God could not deliver them from the resultant terrors of the firey furnace they were unwilling to violate their consciences by the degrad- ing act. So we of the Nonconformist breed refuse to bow to this new degradation. We believe that the strong sense of moral rectitude and the fearless love of right which is the inalienable possession of a true Englishman is sufficient to repel the successful advance of the intruder. And if God is unwilling or unable to defend His own moral law then we are prepared to go down. Those of us who have this faith regard old branches of the war league under whatever guise as enemies to the human race and a clog on the wheels of progress. If Christ came to preach peace on earth and goodwill towards men we cannot but regard those who desire to see a nation in arms as being nothing short of what the old diviners called "Children of the devil." We are unwilling to accept a definition of freedom as being that of a man slinking in the shadow of a wall feeling anxiously 'in all his pockets for an extra cartridge and trembling at every shadow cast athwart his path. It is time some new St. George should slay this wretched hydra-headed monster, and consistent Non- 'conformists are not without hope that some prominent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RIGHTS OF ANIMALS.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHTS OF ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l do not wish to encroach further on your space with this controversy, except to say that Miss Kidd's last letter shows that the anti-vivisectionists deny the science of bacteriology and its practical applications in elucidating and combating the problems of disease. It is well the public should know the position they have t asen up, and how dangerous and futile at e their teaching and propaganda. ith regard to Miss Kidd's protest against my charge of misrepresentation of Sir Frederick Treves, I trust you will find space for the enclosed letter of Sir Frederick's from the British Medical Journal of the 30.1 ult.,—Yours faithfully, J. S. MANSON. 8, Winmarleigh-street, Warrington, December 15th, 1912. AN FIVIVISECTION IN GLASGOW. Sir,—ln Dr Had wen's letter under the above heading the following passage occurs : have quoted Sir Frederick Treves fairly and squarely, and I repudiate his anbsEquent assertion that improper tee has been made of his statement." The first part of this sentenca embodies an important confession, for it shows that the anti- viviscctionist's idea of fair and square quotation is to cunningly isolate one particular sentence from its context in order to loist upon it a meaning which it obviously does not heir. The paragraph from my Birmingham address (oelivered id 1888), when fairly and squarely quoted, reads as follows:— Dr. Halstead expresses his conviction " that there should be a law compelling all surgeons to practise en animals the operations for circular suture of the intestine and for intestinal anas- tomosis.' I hope this view will not commend itself t ) the legislators of this country. Many years ago I carried out on the Continent sundry operations upon the intestines of dogs, but such are the differemJes between the human and the canine bowel that when I came to operate upon man I found that I was much hampered by new experi- ence, that I had everything to unlearn, and that my experiments had done little but malt me to deal with the human intestine." The champion of fair and square quotation would add to the above the following from n letter to the " Times " (dated April 18th, 1902), in which I pro- test against the outrageous methods of the anti- vivisectionist in connexion with this identical paragraph : "No one is more keenly aware than I era of the great benefits conferred upon suffering humanity by certain researches carried out by means of vivi- section. With regard to the second part of Dr. fladwen's statement, it is difficult to speak with moderation. For years the above observation of mine has been used in advertisements, pamphlets, and speeches as affording evidence that I condemn all vivi- section experiments as useless. For years I have been so pestered with correspondence On the subject that I hare had to keep in band a supply of reprints of the " Times " letter above alluded to in order that I might deal with this ever-recurring matter with the least inconvenience. The com- munications I receive are for the most part, of two types. Some open with such a sentence as "Is this another antivivisection lie?" while others libel me with the offensive assumption that I am opposed to vivisection, and conclude by asking me to speak at a drawing-room meeting against vivisection or to subscribe toe branch. I hope that Dr. Hadwen will continue to make his fair and square quotations, for they are very efficient in exposing the pitiable controversial methods of his party, while, on the other hand, they provide the opportunity of sending wholesome literatore to the many who seem—even in this enlightened age—to be still in some doubt as to the truth.—l am etc., Kingston-on-Thames, FREDERICK TREVES. Nov. 24th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ROMANISM v. PROTESTANTISM.</head>
        <p>ROMANISM v. PROTESTANTISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lam rather surprised at the attick made on Protestants in your last issue, by Mr Richardson, and, I doubt whether. if he had been a Protestant attacking Romanism in a Romanist country, be would have been permitted the same amount of liberty. Thanks to The liberty we have hitherto enjoyed, under a beneficent Protestant government, in this dear land of liberty everyone may sit under his own vine and fig tree none daring to make him afraid. Though the term " Protestant " only dates from the time of the protesting Princes against Popish corruption, the dogmas and beliefs are the same that were preached by Christ and his apostles in the first days, before pagan Borne imported its pontiffs, legates, auuura, vestal virgins, and other paraphernalia into the simple gospel story. It was to maintain this simple story and the free- dom to worship God that John Huss, John Wycliffe, Rogers, Bishop Hooper and a host of others laboured, all of whom except Wyclifie succeeded to the martyr's crown, and in his case, though allowed to die a natural death, yet with relentless animosity they dug his mortal remains from the tomb and burned them at the stake. It was to suppress this simple story that the Waldensees of Piedmont, the Huguenots of France, and the Vandois of Italy, were hunted to death and deprived of civil and religions liberty. Was there greater liberty in Rome under the Popes than Italians now enjoy under the Eing of Italy? In modern days Rome has always been on the side of darkness and superstition, and, ti quote Macaulay: " From the time when the barbarians over-ran the Western Empire to the time of the revival of letters, the influence of the Church of Rome had been generally favourable to science, to civilization, and to good government. But, during the last three centuries, to stunt the growth of the human mind has been her chief object. Throughout Christendom, whatever advance has been made in knowledge, in freedom, in wealth, and in the arts of life, has been made in spite of her, and has everywhere been in inverse proportion to her power. The loveliest and most fertile provieces of Europe have, under her rule, been sunk in poverty, in political servitude, and in intellectual torpor, while Protestant. countries, once proverbial for sterility and barbarism have been turned by skill and industly into gardens, and can boast of a long list of heroes and statesmen, philosophers and poets. Whoever knowing what Italy and Scotland naturally are, and what, four hundred years ago, they actually were,fitall now com- pare the country round Rome witti the country refund Edinburgh, will be able to form some judgment as to the tendencies of Papal domination. The descent of Spain, once the first among monarchies, to 'the lowest depths f degradation, the elevation of Holland, in spite of many natural disadvantages, to a position such as no commonweath so small has ever reached, teach the same lesson. Whoever passes in Germany from a Homan Catholic to a Protestant principality, in Switzerland from a Roman Catholic to a Protestant canton, in Ireland from a Roman Catholic to a Protestant country, finds that he has passed from a lower to a higher grade of civilization. On the other side of the Atlantic the same law prevails. The Protestants of the United States have left far behind them the Roman Catholics of Mexico, Peru, and Brazil. The Roman Catholics of low Canada remain inert, while the whole Continent round them is in a ferment with Protestant activity and enterprise. The French have doubtless shown an energy and an intelligence which, even when misdirected, have justly entitled them to be called a great people. But this apparent exception, when examined, will be found to confirm the rule ; for in no country that is called Roman Catholic has the Roman Catholic Church, during several generations, possessed so little authority as in France. The literature of France is justly held in high esteem throughout the world. But if we deduct from that literature all that belongs to four parties which have been, on different grounds, in rebellion against the Papal domination, all that belongs to the Protestants, all that belongs to the asserters of tile Gallican liberties, all that belongs to the Jansenists, and all that belongs to the philosophers, how much will be left ?" Dr. Wylie dealing with the persistent aggressions of Rome says : "This conflict, intelligently viewed, reads most emphatically the lesson, that in every condition and in all ages Popery. is the same restless, intriguing, and malignant principle. No reverse can subdue, as no concession can mollify its intolerant and tyrannical spirit. Though humbled in the dust one moment, and seemingly a poor, pitiful, cringing thing, it is the next as ready as ever to set its foot upon the neck of kings, and to strangle in its grasp the liberties of nations. You cannot break its pride, do to it what you will, nor satisfy its ambition by any amount of power and dominion whatsoever. Chase it to dungeons,—strip it of all possessions,—still with the poverty of Lazarus it will combine the pride of Lucifer. Thrust sceptres into its hand,—lieap diadems upon its head,—it will clamour for more power, and strive avariciously to grasp more wealth. Leave it to itself, and not a vestige of liberty will it suffer to exist. While a single right of a single human being remains unin- vaded, it will not cease to plot. Such a principle you cannot regulate,—you cannot reform,--you cannot</p>
        <p>restrain,—you can only destroy it; and destroy it you must, if you would save society, or give peace to the world. And beyond doubt it is the duty of all to unite for the overthrow of what is equally the foe of the rights of all and the liberties of all, —a system which would tread out every spark of intelligeoce,and stifle every aspiration for freedom' and which would entomb the world iu the dense shadow of a universal and colossal despotism." I.et me in concluding remind Mr R;chardson that the period of Rome's retrogression in religious matters coincides with the founding of the Jesuits. Let me also call his attention to the sublime declaration of the prophet: " Hast thou not known, hast thou not heard, that the everlasting God, the Creator of the ends of the earth, fainteth not, neither is weary ?" And again "lie doth according to his will in the army of heaven, and among the intisbitants of the earth, and none can stay his hand, or say unto him, what doest thou 9" Finally let me tell him that if Protestantism is right, all the powers of Rome will not destroy it, however low its numbers may sink, and, if Rome is right its professor need not be so troubled about a few Pro- testants.</p>
        <p>"Follow with reverent steps the great example Of Him whose holy work was 'doing good'; So shall the wide earth seem our Father's temple, Each Joe ing life a psalm of gratitude." I am &amp;o.</p>
        <p>PHIL SQL; OD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNINti.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNINti.</p>
        <p>RACE AT LATCHFORD. DUAL LOCAL VICTORY. PEnp.</p>
        <p>By the lind permission of the Parka Committee, Victoria Park, Latchford, was the venue for the Novice Championship of the West Lancashire C.C.A. The arrangements for the race had been placed in the hauds 'of the Warrington Athletic Club, whose energetic bon secretary, Mr Charles Berry, mapped out a splendid course for the event which took place on Saturday last. The distance was about ISO yards short of five miles, made up by three complete circuits of 'the Park, also three larger circuits comprising a portion of the adjoin- ing land and pasAng through the Park each tims with the addition of about a furlong for a run at the finish.</p>
        <p>Toe going was on the hea"vy side and provided sufficient test of the stamina without racing the youngsters over any ploughed land, which the curse was slightly curtailed to avoid. Fourteen teams had entered and the absence of St Helens Parish Church was due to missing their train and despite a slight delay in consequence Mr Water's flag fell to a capital stars without the Rev Chavasse's bo3s contributing any of the 74 starters whose various coloured costumes presented a pretty sight as the raced for position. Rignt from the start the pice was a cracker, especially when it is borne in mind th It the race was confined to those who had never won a prize or award of any kind in open competition and the old hands present, including Charley Straw, erred but</p>
        <p>Wallasey 23-50 ; 38 E Davies, West Cheshire, 28-57 • 39 L Brown, Liverpool Boundary, B ; 40 El Hood, Sutton, 29-2 : 41 A Dobson,Sef ton, 29-6 : 42 J Ferguson, Sefton, 29-1 ; 43 11 W Barnett, West Cheshire, 29-20 ; 44 H Parrey, Boundary B, 29-54; 45 J Elvers, Wallssey, 30-1 ; 46 D Black, Boundary A, 30-2; 47 W Smith, West Cheshire, 30-3 ; 48 fl 11 Boadle, Sefton, 30-4. The first four home of each team counted, for the lowest aggregate, for the team placiugs :—1 Warrington A (gold centre end enamelled medals) 17 points ; 2 Runcorn Tally Ho (silver and enamelled medals) 40 points ; 3St Helens Rens (silver medals) 48 points ; 4 West Cheshire. 95 points ; .5 Lancaster Primrose 96 ; 6 Sutton, 110 ; 7 Wallasey, 135 ; 8 Sefton, 143 ; others unpinned. . . . .</p>
        <p>- • i . the ndtviduil winner received an additional gold centre end emimelled medal ; all the medals are of a new design which includes the County Arms of Lanca- shire and Cheshirf.</p>
        <p>Hanson ran with credit to his mentor, F Shsdwell and showed form superior to any of his rivals, many of whom are distinctly above the average fr this type of contest; before the half distance was reached the field tailed for a length of over a quarter mile and Hanson met every challenge without flinching and would pro- b Ably have lapped several competitors in another circuit of the course, his most threatening opponents were Brown and McCoy, but they were eventually shaken off and lost their places to Carey, Nurse and Woods respectively; Pickering ran well but lost the seventh place to Walters after one ef a series of interesting struggles in which the local 3 outh shone with a "long run." Indeed the spectators who numbered several hundreds were treated to a good half hours sport as the see sawing between</p>
      </div>
