      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>slightly when they anticipated the usual " coming back to the field "set the mAjority of the 48 who completed the course did to in very creditable time. Details :</p>
        <p>INDITIDUAL PLACING.</p>
        <p>Time Min. Sec. 1 Joseph Hanson, Warrington A 25 50 2 J Carey, Sacred Heart 3 J McCoy, St Helens Rees 4 J Nurse, Warrington A 5 H Woods , 6 J Brown, St ftelens Rees 7 W Walters, Warrington A 8 J W Pickering, Runcorn 9 E Pye</p>
        <p>10 J Watson, Warrington A 11 J Harper, Runcorn, 26-50 ; 12 T Jones, Runcorn, 26-52; 13 N Stafford, IVarrington B, 27-0; 14 Ft Hanson Lancaster Primrose, 27-4 ; 15 H Atkinson,' Lannaster Primrose, 27-10 ; IG a Marsh, St Helens Rees, 27-16 ; 17 G Wakefield, Warrington A, 27-17 ; 18 J B Preston, West Cheshire, 27-32 ; 19, E G Nloutgornery, West Cheshire, 27-33 ; 203 H Eastwood, West Cheshi re, 27 -44; 21 J Chamberlain, Sutton, 27-45 ; 22 J A Woolley, Runcorn, 27-49 ; 23 H Slowcumbe, St Helens Rees, 27-50; 24 R Hugherr. Sutton, 27-51 ; 25 J French. Sutton, 27-59 ; 26 T H Hughes, Wallasey. 23-1 ; 27 S fl Edgar, Wallasey, 28-2 ; 23 J P vf ills, Sefton, 28-9 ; 29 J Thorniley, Warrington 11, 8-21 ; 30 .1 Cutler, St Helens Rees, 28-23 •, 31, F Price, ‘Varrington B, 28-21 ; 32, W L Rainford, Sefton, 28-26 : 33 W Ireland, Lancaster Primrose, 28-27 ; 34 J Baines, Lancaster Primrose, 28-28; 35 tj E Shevlan, St Helens Recs,2B-38; 36 W Carroll, Sacred Heart, 28.46 ; 37 E , Figuerido</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Six exceptionally clever acrobats constitute the Lodi Family who have scored such a great success at the Hippodrome this week They present an original sketch " Fun on the Alps " in a very acceptable manner, introducing daring hand to hand balancing feats, pantomimical tumbling, and numerous eccen- tricities of the acrobatic art, with such skill and alacrity as is very rarely seen. Not a single " slip" was made on the occasion of our v:sit and needless to say the audience were spellbound with admitation The scenery and general effects are excvllent and give a distinct 'tone " to the sketch. There is a strong flavour of originality surrounding the clever musical sketch " The absent-minded Professor " presented by Stoddard and Hynes. The " lapses" of the professor when the lady comes for her lesson are decidedly amusing and the volume of laughter which the audience cannot repress proves beyond d(ubt that the comedy is genuinely good. The professor is a clever insttumentalist and his playing is very skilful. ju ggling turns; Hera presents one of those exceptional the ones which are not monotonous in their simplicity. In a quiet, unostentatious way he performs all manner of sleight of hand tricks with a cleverness which defies -perception. Cavalli, described as the " sleepy ventriloquist," is a past master of the ventri- loquial art and he makes the doll "speak "in such a clear way that he is at once a favourite with the audience. The movements of his own mouth are barely perceivable, and a return visit of this clever entertainer would no doubt be welcomed by all. Eileen Hall, a charming soprano vocalist, is highly successful in two well-chcsen songs which she renders in a very pleasing manner. Her enunciation is perfect, her manner chic, and the whole turn capital. A graceful triple trapeze act is given by the Three Sisters Estop. One of the few new features in the turn is a simul- taneous performance of intricate evolutions, otherwise the feats are nothing above the ordinary. Jack Shevill is a comedian with a style which greatly pleases the " popular " parts of the house. "Brown's Day Off" is a clever humorous picture shown on the cinematograph. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. A capital Christmas week programme has been arranged and is headed by A D Robbins ani Co. introducing the " famous bucking bicycle " for which £5 will be given to the person riding the machine five consecutive tours of the stage. Other turns will be provided by Duncan's Royal Scotch Collies, wonder- ful exhibition of animal sagacity ; Trumpet Major Wix, bugle and cavalry trumpet soloist ; Musical McGregors, musical scene; Lytton and Desilva, operatic and ballad duettists; Frank Cass, comedian and vocalist ; and Florence Cooper, refined c'intralto with refined songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>thedifferent club representatives was always interesting although the placed teams were well on the premises throughout. The strong contingent from Runcorn came very hopeful of the result yet returned satisfied that they had put up a good performance against the very strong side which the Warrington youths proved to be. Several of the competitors shovied better form than previously but none more so than Herbert Woods, formerly of Rylands Harriers, and he should prove a useful addition to the junior section of the local club. As befitted a veteran athlete Mr Corbett assisted with the course arrangements and the premises of the Knots- ford-road Garage were generously placed at the disposal of the athletes for dressing accommodation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>TO-DAY'S FIXTURES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>The majority of the clubs will he running off their Christmas haudicy this afternoon, but Runcorn and Warrington are of those who have decided to leave this event until Christmas morning when their runners are due to meet at headquarters at 10.30 a m., so that their trips this afternoon will be more of the" go as you please " type.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CHRISTMAS DAY.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS DAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington A.C. have not yet decided their course which will probably he about seven miles over country starting from the bottom of Chester-road and the limit brigade should be prepared to start at it a.m at the latest, the other competitors must, be ready according to their time allowunces with the two internationals, C Vose and H Holbrook the last to start. To see the race a good point will be the other side of Walton Arches A good time and place to see the fhlish, 11.45 e.m. about a (1-tarter-mile up the Chester road from Warrington to Walton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" No Mother to Guide Her."</p>
        <p>The highly sensational drama, "No Mother to Guide Her," which has been so ably presented by Mr W H Flazes Company at the ltoyal Court Theatre this week, deals in a vivid manner with life in the world as it is. Although in parts gross exaggeration over-rides naturalness, there is a pathos about the production whice ODO and all appreciate. The characters are on the whole, well patronized, but the most realistic representation is by Mr H Ryeland- Leigh, who appears as the bibulous hubby, Bill Bunce in speech and gesture he is the typical accomplice of "crook " but his acting is best in the scene where he is supposed to suffer very much from the effects of excessive drinking. Mr Leonard Clarke is clever in his delineation of the character of Felix Campbell, the unscrupulous arch-villian, who endeavours to satisfy his evil desires at the expense of the hero aud heroine. Miss Betty Seymour is a charming Nan, the cheerful street urchin who has been neglected by an aristo- cratic mother. Miss Lila Wilde appears as Julia Patterson, an able foil in the hands of Campbell. Her 1, acting is forceful and worthy of commendation. Mr I Charles Dickens has the part of the Rev Tom Pelham in hand, and although a little more verve might be introduced in his acting he is fairly successful as the lover of Millicient Sastry, a charecter treated with consummate skill by Miss Roma Pendons. Mr George Gormley infuses real life into the part of I William Saxby the conscientious banker. The other characters including that of " Bob " are in able hands and the staging and general effects are excellent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LORD SELBORNE AT EARLESTOWN. CONSERVATIVES AND LAND REFORM.</p>
        <p>Lord Selberne spoke on Saturday at a meeting in the Earlestown Town Hall. At the beginning of his speech he made some references to the speeches made by Mr Lloyd George in Scotland, and said that if Scotsmen were offered parcels of land few would accept them, because there was no industry in the world which produced so small a return on the capital and labour expended as that of agriculture in this country. Agricultural land here bore Ilea, ier burdens in rates and taxes than any other agriculturnl land in the world, and it was also unpro- tected. No wonder agricultural w ages were low. One or the great questions coming for 4 ard was that of the wage of the agricultural labourer. We must give the land relief here either by taking off part of the burdens it bore or by giving it protection as Canada did. After- wards every possible precaution must be taken to see that the agricultural labourer w as the first man to benefit therefrom. Lord Selborne referred also to the position of the Conservative party on "Imperial Preference." They did not mean, be said, to go into negotiations with the Dominions with their hands tied behind their backs. They wanted freedom to negotiate,including a moderate duty on foreign wheat, and on foreign wheat only. Empire wheat was to come in free. Any evenue raised by such a duty would be used for the purpose of allevi- ating other burdens falling on the shoulders of the working classes—a simple business readjustment. He devoted much of the remainder of his speech to an endeavour to show that duty so raised does not fall upon the consumer. LORD WOLAIER'S TILT AT THE LABOUR PARTY. Lord Weiner, who presided, said he learned from the Radical Press that the Labour Party were searching about for a new polity. He could quite believe it. It 4:3,1i1d not be worse than their present one because they had. not got a policy at all. He thought, however, it would be impossible for the Labour Party to go In for a new land policy, because that was the special perquisite of Mr Lloyd George ; neither could the party go in for the policy known as the " Free Breakfast Table," because they had twice voted in favour of the tea tax, when it was proposed by the Unionist Party to reduce it. Thetefore, he con- I eluded that the only new policy possible for the Labour Party was that of the wiinimum wage. How they would work out that policy would be very interesting, but whatever the difficulties connected with it—and they were very great—he was perfectly convinced it would be absolutely impossible to arrange a standard minimum wage in this country as long as there were free imports into the country. What would be the sense of fixing a rate of wages at which men should be employed its England while it was possible to bring in free the work and products of ,the foreigner ? (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>"MEAN TACTICS."</head>
        <p>"MEAN TACTICS."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GUARDIANS AND CHRISTMAS OUT-RELIEF.</p>
        <p>CURIOUS PROCEDURE.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Board of Guardians have a curious way of showing that consideration for the deserving poor which they so loudly profess. Hitherto a majority of the Guardians have resolutely set their faces against the granting of any extra out- relief at Christmas-time, but this year, by what looks uncommonly like a discreditable conspiracy, the question has been neatly shelved and members have not been put to the necestity of declaring themselves for or against. ',l'he Rev A M Mitchell, who has con- sistently championed the cause of the outdoor poor in this matter, had given notice that at the Board meeting on Wednesday he would move : .. . . _</p>
        <p>(1) That each family within the Union receiving outdoor relief be allowed one cwt. of coal per week for one month. (2) That for the week ending the 28th December, 1912, the undermentioned extra relief be allowed to all persons in receipt of outdoor relief from this Union, viz :—Adults, one shilling each ; children, sixpence each. Before reaching this business, the Board had under consideratik n a £12,000 scheme for providing a new building for the accommodation of imbecile and epileptic patients. Although it early became evident that the general feeling was in favour of referring the scheme back for further consideration, there was a disposition in some quarters to prolong the discussion to the fullest extent. At 12.15 noon, the Chairman was about to call upon Mr Mitchell to move his resolu- tion when, on the proposition of Mr G MATHER, seconded by Mr A J JoLLUV, the Board took the very unusual course of adjourning until the following morn- ing. Whether this was done with the expectation that a quorum would not then be forthcoming, is a matter for conjecture. However. on Thursday morning there was just a quorum. Mr A J Jolley presided, and the other members present were ;—Mrs Collingwood, Rev A M Mitchell, Messrs W Furness, G Slather, C Brooks and M. W Seta°.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the fact that Mr Mitchell's resolu- tion stood first in order on the agenda, the meeting pro- ceeded to deal with the Clerk's report and correspond- ence. The latter included requests from eight other Boards of Guardians for the payraent of extra out-relief during Christmas week to recipients of out-relief chargeable to them in the Warrington Union. In the case of the Rotherham Union the amount mentioned was 2s. for each adult and Is. for each child. When the Res A M Mitchell rose to move his resolu- tion, Mr G Mather promptly left the Board room. Mr W C BROOKS then drew the attention of the Chairman to the tact that there was not a quorum. The Rev A M MITCHELL : This is mean and con- temptible, Mr Chairman. and I hope you will ignore it. Mrs COLLING WooD : It is too had.</p>
        <p>The Rev A M MITCHELL: I can only ray that if this sort of thing is done by a member in face of all the requests that have been read out, I don't think he is a fit person to sit upon this Board and administer the money of the ratepayers. It is an insult not only to me but to the people. It is a shame to say that the poor people in other Unions are to receive some extra Christmas allowance and here, when a proposition is put forward to do the same in the Warrington Union, it is first of all shunted to an adjourned meeting and then, in order that the matter may not be discussed and carried through, a member jumps up and leaves the Board, FO that our hands are tied. That is how I understand the matter.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : That is exactly the position. The Rev ANI MiTcHsu. : So that all these recom- mendations from other Unions fall to the ground. The CHAIRMAN : Yea, you cannot do anything. The Rev A M MITCHELL : Is it not a disgraceful thing? The CIIAIRMAN : That is a matter of opinion. The Rev A M MITCHELL : YOU said yesterday that I had disgraced myself. I don't think you meant it. I think you said it in the heat of the moment. But I think the member who has disgraced himself is the member who has left this Board, and I don't hesitate to say so. Further, as the result of this, I shall certainly put myself in communication ' with the Press and I shall make it quite plain how this Board of Guardians has acted in connection with the considera- tion of the poor at the Christmas season. I can only say, that I think it is a discredit to the Board and to the town.</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN: Well, we cannot do anything. Mrs COLLING WOOD It is the meanest thing I have seen, after coming all this way to support the resnlut;on. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr WO BROOKS protested against the conduct of Mr Mather. If he had not wished to discuss the resolution be should have stayed away but, having come, he should certainly have remained. The Rev A M MITCHELL I have exercised the greatest possible patience. I don't think you can find the least fault with the mover of this resolution. The CH AIR :NI AN.: I am not finding fault with anybody. The Rev A M MITCHELL : You should have taken my resolution at the beginning of the meeting, but I did got wish to raise the question and make any clamour. Mrs CoLLINGWOOD : It ought to have come before the correspondence. The Rev A M MITCHELL : Certainly. It was first on the agenda. The Board then rose. As there will he, no meeting before Christmas, it will be impossible for the poor to be granted any extra allowance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SUNDAY TRADING.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY TRADING.</p>
        <p>PETITION TO THE WATCH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>An effort is being made to create a strong publics opinion against Sunday Trading in the borough and a petition on the question, promoted by the "clergy, ministers of religion, burgesses, ratepayers and others of the borough,' is shortly to be presented to the Watch Committee. It reads as follows : "That your memorialists approach your committee with a deep sense of the arduous dories which the Council heti imposed upon you for fulfilment at the meetings from time to time of your committee. They therefore desire to respectfully assure your cominfttee that every effort which you may make to restrict the evil of Sunda,' Trading in the borough will meet with the approval of the great body of inhabitants of this town. Your memorialists have learned with deep concern that several shopkeepers during some part of Sunday open their shops for the sale of goods. In view of the unenviable results of Sunday trading, and having regard to the adequate facilities already afforded tor the reasonable supply of goods of every kind during the ordinary workdays, your memorialists earnestly and respectfully pray your committee to exercise, in the, public interest, your undoubted powers, and to stop Bli such means which are at present in existence for Sunda,. trading, which, in the opinion of your memorialists,.i. grossly unfair to the majority of the tradespeople ii this town, and degrading to the welfare and benefit et the inhabitants of the town generally. The pstitko is for the signature of those over lg years of age, and the Rev W Cooper is acting as honor- ary secretary. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) in commending th° petition to the congregation of the Parish Church said Sunday trading was not only an act of dishonc° to God, but it was unfair to those tradesmen wb° declined to engage in it. iro The Rev W Bracecamp, in a sermon at St. Pau' Church, said he should be agreeably surprised if it trn"" not found that there were nearly 400 shops open on Sunday last when the census was taken. When some the shopkeepers were spoken to about the matter the/ replied that their supporters included Sunday oche° children and members of church congregations, vi'b°' said Mr Bracecamp, profeFsed religion, but were ttY laity in general to buy their food on Saturday. jP regard to Sabbath breaking, we were living ill periloce times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
