      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WARRINGTON NONCONFORMITY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON NONCONFORMITY.</p>
        <p>ITS 250th TINNIVERSTARY</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON NONCONFORMIST'S "TRUE</p>
        <p>NARRACCION. " Discovery of a Remarkably Intel esting Document.</p>
        <p>The enmity of the Rector and the severe penalties inflicted for barn-worship brought a change in the temper of the Warrington Nonconformists. After ten years of conven- tides, enjoyed with comparative immunity from suffering, followed by the formation of a congregation in the Court House, it would come as a shock to be pounced upon and heavily fined for meetings held in a barn. The blow doubtless fell heavily and discretion began to blend itself with courage. The contumacious Richard Nicholls who "cared for neither the lieutenant nor his warrant" is now .carrying to Sir Roger Bradshaigh a document which, taken by itself, would prove that the Warrington Nonconformists were ever the most temperate, reasonable, law-abiding and considerate of men. _ .</p>
        <p>We would gladly know more of this episode, but there is no knowledge to be had save what is furnished by the document itself. Its deep interest makes it imperative that it should be quoted in full ; and our readers will not desire to miss a single word. This is how it stands as copied in Sir Roger's letter-book.</p>
        <p>A NARRATIVE BROUGHT MEE BY MR. NICHOLS AND LEECH OF WARRINGTON IN THE NAMES OF THE REST OF THE CON- VENTICLERS THERE, MARCH THE THIRD 1673(-4). _</p>
        <p>DATED WARRINGTON FEB. 27TH 1673(-4). To the Right Worp'll Sir Roger Brad- shaigh, Knt the Humble Addresse, Narraccion and Petition of several con- victed Persons, inhabitants of the Towne of Warrington etc. Hon,rd and Worthy Sir—The honour- able Relation you stand in unto your contrie and the Influence your Worshipp has upon all Publike Proceedings in this County and likewise the sense of damage and danger that wee might incur by the losse of your Worshipp's favour, should wee be altogether _ sylent at this tyme when such informaci- ons are brought to your Worshipp, against us as tho wee weare seditious, stoborne, insolent persons, defyers of Government, whose practices are of dangerous consequences. And (that which is worst of all) that wee may have thoughts to be Protected or defended herein by a new Warrant. These and the like things being layd to our charge that are of so haynouse a nature, wee Judge it our duty not onely to give your Worshipp all Possible satis- faction wee are able but alsoe for the clear- inge of our owne innocence that soe your Worshippe and the rest of our neighbour Gentlemen may be assured that the things which are ,reported of us are not soe, But are the groundless calumnies of some persons who have noe other way to carry on theire designs against us than by Renderinge us infamous and odious unto your good Worshipp. And in this good worke wee shall be both fruitful and as briefe as possible in giveing your worshipp a narrative of proceedings how they have bine carryed on amongst us since the tyme that the King's declaration for libertie came forth; which, as we Remember, was in March 71. At the coming out whereof their was noe little astonishment begott in us what might be the ground or reason of it. But Con- sideringe it was our sluty to acquiesce in the Actings 9f our superiours and not to Curiously enquire into the Reasons of them at last it begott in us this thought that it might possiby bee in the pursuance of, or have Relation into that provisoe in the later end of the Act concerninge the Kings prerogative; and that which did the more confirme us in our opinion was the generall submission of the Justices of Peace (or at least cersation of accation) in all places where his Majistis Lycenc es came. Yet notwithstanding we weare Resolved to see what others did, and how it would be Resented by our country Gentleman, before we would make any application to any person to be made par- takers of the privelidge thereof. Tho wee did earnestly longe for it in case it might come to us in the Rightj way. And thus things Continued with us untill the Generalitye of nonconformists Ministers both in Cittye and Contrye throughout the nacion had theire libertye to Preach publiquely and Places assigned unto them. and that in Peace and without controule or disturbance of any soe farr as wee either knew or heard. At last, hopeing all was Right in the businesse, we essayed to obtain a lycence for our old minister, Mr. Yates, and his hired house to be the place (which he tooke near() the Church for Convenience) ; which Lycence was signed the 25th of July after; and soe returning to us again, who for severall yeares had lived five myles off us in obedience to the lawe in that case, went into Sir Gilbert Ireland and shewed his licence (as it was meete he should) being then at Beusy. And some of us went to Mr Ward, our present parson, and tould him that notwithstandinge the libertye given we had a purpose not soe to cleave to Mr. Yates as to neglect his ministry, but desired the benefit of both; which wee did enjoy for a season to our great comfort and satisfaction and without the least intrenchinge upon Mr. Ward 's usuall time for his Publique Ministry. But after a while Mr. Ward began-to be highly dis- pleased (and what invectives he uttered in his Publique Ministry we shall let passe) and tould some of the neighbors that if Mr Yates would preach he would have him take a place further off from him ; and those that would heare Mr Yates, let them and those that would come to heare him might, and soe he should knowe his friends from his foes. At which sayinge of his the Towne was much trebled and went to him and tould him that they would still continue their contribution to him(which was some- thing considerable) and that he should not anyways be damnifyed by Mr. Yates comeinge into the Towne. But notwith- standing all that could be said to satisifye him it would not be accepted, but remove</p>
        <p>wee must. And ever after that tyme to disapoynt us (he) did alter his usall tyme of preachinge Unto Mr Yates his tyme, which was betyme in the morning and soone after midd day (before his). Thus when he had tried all meanes and saw it would not be better, we procurred another Place in the Towne and obtayn'd a licence for that, signed the 9th of December 72; and theare have in peace continued ever since (some- tYmes heareing Mr Ward and sometymes My Yates). Until of late Mr Ward beinge shred up against us by what means we ltnowe not, unless it weare from a ground- less conceit that a suspension which was</p>
        <p>sent out of Bishopp's Court at Chester against him might be procured by some of us—from which aspersion he hath since ' cleared us before severall wittnesses. By what hath bene sayde your good Worshipp may take notice that wee weare not over forward at the first cominge out of his Majesties declaracion but stayed a con- siderable tyme untill wee saw what was generally done; but did wee forsee it would give offence to any to take the Benefitte of his Majesties declaration; neither have we since carryed it on either contemptiously against authority or seditiously amongst our neighbors, But have Endevered all that ever we could for a Right understandinge and finding accomodacion betwixt the two parsons ; and have always judged our pre- sent libertye and freedom° to proceed from the clemency of our neighbouring Justices, which wee well knew they might interrupt at their pleasure; nor have wee at any tyme asserted or mentayned any other ground thereof And have alwayes looked upon ourselves as secure, and promised unto ourselves as much libertie and freedome as any others in the Nacion did enjoy, and all throw the Wisdom and Prudence of our surperiors (wee mean our owne Contrye Justices of the Peace) Untill Mr. Ward by his impor- tunity with Mr. Slater procured a Warrant against us. Upon the sight whereof Mr. Yates submitted and ceased Until the mediacion of some friends that wee made use off to Mr. Slater that we hoped would be prevalent with him That his Worship would not be offended if we began againe, and that he would graunt out floe more Warrants against us—both of which we had some intimacion would be condicended unto (with this provisio, that it must be at our owne perills)—we tooke the Incour- agement to begin againe, judging it might be a surprisall or earnest solicitation of the Informers which might have moved Mr. Slater to grant out a Warrant against us. In the meane time hearing nothing of your Worshipp's mind in the businesse, we hoped all would doe well. And thus things continued with us (till) Wigan Sessions last, at which your Worshipp and severall other Justices weare present, who not- withstandinge the Earnest importunity of the informers were hardly prevented with (as we heard) to grant out any Warrant against us, nor did wee either heare or could learne that any was granted out; For which gret favour and Clemency we (Judging it our duty) sent a letter of thanks by a spetiall hand unto one of the Justicos that was then upon the Bench, hopeing in due tyme it would be communi- cated to the Rest. But see it fates out that severall Warrants are issued out against us, as allsoe a letter from your Worshipp to the Ward wherein you declare your great displeasure against our proceedings and Judge it to be out of Refractoriness and contempt of Government that wee hould on in our former course. Now that your Worshippe and the restof our Country Gentlemen may bee assured that noe such cry me can justly be charged upon us. Att the sight of your Warrant and letter,which Mr. Yates saw upon Munday and Tuisday last (beinge made Publique by Mr. Ward). In humble obedience thereto and to athority is Resolved to decist. And if your Worshipp or any other Justice at the Sessions had but hinted the least dissatis- faction or displeasure against our proceed- ings to any person amongst us whom your Worship should have thought most meete wee believe theire would have beine a ' Readye Submission and none of this severitye would have needed. And theare we shall turne our Narracion into humble Supplicacion. And doe humbly beg (that since things are thus with us and trouble is falen upon us which we did not expect or doe beleave your Wo-shipp would have imposed had you understood the whole truth of the matter, as we hope your Worshipp will doe by the proceeding narrative which might have beine more large but that wee feare we have bine over tedious alreadye wheareby wee hope in some measure our innocency will appeare.) That your good Worshipp will be pleased to accept of us into your favour againe (the unworthy), which we had meritoriously forfeited had all things beine true which has beine Reported of us unto your Worshipp; And that in your wisdome you would find out a way whereby the Rigerous Execution of the Warrants may be suspended and see many inocent persons may not suffer, some of which in Testimony of theire good affection to his present Majestic ventred life and estate and all in Order to his happy Restauracion. And what your Worshipp thinks meete to doe in Answering our desire wee shall take as a favour, and as in duty bound shall Ever Pray. Sir Roger gives no answer to the Petitioners but upon the same day,remember- ing all the proprieties of the situation addresses his reply to the Rector. MY ANSWER TO MR. WARD, MINISTER OF WARRINGTON, CONCERNING THE CON- VENTICLERS ; MARCH 3RD, 1673. --</p>
        <p>HAIGH. Sir,—Tbis day Mr Nichols and other of your Towne Presented mee with a Narrative of some inducement and Encouragements they had which ' made them presume to hold theiro Conventicles as they did, and therein they also° seems to justifye themselves very much to be innocent of those Informacious which have rendered them insolent, and withal' have therein made theirs applicamon with a submission and promise of future olpdi- ence the Perticulars whereof they have promised to show you. In the meane tyme I have told them that I shall give you orders to lett the Constables knowe to gce on with the Execucion of their Warrants att theire Peril's, and to give an accompt thereof at the sessions of such goods or moneys as they have distrayned or Received a till which iyme I shall give floe other Answer to this paper but chill then represent it to the Rest of the Justices. At which tyme it will be necessary to have sufficient and positive Witnesses to the words that any of the Conventiclers have given out and hens charged with, since they seems to charge you and others with misioformations against them. I most con- fess the end of the Lowe to be to endeavour their suppression more then benefitt by fine ; and your beleife of their future demeanour° will goe farr with us. Soe Referreing the Rest till the Sessions I rest—Your affectionate freind to serve you, RoGER BRADSIIAIGH. I should be glad if these People would continue to be as Cautious as they seem to promise. The tone of the letter, and especially its postscript, suggests that .the champion of law and order was getting tired of the struggle. The very next letter in his book addressed to Parson Baldwin upon the subject of his preaching at Coppull Chapel makes a mild and almost wistful appeal to him to desist.</p>
        <p>NADINA AND THE " CHOCOLATE SOLDIER."</p>
        <p>" The Chocolate Soldier" is one of the most wonderful Musical pieces the stage has ever known. Originally produced in Vienna, it leapt into p3pularity in a single night, and wititin a few months ths whole of the continent of Europe was ringing with its famous waltz. At the present moment, there are twenty- three companies playing it on the Continent. It was next prOduced in New York, with similar success, and five companies are now playing it in America. It was then produced at the Lyric Theatre, London, and within a few months four companies were playing it throughout Great Britain. It has also been presented in Australia, South Africa, Canada and India, with equal success. It is estimated that the fortunate composer, Oscar Straus, has received more in fees from "The Chocolate Soldier " than has ever been earned by any musical play in the history of the world.</p>
