      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1912.</p>
        <p>Warrington troubles do not again figure upon the pages. The curtain falls suddenly leaving Parson Ward in the midst of his bad temper and the Nonconformists upon their promise of admirable behaviour. -</p>
        <p>A LETTER FROM RECTOR WARD. Before we take our leave of Rector Ward there remains to be quoted another of his letters.</p>
        <p>• Sir,— Ab( ut a yeare agoe there were Mr Samuel Yates and Mr. John Heapie both excommunicated for preaching° without a Lycense and have layne under excommunication till now. But 4 now that Mr Yates father and mother are both lately dead, who encouraged him in his blindnesse and stubbornesse and Heapie and his father fallen out because the yonge man is resolved to conforme, they both have sundry times requested me to imploy some one to procase there absolutions, for which they will readily pay with thanks. I thought you were as fit to undertake it as any and therefore if you please to doe it and send to me word what they come to I will send you ye money at speed. This all from your faithful friend and servant.—J. WARD.</p>
        <p>This communication is written upon a scrap of paper in a crabbed hand and has nothing to show to whom it was addressed. Its date can only be inferred from the state- ment that the father and mother of Samuel Yates are "lately dead." Unless the Rector were unduly prejudiced the letter shows Samuel to have been made of less resolute stuff than his father and as having been willing to conform when his parent's influence was no longer felt.</p>
        <p>LOCAL CHAPELS LICENSED UNDER THE TOLERATION ACT.</p>
        <p>The events of the years which follow receive no illumination from any documents we have been able to discover, save that among Lord Kenyon's papers there has sur- vived a list of the " Chappells in this country certified and recorded for meeting places for Dissents," under the Toleration Act of 1689. Five out of six local " chappells "are therein discovered to be barns. This is the list: Peter Aspinall Ecclestons Barn in Warrington Thomas Risley— Richard Jackson's Barn in Culcbeth. Wm fireball—John Haab den's house in Ashton-in-Mgkerfield. John Walker —flm Hasleden's barne in Park Lane in Ashton in-Makerfleld. William Lathwait's barne in Wygan. William A spinall. From this it appears that the ministry of Peter Aspinwall was exercised not in the Court House but in a Barn,and Charles Owen would succeed him there. it would be a happy thing if some document or title deed revealed to us the site of" Eccleston's barn," for the building would appear to have been the modest home of Nonconformity for at least fifteen years. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CHRISTMAS ATTRACTION AT THE ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS ATTRACTION AT THE ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Dance's company of 40 artistes will present the famous musical play " The Chocolate Soldier " at the Royal Court Theatre on Thursday and Friday and Saturday evening of next week, with a matinee on Thursday. The attraction of this fist visit to‘Warrington will undoubtedly be great, and our readers will be well advised to book seats early.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>LETTERS OF A SLENDER SCHOOL CHUM TO HER DUMPY FRIEND.</head>
        <p>LETTERS OF A SLENDER SCHOOL CHUM TO HER DUMPY FRIEND.</p>
        <p>Fi th Letter : —TREATS OF VIE GENEUAL IMPATIENCE OF FAT FOLK.</p>
        <p>My dear Sallie, Can you explain to me why over-fat folk are so testy and worrity and impatient? Only last week I had a visit from two sisters whom I know rather well. They are frightfully fat, and life seems a constant burden to them. They asked me, under the seal of confidence, to tell them if I had "taken anything" to bring down my weight, and of course I explained quite frankly about the Marmola Prescription Tablets, and said that they could be bcught from our own chemist in the village. The sisters were appalled at the idea of buying a flesh reducer in our chemist's shop, and it was as much as I could do to keep from laughing outright as I explained that, if they sent 2/9 to the Marrnola Co., 1238, 86, Clerkenwell iload, London, E.C., they would get the tablets by return post in a perfectly plain package. I explained afterwards that, for my own part, I would quite as soon ask a chemist for Marrnola Tablets as a cure for over-stoutness as I would ask him for syrup of phosphates as a remedy for anemia; but they looked very shocked. To-day they have been in again, and both of them seemed quite annoyed because they have taken eighteen tablets apiece and are not reduced as much in one week as I myself was in three months. Such utter absurdity! Just think what scarecrows they'd be if they had been brought down several stone in weight in one week. lam delighted, dear Sallie, to hear how famously you are reducing. Down five pounds! Isn't it lovely? Accept the congratulations of Your affectionate MARGARET.</p>
        <p>THE INTERNATIONAL WHITAKER (1913). This is the first issue of an " International Edition of Whitaker's Almanack," in cloth covers, price (2s. net.) Its" biography " is contained on page vi.—facing a woodcut of the originator of "Whitaker 's Almanack " audits contents are submitted for criticism. The first issue is already exhausted, and the book is now being reprinted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>SCROOGE IN THE FLESH.</p>
        <p>As the great Christian festival approaches, and all about us begins to look " Christmassy," I think of a certain sour-visaged old townsman of the bygones, whom many habitually avoided because of his grumpiness. Once I ventured to wish him "A Merry Xmas," and the surly fellow growled in response that there would have been "a lot more sense in it if I'd taken a bit of goose! " As he had money, one cherished the hope that some day, at this particular season for preference, he might change like Silas Marner, of Tennyson's "Farmer Allen," or the Scrooge whom in his unregenerate days he most resembled; but somehow it didn't come off. After all such folk are decidedly in the minority. Most people, especially about Christmas time, are ready enough to echo Tiny Tim's "God bless us every one." One need not therefore hesitate about wishing all and sundry the compliments of the season, and I do so very heartily. THE ORIGIN OF CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>A Sovereign born in November may elect to cele- brate the anniversary in May, but in the case of the festival now in question the order has been reserved, and we observe on the 25th December an epoch- making event which must have occurred later in the season. The early Church fathers had good and sufficient reasons for adapting for this propose the ancient feast of Saturn. Like some other pagan customs dating from pre-Christian times, they were brought into the service of the Church and ennobled. SEASONABLE ADORNMENT.</p>
        <p>It was at the same feast of Saturn that the ancients used to decorate their temples with greenery. Christianity found the custom firmly established in popular favour, and not merely acquiesced in but helped to preserve it, Jesus Christ being substituted for the mythical Deity that was formerly the object of adoration. True, good Churchpeople throughout cur own district who now-a-days embellish their places of worship with certain evergreens—mistletoe being a conspicuous exception—are able to cite Isaiah as referring to the use of cedar, fir, box and pine "to beautify the place of my sanctuary" ; all the same the cust,m of decorating with evergreens at Christmas is of heathen origin. The pious need not be shocked by this ; let them rather commend the wisdam of their spiritual ancestors. HOLLY AND THE BIRDS.</p>
        <p>The absence of red berries from the hAly is supp-aed to indicate a mild season, the theory being widely received that nature makes special provision in that respect when there is to be a severe winter, the berries being intended as food f,,r birds that would otherwise starve. But just when we are remarking how wondertltl it all is, one ef those severely practical emeLtific persons Lterposes with the prosaic suggestion that viet weather tromotes an excessive growth of wood in the holly tree, aud so tends-to reduce the yield of ben ies. "THE MESSIAH."</p>
        <p>Han(las masterpiece was performed at the Primitive Methodist Church, I.RO-street, on Sunday, and such efforts are becoming incieasingly popular An authority obssrven not long ago that the business of the average member of an audience is not to make music or to Jurige it, but to tnjoy it; and I for one agree that most people get more pure, ierthetic en j ruent out of an old familiar oratorio that has stood the test of time than out of new works by young corn- posers, many of which are exprimeutal. A pro- fessional musician who has been trying to pierce through the accidents Ind get at the essentials of musica'. appreciation confesses that one c.,n hardly expect the arnauur—and it is the amateur who is the final arbiter—not to prefer that music which he knows by experiei.ce speaks "from the heart to the heart" to that whose heart he has not yet found, or which may prove to Lave none at all. "This new music," he may well say "may be profoundly interest- ing as indicating a stage of progress. a tendency, or a future development, but it does not do that for me which is the ultimate object of all music." Thus he con- tinues to argue that "the old's better" and above all he insists on the annual repetition of the "Messiah." "THE KITCHEN PARADISE."</p>
        <p>More than once I have heen constrained to side with the Rev A M Mitchell against well nigh overwhelming odds; but when he mcunts his vegetarian bobby even I find him impossible. In his magazine article headed as above, he is hopelessly extravagant. I wonder if Mr Mitchell read a while ago how M. Magean, the well-known savant and the friend and collaborator M. Edmond Perrier, bad made a little experiment to prove that growth is much more rapid on a carnivor- ous than on a vegetarian diet. He took a number of duckliogs just hatched, and divided them into four lots. The first lot were fed exclusively on vegetarian principles ; the second on fly larva, the third on fish, and the fourth on butcher's meat. Eventually, M Magnan announced the result of his experiments to the Academy of Science. Every week he weighed the birds, and from the first it was obvious that the meat- eaters were going to win. After two and a half months they had already reached their full height and IV( ighed 3 1-slbs. Next came those which had teen fed on fly larva', weighing 2 bs. Next were the fish-eaters at I.lb. 1310 z., and last of all the "vegetarians," which weighed only Jib. 5 z. In three and a half months the meat-eaters were fully grown and weighed 4 3-slbs , while the other ducks took another two and a hall month) to reach their full growth. The topic is rather a favourite one of mine, but to do anything like justice to my case would require the whole column, and of course that is out of the question. Two very brief quotations must suffice for the present. Dr Allison ia cons baced that the Jewish race has survived through centuries of oppression because it is the best fed race on earth ; but even the Rev A M Mitchell will not contend that they are a vegetarian race. Of course he has a perfect right to his belief, and none will question his sincerity; but he ought not to try to cram his beans and • carrots down my unwilling throat, much less to consign me to perdition because I do not happen to see eve to eya with him here. Years ago the most reliable doctor that I ever had occasion to consult advised me that "as a rule, what you fancy agrees with you." Much more recently, the Right Hon G W E Russell has written in a paper circulating all over these parts ; " Eat the food you like best. The fact that you like it is pretty sure sign that it is good for you" Surely this is commonsense SELF-MADE MEN.</p>
        <p>In his Warrington lecture " Casey " mentioned that Verdi was the son f a baker, and that Dvorak was the son of a butcher. I may add that Defoe, the author of "Robinson Crusoe, ' and Cardinal Wolsey, Henry VIII's famous Prime Minister, were likewise the sons of butchers. lam tempted to enlarge some- what for the encouragement of young fellows who may read this. It has been noted that in all ages and in every land, men have won fame in spite of humble birth and poverty, and in the face rf circums'ances. No handicap is too great for the man who means to win. Here are some further notable instances: Sir Richard Arkwright, inventor of the cotton-spinning frame, was a barber. John Bunyan, of '' The Pilgrim's Progress " was a travelling tinker. Robert Burns, Scotland's lyric poet, was the son of a poor nurseryman, and was himself a small farmer and a revenue officer. Christopher Columbus, discoverer of the New World, was the son of a wool-comber. Ben Jonson was a poor boy, the stepson of a bricklayer. Keats was the son of a hostler. Linntrus, the great Swedish naturalist, was a poor student who mended his shoes with paper and often depended on chance generosity for a meal. Thomas Moore, author of the "Irish Melodies," was the son of a country grocer. Napoleon was a penniless second lieutenant in 1785; in 1801 he was crowned an Emperor. Samuel Richardson, the first famous novelist, was a journeyman printer, the son of a carpenter. Shakespeare was the son of a glover in a little country town ; both his grandfathers were husbandman. George Stephenson, the inventor of the locomotive, was the son of a fireman at a colliery, and began life as his father's helper. Trajan, perhaps the , greatest of all Rome's Emperors, was the son of a c ,mmon soldier, and began hie career in the ranks. James Watt, inventor of the condensing steam-engine, was the son of a small merchant who failed in business. CONCERNING ERRATA. 1 All that has arisen out of Casey's visit to the town ; but there is more—a lot more—enough to fill another column at least. In last week's contribution, I wrote 'that I had not forgotten his capital interpretation of Raff's cavatina, but the "comp ' set it caratina, and, as the little slip escaped the lynx-eyed proof-reader, local musicians are looking somewhat askance at me. I assure them it is a small matter as compared with what the printer can do when he really tries. As a I brother scribe wrote some time ago, the misprints that actually get into the papers are nothing to those which both amuse and appal the sub, when they reach him in proof. What tales one could unfold if we were I telling them out of school 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>THRIFTLESS WORKERS.</head>
        <p>THRIFTLESS WORKERS.</p>
        <p>MAYOR ON LESSONS OF THE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>SAVINGS BANK'S CONTINUED PROSPERITY</p>
        <p>How greatly the Warrington Savings Bank is appre- ciated by the thrifty working classes was again evidenced bythe record of inereased prosperity presented at, the annual meeting of the trustees and managers held at the bank in Buttermarket-street on Tuesday moriing. Incidentally the Mayor availed himself of tire oppor- tunity of recounting one of the lessons of the coal strike, which was that many workers in receipt of good wages, by neglecting to make provision for the" rainy day," found themselves practically destitute after about a week's unemployment. In the absence of the chairman of the bank (Mr Win Lorg), the meeting was presided over by the vice-chairman (Mr C Jackson Holmes). Amongst others present were :—The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph), Alderman Bennett .(auditor). Councillors li Roberts and 1' Birtles, the Revs. W Bracecamp, James Collins, 0 Vale Owen and John Yonge, Messrs G Dakin, W Tinsley, J Longland, C E Parker, W Roberts,J Upson, J W Hay ward,W Fletcher, W Clegg, J Moore Murray, J H Smethurst, J S Hill ,A Down, and W A Proud (actuary). Letters of apology for non-attendance were received from Mr William Long, Mr F A Frost, and Mr L Rowson. Captain T E Withington, of Culcheth wrote resigning his position on the Committee of Management, after having been connected with the institution since 1856. The resignation was accepted with great regret, and apprecirtion of Captain Withington's long services to the bank was placed on record.</p>
        <p>THE YEAR'S PROGRESS. The 95th annual report stated :—Another successful year's business has to be recorded, there being again an increase in accounts and in money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NEW ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>NEW ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>In the ordinary department, 810 accounts have been o_pened or renewed and 651 closed. In the investment oreprtrnent, 113 opened, and 51 closed. The total numbPr of open accounts in both departments is now 17,107,an increase of 218 for theyear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TRANSACTIONS.</head>
        <p>TRANSACTIONS.</p>
        <p>There have leen 33,601 transactions, 22,737 of which were depocits and 10,867 payments. The average amount of each transaction is,deyo its £4 10s 3d,payments £9 58. The total amount received from deoositors, including transfers from the Post Office Savings Bank, £859 3s 8d ; transfers from other Banks, £584 12s 4d, and dividends, &amp;0., is £102,681 17s lid ; the total amount paid. inclusive of transfers to oth•T Savings Banks, £697. 16s Bd, purchases of stock £ll6 lis 3d, is £100,521 8e lid, making a turnover for the sear of £203,203 6s 103. The following is the summary of deposits and payments :—Deposits, Ordinary Depart- ment, £83,025 5s 3d ; Investment Department, £19,656 12s 81; purchases of stock, £lBO 93 lid; interest added, Ordinary Department, £8,281 7s 83 ; interest added, Investment Department, £2,881 10s Ild, total, £114,031 6s 5d ; payments, Ordinary Department, £88,507 3s 7d; payments, Investment Department. £12,014 5s 4d ; sales of stock, £B5B 2s 43, t0ta1,£101,379 lie 3d ; increase for the year, £12651 15s 2d. Sines the lstJanuary, 1909, when the hours for busi- ness were extended to the full locll Bank bows, the amount due to the depositors has be. n ircreased by .£58,539. The assets of the bank at 20th November,lPl2, exclusive of Bank Premises valued at £1,300 were : Ordinary department, £359,692 12s ; investment depart- ment, £105,566 43 10d • stock, £15,764 10a 5d ; total, £481,023 7s 3d ; deduct liabilities,ordinary departments, 1:33,124 3s 23; investment departments, £101,161 Os 4d; stock, £15,761 10s 5d ; total, £473,019 13s lid ; leaving a cash surplus after paying every deppsitor in full of £7973 13s 4d. The surplus assets over liabilities, inclusive of the value of the Bank Premises (valued at £1,300), now stand at £9,273 138 43, an ircrease of £425 Is 10d for the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>BRANCHES.</head>
        <p>BRANCHES.</p>
        <p>The business transacte-cf-a-t--ibe branches again shows that good progress has been made and they are fulfilling their object viz : in extending the facilities for thrift over a much larger area and at the same time acting as feeders to the head office. At Bank Quay where £1,301 has been received for head office accounts. there are 425 open accounts, with £5,991 15s 3d standing to their credit, whilst at Berlestown 362 depositors have the larger sum of £13,287 7s I Id to their credit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TliE SPECIAL INVESTMENT DEPARTMENT</head>
        <p>TliE SPECIAL INVESTMENT DEPARTMENT</p>
        <p>bas again been most successful, the total amount now due to depositors being £101,161 N 4d. This department is proving of great utility and is adding mu3h to the usefulness of the Institution, as it enables the depositors to put in over £5O in the year.</p>
        <p>HOME SAFES Still remain a popular metos of accumulating small sums. There lave keen 576 openings, the total .mount received being £1,274 183 sd, an average of £2 4s 3d per opening.</p>
        <p>TIIE SCHOOL BANICS now number 30 against 22 last year and have standing to their c edit .£1,530 108 7d, an increase of £285 2s 3d for the year. The success of the school banks is a striking tribute to the t fli.dency and ability with which they are conducted and the committee take this opportunity to renew their thanks to all those ladies and gentlemen who have given their services in so good a cause, and to record their high appreciation of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>OBITUARY,</head>
        <p>OBITUARY,</p>
        <p>The c-unmittce have to rec)rd with much regret the loss the Institution Iles sustained by the deaths of Mr Charles White, a truitee and manager for over hilf-a- century, of Mr E J Guest, of Mr Henry Fairclougb, and of Mr D Garnett, managers.</p>
        <p>ONE IN EVERY FOUR THRIFTY. The statement of accounts having been read, the CIIAIRMAN moved its adoption, tie commented on several salient features of the year's business, pointing out that they bad opened in the ordinary department 810 coconuts and closed F5l, a gain on the year of 185. En the special investment department they had opened 113 and closed 51, making an increase in the two departments of 248. The transactions had been 33,601— not quite so many as the year before—but the amount per transaction hccl been larger. The turnover was £203,203. The increase in the funds at the end of the year was £12,651, and the total amount duo to depositors on both acctiunts was £173,019. leaving a cash surplus after paying all depositors in full, including £1,300 at which the bank premises stood in the books, of £9,273. On such a large sum of money as they had deposited with them, that might not seem very large but when they bore in mind that all the money in the ordinary account was deposited with the National Debt Commissioners, end that belonging to the specie, investment department was with Corpora ions having the power to levy rates, and that all the money could be called in et shirt notice, ha trusted they would agree that the margin was amply suflicent and that the bank still remained in a sound financial position (Hear, hear, and applause) The branches at Bank Quay and Earlestown were doing better. At the former they had 425 accounts as against 379, with an increase in deposits of £757. Earlestown had a larger emount to its credit, viz, £13287, being an incr ass of £906. The special investment department had increased by £10,526 183 3d. This form of investment had now practically superceded the old system of purchasing Consols. The public seemed to appreoiate the fact that they had a safe three per cent investment free from any thictua, ion In price. (Hear, hear.) Ile was glad to say that there still was</p>
        <p>AN INCREASING DEMAND FOR HOME SAFES; 462 were issued to depositors, showing an increase in cash takings of £1.76. Thanks to the headmasters and mistresses, there was an increase in the amount credited to School Banks, and the cammittee ere pleased to see that form of thrift. It might be worth while for the Committee of Management to consider whether some portion of the surplus might not be devoted to the establishment of other branches. He thought tte more facilities the people bad for saving, the greater would be the amount put by. The surplus bad doubled itself in the last 20 years, and the amount due to dopruiitqrs had increased by £1.61,000. They could not induce everyone to save for bald times and rainy days, but it would be seen that they were doing very well when he told them that their depositors worked out at</p>
        <p>ONE IN EVERY FOUR OF TIIE PSPULATION. (Applause). Mr GEORGE DARIN seconded. Councillor ROBERTS said that unquestionsbly the position of the bank was extremely satisfactory from every point of view, and it was filling a very big place in the social economies of the town. They all believed that under the able chairmanship of Mr Long and Mr Jackson Holmes its prosperity would continue to increase. He congratulated Mr Holmes upon occupy- ing the position of chairman for the first time. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The motion was carried. THRIF MESS WORKERS.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND COAL STRIKE DESTITUTION. The MAYOR, in moving " That the report of the Committee of Management he approved, and that the meeting acknowledge the valuable services rendered to the institution by the gentlemen acting on the com- mittee during the past year," said they were gentlemen of undoubted business tact, ability, and integrity. Therefore, they had the confidence of all present, and of the town generally. (Applause.) He was pleased to find that the institution was being taken advantage of to the extent which the Chairman named in his able address. Councillor Roberts had said the institution was filling a big place in the social economies of the town ; but he (the Mayor) thought he must have come across the cases of exceptions to thrift daring the coal strike. His experi- ence, and Mr Murray would bear him out, did not show that thrift was exercised, as many people in the receipt of good wages seemed to get rid of them immediately and did not prepare for a rainy day. They were, in I fact, practically destitute after having been out of work for a week or ten days. He hoped these people would be induced to mend their ways in that respect. and if the bank did nothing but reclaim a large number of them, it would be performing a useful service to the I community. (Hear, hear, and applause).</p>
        <p>The Rev H V PIGOT seconded and alluded to the death of Dr Chas White, who, he said, was a member of the committee. They were IB deeply attached to him and that meeting afforded an opportunity of expressing appreciation of his services. (Hear, hear). The CHAIRMAN said it was his intention to tell the meeting that during the coal strike the business at the bank was quite normal. They had not quite so many transactions, but the amounts paid in were larger. The resolution was carried. CHAIRMAN AND VICE-CHAIRMAN. Mr PARKER proposed the re-election of Mr W Long as chairman, and of Mr C Jackson Holmes ss vice- chairman. They all knew what a great interest Mr Long had long taken in the bank and in everything connPcted with the welfare of the town and district. They regretted that advancing years prevented Mr Long taking that active part in the business of the bank to which he had been accustomed, but he was sure it was the desire of all of them that he should retain his present position as long as he opts able. (Hear, hear.) They were fortunate, however, in having such an excel- lent vice-chairman as Mr Jackson Holmes, who was an able and energetic gentleman and was deeply inter- ested in the affairs of the bank. (Applause.) Councillor BIRTLES seconded, and this proposition was also carried.</p>
        <p>TREASURER AND AUDITOR. On tho motion of Mr LONGLAND, seconded by Bfr SMETHURST, Mr Wm Skinner was re-appointed treasurer and Alderman Bennett auditor. Alderman Bittcsrrr said he was proud of the position of auditor of the bank, which he had held for nearly 20 ' sears. During the whole of that time the bank had been steadily progressing and fulfilling an increasingly useful purpose in the town He hoped that progressive spirit would continue, and that by the time they had attained their centenary as a bank, which would be in about five years, they would top the half million in investments, and possibly be housed in a still more suitable building. He had heard it whispered that such a move was dimly and vaguely contemplated, and if the bank authorities wanted to get into the n.agio circle at Market Gate the Corporation would be pleased to supply an admirable site. (Laughter, and applause). This motion was also agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE COMMITTEE OF MANAGEMENT. Councillor ROBICRTS moved that the following managers be elected to join the trustees on the Com- mittee of Manag.ment for the ensuing year, viz : Mr T Fairclough, Mr J S Hill, Mr L H Howson, Mr J H Emethurst, and the Rev J Yonge. He said that public work attracted certain individuals and repelled others, but instituticns of that kind would come very badly off indeed if it were not for the services of such gentlemen as served on the committee of management. Their only reward was in the fact that they were conscious of doing good service to the interests of their fellow men.</p>
        <p>Mr TINSLEY seconded and the motion was agreed to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>VO `ES_ OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VO `ES_ OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr FLETCHER. seconded by Mr ROBERTS, a vote of thanks was paned to the staff, and Mr PROUD in replying said they highly appreciated that mark of confidence. _</p>
        <p>The Rev G VALE OWEN moved a vote of thanks to Mr Jackson Holmes for presiding over the meeting. He found that the managers numbered nearly 100, and yet the attendance that morning was comparatively small. He hoped some of them would follow the example set by the trustees and attend to their duties more faithfully. Mr FLETCHER seconded and the motion was carried. Mr flout ES replied,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>A TALK ABOUT THE CHRISTMAS TREE.</p>
        <p>I suppose most people think that the Christmas Tree is a very old English institu- tion, established long ago. If they do they are mistaken, for it is one of the things that was brought over from Germany by Prince Albert and the beginning of the fashion was set in 1840 when he and Queen Victoria had one at the Palace. In Germany everybody has a Christmas tree no matter how poor or lonely they may be, and they put round it quite an air of romance. They do not hang presents on it, they take a piece of natural tree and just make it resplendant with lights, tinsel, golden fruit and shimmering silver. It is kept care- fully hidden until Christmas Eve and then the curtains are drawn aside and it is shown in all its splendour to the admiring household.</p>
        <p>The German market places are like little forests before Christmas, and people flock to buy the little trees and carry them home in triumph. They have them in the Churches too, and I am told that the German churches in London have each two wonderfully decorated trees for Christmas time.</p>
        <p>If you should ask them how the custom began they will:tell you that Martin Luther, after wandering one Christmas Eve under the clear winter sky lit by thousands of stars, set up a tree wth lots of candles to represent the starry sky. This, however, is only legend for there is no mention of a Christmas tree in Germany until about 1605. The custom only spread in the 18th century and it was not common until about 50 years ago. It was a Protestant custom rather than Catholic and was adopted very slowly. But where did the idea of the Christmas Tree come from ?</p>
        <p>Curiously enough as we get back to very remote days we find that it was not a Christian practice at all. Among the Romans there was a custom of decorating their homes with evergreens at the January Kalends. The early Christian teachers pro- tested against this, and rertullian said 'Let the heathen kindle lamps, they who have no light ; let them fix on the doorposts laurels which shall afterwards be burnt, they for whom fire is close at hand; meet for them are testimonies of darkness and auguries of punishment. But thou art a light of the world and a tree that is ever green ; if thou hast renounced temples, make not a temple of thy own house-door."</p>
        <p>This shows that in the early centuries of Christianity, New Year decorations were considered wrong and perhaps idolatrous. Whether our custom has descended from the Roman one or whether it has sprung up in some other way nobody seems to know. MARY'S LITTLE CHILD. FRANCES BENT DILLiNGF/A3l. Down the still streets of Bethlehem The Christmas Child went oft, And as He went His child-like voice Hummed a tune sweet and soft ; When all the kindly neighbours heard, They listened while they smiled : "How t&lt;,nder is 11,13 gentle tune— 'Tis Mary's little child." And where the road was steep and rough The Christmas Child oft went, That be might help some weary man With wounds or hunger spent ; And when the neighbours saw Him thus, They always paused and smiled "'How kind a heart the young lad has— 'Tis Mary's little child."</p>
        <p>And where the children quarrelled loud The Christmas Child came too : He made the peace by some low wcrd, Though how none really knew: But all the neighbours noticed this, And all the mothers smiled ; " If He has come, the trouble's o'er</p>
        <p>'Tis Mary's little child."— " He walks no more through Bethlehem—. The Christmas Child—to-day; Through myriad worlds, amid the stars, He takes His mighty way. He hears the tender voice of song That swells from earth the while; The lame are healed and peace proclaimed Through Mary's little child.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>U</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>; fame proclaimed ..‘de</p>
      </div>
