      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>COMMON-SENSE RULE</head>
        <p>A COMMON-SENSE RULE</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 21, 1912</p>
        <p>THE WELSH BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clement Edwards, M.P., Voices the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>National Demand.</head>
        <p>National Demand.</p>
        <p>"A GENEROU</p>
        <p>By an overwhelming majority—the dissentients numbering not more than six— a rnms meeting held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Saturday evening, under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association, passed a resolution welcoming the Government's measure for the Disestablishment and Disendowrnent of the Church in Wales. There was a very large attendance, the hall being filled, and the enthusiasm testified to the strength of rrogressive opinion in the town in favour of one of the most important measures which the Government Iris in hand. The ptincipal speaker was Mr Clement Edwards,ll.P., oce of the phalanx of Welsh stalwart- who have been returned to voice the demand fur Disestablishment, and he dealt with the question in a forceful, temperate and convincing manner. Mr C E Parker (president of the Liberal Association) presided and was supported on the platf )Trn by Mr Clement Edwards, M.P., Councillors H Roberts and J Shaw, Rev W Cooper,Mrs Osborne (secretary of the Women's Liberal Association), Captain II G Roberts, Mr E Wynne jones, Mr J G Withinshaw, Mr P Delaney and Mr E A Sanders. LETTER FROM MR. A. H. CIIO3FIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr A II Crosfield sent the followin"g letter which- was read by the chairman :- 39, Hyde Park Gate, S.W. Decemler 13th, 1912. My Pear Parker, I am right glad to know that you are to-morrow presiding over the eirnonstration that is to be held in favour of Welsh Disestablishment, aud that our chief guest of tho evening in Warrington will be so excellent arid powerful an advocate of this cause as Mr. Clement Edwards. ith so strong a platfmrn I am very sure that the meeting will be decisive and determined in support of so good a c .use. After all this cause of religious Equatty is not only one of the ldest, but still to my mind iernains one of the very noblest articles in the Liberal c'eed, forming as it does a large and important part of the ideal of equal citizenship. And if with enormous majorities extending over many years the case in favour of Disestab- lishment in \Vales is overwhelming, that necessarily carries with it the case for Disendow- ment; The Established Church in Wales holds a large part of its Endoaments because, and only because, it is Established. When it is D.sestab- lished it follows therefore that that part of its Endowments should cease. At the same time I submit that two conditions should be observed : (1) Only these Endowments which the Chu-ch holds by virtue of its being Established must be , relinquished ; (2) The determination as to which these are must err, if at all, on the side of generosity. In the case of the Bill now before Parliament both these conditions are c3-npletely satisficd,and this Bill therefore has my hearty support With all good wishes for a successful meeting, I remain faithfully yours, A. H. CROSFIELD. Apologies for non.attendance were also received from Mr Ziba Armitage and Mr J Jarman.</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN'S ILLUSTRATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>ENDOWMENTS ENHANCED BY COMMUNAL ACTIVI I lES.</head>
        <p>ENDOWMENTS ENHANCED BY COMMUNAL ACTIVI I lES.</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRMAN observed that a meeting in favour of, the Welsh Church Bill was almost over-due in Wit-ring- tan. (Hear, hear.) They had heard a great deal on the other side and the people needed educating on the principles upcn which the Bill was based. As an illustration on the question of endowments Mr Parker quoted from a deed of endowment, by which an ancestor of his—one Hobert Parker who lived about 200 years ego—erected a hospital for "the cc nvenient reception of ten poor widows," with en oratory for divine worship, and endowed it by giving a piece of land. Mr Parker pointed out that, as compared with the date whea the endowment was given, the income from that land had increased at least ten fold and the question which suggested itself to his mind was : Who had produced that increase ? It was very evident that the raid Robert Parker could not have done so, because he had been in his grave for the last 200 years, and he was certain that none of his thscendants bad done anything to enhance the value of the land, because they all migrated from the locality in which it was situated. Undoubtedly the increase was w hat, for want of a better name, we called unearned increment—that was an increase which had been socially created, and the ten-fold income which now accrued to that trust was the outcome of the work of the people who lived or worked upon the land. (App!ause.) Ile thought that had some bearing upon the general question of endowraents, and it would be interesting to find cut what was the original value of tbe endowments of the Welsh Church which were, in the main or almost entirely, given in the form of land. We had rkached the point when all parties in England agreed that the increased value cf land— particularly in the neighbourhcod of growing towrs— was a legitimate item for special taxation, for it had been created by social activities and not ty the effort of the owner. lherefore, when they considered the large revenue now produced by the gifts of piens ancestors, they had a right to ask what was the oii;inal latue of the endowments. (Applause). MR. EDWARDS EXPLAINS TIIE BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr Clan' ENT. EnwAnDs,M.P., who had a great recep- tion, began his sin ech with a reference to the great stand sNhich the Chairman had made, in his particular indu try, for the cause of Free Trade—a stand which bad had an effect throughout tho United Kingdom. (Applause.) lie (Mr Edwards) was also glad to be in the constituency for which his friend Mr Crosfleld— (applause)--would be returned as member at .the next election. (Applau-A) ) After a reference to the Tory conspiracy against trade unionism (which will be found reported i a another column), Mr Eiwards said the Welsh Church Bill had been denounced in very strong terms by their oppnents, who had accused them of all sorts of wickedness, from robbery to spolia- tion. T..ey h.nl bad the war-like Bi:h:p of St. litvid's going through Eng/and,and the more diplomatic Bishop of St. Asaph busily occupied in the columns of the " Manchester Guardian and elsewin re. There bad also been sent up to the House of Commons a large number of petitions against the Bill First be wanted to make it perfectly clear that under our democratic system of Ciovernmeat,</p>
        <p>TM: BIIMAT TRIBUNAL which decided v.hat should be done politically was the ballot box—(applause)--end so sacred did we regard the use of the ballot box that w especially selected the people who were to exercise the right of voting. Wales had had the ballot box in eleven general elections and at all of them, whattver had teen the differences of opinion on other questions, Wales had been strong and persistent in returning an overwhelming majority of its representatives in favour of Disestablishment. (Applause.) Last time thirt3 -one representatives were so returned and, speaking as one of them, he said that Disestablishment was a definite and specific issue, the electors decided, end all the petitions in the world wonla not cause him to swerve one half-inch from his duty to carry opt he undertakings and the representa- tions that were mad nat the last election. (Applause.) But that was assuming—he puE it as gtrongly as that —that the petitions were genuine petitions, signed by every person whose same appeared on them, and that those persons were all adults. But what did they find ? These</p>
        <p>CLERICAL OPTIMISTS IN STATISTICS evidently thought they would be able to seud up great loads of petitions which would be consigned to the cellars of the House of Commons, and that they would then_be able to say that the petitions cont ained so many thousands of signatures and their word would be accepted, " Eut unfortunately " commented Mr Edwards" those of us who brave been brought up in the atmosphere of the Welsh hills are a little bit suspicious of the clerical mind and, however high may be their authority on matters of theology and piety, we have found, on more than one occasion, that they have gone a little astray when it has been a question of dealing with figures and statistics. Speaking for my own constituermy, I have taken the trouble to go through a gnat numbor of the petitions and this is the sort of thing I find. I come too page and there is a great that of variety in the writing, hut on coming to another page I find that every perAon whose name appears there has written in the sums nice, round handwriting. (Laughter.) I go to places where the name Jones is fairly common. (Laughter.) I suppose the, think it is not very likely that, if a few: additional Jone:es are put on the petition, there will be much chance of detection. (Laughter.) But I find in one case that there are more Jonezes put on the petition than have yet actually arrived. (Lftighl•er.) I find also that certain youthful Joneses—in one ease „t, quits a year old and in another case, not quite two years old—have shown marvellous precociousness— (laughter)—tecat se their signatures appear in the most matured handwriting—(laughter)—you would have thought that they had lived at least tinny 3 cam,' (Laughter.) Mr Ed ocards went on to state that peti- tions in Llandrindod—a watering plate—had been signed by Welsh visitors who had previously signed at hlme and whose names were thus counted twice over. Further, although it was supposed to be a petition of the Welsh people, the petition at Llandrindod contained the names of many Englikb and Scottish visitors. He contended that such a petition was</p>
        <p>--</p>
        <p>Iyour child is fretful, it is most likely that his stomach is out of order. Remember that Steedman's Powders are unrivalled for regulating the bowels. This medicine contains</p>
        <p>no poison or narcotic,</p>
        <p>their needs. What the Government proposed doing with the tithe was to restore a large part of it to its original use, as laid down in that Act. (Applause.) They also proposed that those lands which were left prior to 1662 —the old gieles—should be devoted to public uses. It was said with scorn in the House of Commons the previous day by Mr Alfred L)ttleton that the Bill was NOT GOING TO BENEFIT THE WELSH NONCONFORMISTS by a single penny. Of course, it was not. (Applause.) They were not out for the mercenary advantage of Nonconformity, but] for religious equality. (Applause.) They reposed to devote the monies to purely public uses. They i 1 not want as Nonconformists to be endowed—all they asked was that =nits which were given for public purposes for the benefit of the whole community, should still be used for the benefit of the community, as intended. It was aid that the Church had established a prescriptive right to the property—that it had been left to it for so long that they ought not to take it away now. There was a good old axiom of the English law which said : " Once a trust, al ways a trust," and, the trustee could never, by prtsoriptive right, acquire the beneficial ownership of the trust funds which he was pot there to administer. (Appleuae.) " Wales" continued Mr Edwards `• through fair weather and through foul, has been true to the traditions of Liberalism. Even you in Lancashire, who boast of your sturdy adherence to con- viction and principle, have wavered in elections, on one side and another, but we in Wales, through the genera- tions,have remained true to Liberalism and have kept the flag flying. Throe generations of our leaders have gone to their graves, after fighting valiantly for this great, cause, still with their hearts fixed upon the anticipa- tion that this measure wou'd come to pass. You ratty take it that Wales is asking for this, not as a nati in that is indifferent to religion or silent on the groat problems of the spiiit and the soul. But Wales is asking for this with a great live religious soul. She ' asks that ter spiritual life shall be tel according to her national characteristics —that there shall be no artificial barrier placed 1 etween one denomination and the other, hit tht tl e whole of her people shall, with. out interference from outside, worship their God in their own way. She asks for religious sq tality." (Applause.) . .</p>
        <p>EDWARDS then proposed the rollover); resolu- tion :—" That this meeting heartily welcoves the Bill for the Disestablishrnent, and Disend iwment of the Church of England in Wdles, giving effect to the long- expressed desire of the overwhelming majority of the Welsh nilion, and pledgeo itself to do all in its power to ensure this measure of justice bEing placed upon the Statute Book." "KING CARSON'S"</p>
        <p>Mr E WYNNE JosEs in seconding, said it might appear surprising that they should have such a splendid meet- ing, hecause</p>
        <p>KING CARSON</p>
        <p>—(langhter)—had been to Warrington, and he under- stood that evertbody hal sworn allegiance to him. (Laughter.) What the Tories did not accomplish on the occasion of King Carson's visit they were reported to have accomplished at the hot pot on the following Monday. (Laugiter.) He (the speaker) heard two men talking in tee street and one said " I had a proper beano the other night—four plates of hot pot and seven gills for a bob." (Laughter.) Evidently they had thoroughly enjoyed themselves at the hot-pot. (Laugh- ter ) He (the speaker) had known Warrington for rrany years, but no statesmin of the rank of Sir Edward Ca•sm had, within his recollection, ever visited the town and left so little impress, or influence upon it as Sir Eiward did. (Hear, hear.) Instead of having been damaged by the demonstrations Liberalism in Warrington was stronger. (Applause.) Mr Crostleld was alive—(applause)—and although Mr Harold Smith got his audiences to repeat "Ne will not have Home Rule," they would see that when the next tight came Mr Harold Smith got home rule and was sent to his mother at Birkenhead. (Laughter end applause.) Dealing with the Disestblishment question, Mr Jones said they had no reason to quarrel with the Established Church. They recognized the good it was doing and the failure of that Church would be their failure as well. No section of the Christian Church could fail without every other section suffering and the success of the Established Church would be the success of the other churches. No one could dispute the fact that the Church in Wales was not an oil-shoot or a missionary development, in any sense of the word, of the see of Canterbury. By a series of events, after they had conquered the people of Wales, they incorporated the Church of Wales into the see of Canterbury. By political means they succeeded in doing with the Welsh Church what England had never done with the Welsh people—they had denationalized it. Defending the use of the term " Free Churches," the speaker said he considered that be was a Church- man, as much as any Anglican. (Applause.) Welsh Nonconformity was not the product of Puritanism but the result of a revolt which took place inside the Anglican Church—a revolt against the wirkingt of the Church being governed by polEical means, when she was a spiritual organization. Some of the best spirits of Wales took part in that revolt. Their opponents on the Disendowment question said they would not yield even if they were alto vol to retain 19s ltd. in the R. In Wales in was not a matter of pounds shillings and pence tut solely one of religious equality.(Applause).By whatever test they were judged, the rree Churches in Wales were far ahead of the Anglican Church. From a sense of relf-respect the Anglican Church ought cot to be prepared to remain in her present position. Cuild it be right that the House of Commons, consisting of representatives of all faiths and of urn', should he the ultimate Court of Appeal in everything that affected tv2e government and control of the Established Ch arch ? It was high time that the chain was broken and Church and State separated. If it was only for the way in which the Welsh Liberals had supported the cause of progress in the British Parliament, he maintained that it was the duty of Btitish Liberals to stand by the people of Wales on this question. (Applause).</p>
        <p>QUESI lONS ANSWERED. Questions having been invited, Mr E iwards w:s asked : If the endowments given before 1662 belong to the Roman Catholics, why not give them back to the Roman Catholics instead of giving them towards museums,dtc.? Mr EDWARDS said the qt.rstion was Lased upon two or three assumptions. It was the Anglicans who said that the mon,y given prior to 1662 was given by the pious ancestor to the Church as a denomination. The reply was that if that were so, it must have been given to the Roman Catholics, when the Established Church was Roman Catholic. In those days, however, the Roman Catholic Church Was the religions exression of the whole community and the endowments therefore were not given to it as a specific denomination but as trustees, representing the whole community. In the same way that Henry V II Idisestablished and disendowed the Roman Catholics Pnd established and endowed the Anglican Church, PArliament to-day could disestablish and,disendow the Anglican Church in Wales and devote the ancient endowments to purely public uses. The assumption that the endowments were to go to museums was not quite true. The money—which was ear-matked only for one rntis um, the Welsh National Museum—had nothing to do with those ancient endowments but WAS part of the proceeds of the Royal Pounty Fund. Ancther question was : Why did so few Liberal candidates at the last election mention Welsh Dis- establishment in their addre-ses ? Mr. EDWARDS : My reply to that must be three-- fold. (1) There was no mistake that the Liberal party were pledged to it by the declaration of Mr Asquith ; (2) Those things that go without sealing never need be said • (3) I am not my brother's keeper. A VOICE : Why should a Welsh Bill be made the mandate for an English election ? Mr EDWARDS : I don't know that it should, any more than the Crofters' Act should be made a mandate. We have a much greater mandate for Welsh Dia- establishment than the Tory Government in 1902 had for their Elucation Act. (Applause). On a vote being taken, the Chairman said six hands were held up against the resolution which he declared carried by an overwhelming majority.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND RICH CHURCHMEN.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr Clement Edwards and the Chairman, said he was very much obliged to Mr Edwards for Bolton.(Applause.) A great many factors helped tobring about that extremely satisfactory result, but he was one of those people who believed that Mr Edwards, by his presence during the last few days of tisat election, and by the extremely important manifesto which he was asked to issue on behalf of the trade unionists, had a great deal to do with that victory. (Applause.) Ife (Councillor Roberts) was, above everything else, a Free Trader, believing that the maintenance of the Free Trade policy of this country was the most important factor in living politics to-day. ()sing to circumstances which were easily explained—to the red herrings in connection with the Insurance Act, and to the abominable and gross misrepresentations of that measure by which allegiance had for a time been drawn away—the govern: ment bad feet a few seats. There was cm old saying lVhorn the gods wish to destroy they first make mid and, after!' somewhat long life, actively associated with electioneering, he did not think he had eat( r known the Tory Fifty to be in such a hopeless rness as it. was to-day. (Applause.) Lancashire, whatever it might be in other respects, was unquestionably a Free Trade county. North-Wrst Manchester went Tory at the by-electi Al not because it had gone back upon , Free Trade, but because a large number of Conservative Free Traders mere foolish enough to believe the promise winch had been made to them that the Tory party would not put tariff reform into operation until a referendum had been taken. 'the Tories talked about having a referendnm in Wales but referendnms gene-ally were not particularly popular just now with the Tory party. (Laughter.) The promise of the refeienduni, which eared the consciences arid solaced the minds of Conservative Free Traders, hid been withdrawn. There was some honesty left among Tory politician., even now, and already they found important newspapers like the " Liverpool Courier " beseeching the Tory party to again promise a referendum. Why ? Ihc fuse the Intel igence of Lancashire had become active again in regard to the importance of the almighty fiscal question. Defiling with the "re-adjustment" of food taxes praposed by the tatiQ reformers, Coln- cillor Roberts sa;d the country would have to pay £18,000,000 extra for corn in order to get back £B,ooo,ooo—which they were told would be produced by the taxation of foreign wheat—towards a debt of £lo,soo,ooo—the present coqt of the breakfast table.</p>
        <p>They heard a great deal in these days about</p>
        <p>TORY DEMOCRACY.</p>
        <p>It was a contr-a-di-c-tie-M-;;T;;;;Lthe two things were absolutely opposed. Democracy, if it meant any- thing stall, meant the expression in legislation of the will of the people. Ile asked any man ho professed to be a Tory democrat how he reconciled his opposition to the Disestablishment and Disendowment of the Welsh Church with the recognition of the principle of democracy. The will of the people in Wales, as expressed at so many general elections, was expressed at the last election when 31 out of 34 members were returned in support of Welsh Disestablishment. Tbe spirit which refused to recognise the demand of nationalization in Ireland in the form of Home Rule— (applause)—was exactly the same spirit which refused that measure of justice to little Wales. Many accusations were made against Nonconformists of being spoliators and sacriligeous people, hut there was</p>
        <p>NO HEAL FIGHT -BEHIND IT ALL. He was old enough to remember the fight over the Disestabliflement and the risendovrment of the Irish Church. That was a fight—there was no mistake about that. They knew where a man was—he was either on one side or the other—and it was a fight to the death. The principles of righteousness prevailed,and what had been the result ? The Irish Church, speaking of it with every possible respect, ass spiritual influence in Ireland was stronger to-day than ever it had been in its history (Applause). Why? Bectuse in its spiritual administra- tions it was not associated in the minds of the people with that which was unfair and which gave a preference to one section of people over another. The same thing would happen in Wales. flaying referred to the dis- abilities under which Nonconfo-rnists had suffered in England until comparatively recent times, Councillor It9berts s,tid he repelled with all the strength of his nature the charge now 1319(10 against them that they were seeking to advantage thernielves at the cost of the Church. It was fulse—there was not an atom of truth in it. Further, to say that they wished to rob the Church of the money specially left to it by members of its own denorninetkn was also false. Every penny of money, every square yard of laud that had been given to the Ps!ablished Church by individual donors, as members of that Church. would be strictly preserved for the interests of the Church. But they said that the money vf hi f h the State itself, in any shape or form, bad given to the Churc,, as the Church of the nation belong&gt; d to the naticn and should be applied for the pure( ses of the I ation. (Applaute.) As to the quest'ou of E Aablishrneut, he contended that the Non- conformist bad as much right to ask for his religious faith to become the Established faith ef the country as the member of any other Church lad for his. But that Was not their programme. They said that religious metters should he detached tram secular and State matters—that they were questions for the conscience of the individual and that the State must take its hands oil. (Applause.) The huge majority of the Welsh people unquestionably did not belong to the Fstabliahed Church and as they had, out of their own poverty, done for themselves, in religicus and educational matters, what had marked them out as a great people, why could not the Church in Wales do the same thing ? Why could not their</p>
        <p>RICHER BRETHREN help them at this stage? We were having a boom in trade just now—at leas: we were told so by the "Daily Mail"— (laughter) —and they ought to accept it as a fact, when a tsrifT reform pap.r told us we were doing so well that we cculd hardly be doing better. (Applause ) The Church in Wales was going to be dis• established and d.sendowed by this Parliament— (applause)—and that disendowrnent, in his opinion, would he fair and righteous. Here then was an oppor- tunity for those rich members of the Church of England —and there were a flue lot of them—to put their hands into their pockets and make good to the Welsh Church what the State was taking from her, and what belonged to the State for general State purposes. (Applause). Mr EDWARDS, in acknowledging the vote, said the cause of Liberalism never looked healthier than at the present time. (Applause.) There bad been many Jererniabs about prophesying all sorts of evil and talk— ing about Liberalism finishing its mission and being replaced by some other sort of organisation, but Liberalism was never more vital than at the present moment. (Applause.) The Natitnal fun-at:me Act had changed the face of politics. The State had assumed the responsibility of looking after the weak, the hilt, the maimed and the blind. In the development of that great policy, uninvited by the spirit of humanity. Liberalism was going on, as the greatest power and the greatest weapon that had ever been fashioned for the purposes of the democracy. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>S MEASURE."</head>
        <p>S MEASURE."</p>
        <p>NOT WORTH THE PAPER IT WAS WRITTEN ON, and they would ignore it. (Applause.) What was the Welsh Church Bill, and why did they ask for it ? It was a Bill which sought to sever the connection between the State and a religious denomination and to utilise, for the benefit of the community, the tithes and endowments which had been given to the Church as trustees for the community. It was not the proper function of the State to teach ally particular religion. (Heir, hear.) The State might do a great many things. Personally, he did not take a r arrow view of what might be the proper functions of the State or a limited view of the sphere of the Government, but he maintaited that a man's faith and l'elief was a matter as between himself tnci his God, and there was no roam for the interven- tion of a Government at all. (Applause.) It was unwire, unjust atd impolitic that the whole community should be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>CMIPULSORILY TAXED</head>
        <p>CMIPULSORILY TAXED</p>
        <p>for the propogailo-n—of .11]-13 -d(;C-tiTres of a single denomination. It was destructive of the best spirit among a people that they should place one c'enomina- tion in a p( sition of privilege and superiority,as against all the other denominations. Those were the hr( ad general grounds, but one went fan tiler with regard to Araks. The Anglican Church in \Vales was not the Church of the people of %Vale. The vast majot ity of the Welah people were Nonconformists and the Welsh were a poor people. Quite true they had two wealthy counties, but, speaking generally and comprehen-ively, it was true to say that Wales was a country of exceedingly poor people, a country of small pert,auts and poor workers who, out of their own frugal earnings, provided for the upkeep of the minister and the chapel of their own faith. Applause.) They reiented having to pay tithes for the upkeep of a Church whin) was not their Church. Th 3 Augliosn Church in Wales was unquestionably</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH OF THE WELL TO-DO, where it, had communic,nts in any number. It was suggested that they were jealous of the Church and desiied to injure it. They had no quarrel with the Church as a spiritual organization, and he went further than that and said that when the Anglioan Curein in Wales bad been disestablished, when it had ceased to be the theological garrison in Wales and had to rest upon the broad sympathies of the people, it would Leconte a great, strong, healthy spiritual organization, MOTO powerful, because enthroned in the hearts of the people, than ever it tad been before, and more powerful than ever it could possibly be so long as it occupied a position of privilege and independence of the local views of the people who should be its communicants. (Applause.) It had been said by many people : We don't object so much to Disestablishinent as we do to Diseadowmeot." How could they _have Disestablish- ment without Disendowment ? People talked about the Caurch being disestablished but nJt disendo wed. If thy were aill to have the pawer to collect tithes every year, how could they be ditest sblished. They collected tithes to-day in virtue aad by force of the law of the land. How could the Church be dkostablished if they :AN continued to have thst power of collecting com- pulsorily which they had hitherto enj qed as a constitu- ent part of the State ? It was one of those platnibili- ties used by the:r oppcnents on the platform, but the moment they looked at it the thing became an impossibility. There was</p>
        <p>NO k UCH THING AS DISESTABLISHAIENT WITHOUT</p>
        <p>DISENDOWMENT.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) The lite Lord 13 aconsfield saw that clearly enough when he made his celebrated declaration in 1867. He said : "It would be a ridiculous anomaly if you continued to a disestablished denomination the power of taking those endowments and those tithes which they had enjoyed in virtue of being the Estab- lished Church of the country." It was said that the present Bill dealt ungenerously with the Church. Let them examine it for a moment. First let them get it quite clearly in their minds that the Church had claimed to be the National Church and had been Established. Either the property which she administered was private property or it was national property. The parish church was hell by the vicar and churchwardens. Was it their absolute property ? Would anyone suggest that it was? Could tho vicar an 1 the churchwardens sell the church? No. Could they keep anybody out of the church? The Parish Church ,vds the church of the parishioners and the vicar or rector ani church- wardens held it as trustees, not for the living com- municants who went to that church, but as trustees for the whole of the parisiconers. Tithes had been collected, since the passing of the Act in tha time of Richard 11., by compulsion of law and administered by the vicar or incumbent. D:d those tithes constitute the personal property of the vicar ? Could the vicar mortgage his tithes or sell them? Of 0 aurae not. The tithes bad been collected and administered by the vicar cnd churchwardens as trustees for tbe whole of the parish. The Charch said that those endowments that were left to toe church before 1662—tw0 years after Charles 11. came back to the throne—were left to the Church as such. They were all pretty familiar with the history of the Church up to the time of Henry Vlll.Clearly, for several centuries at. all events, the Church was Roman Catholic, and it was not until Henry VIII. disestablished and dis- endowed the Roil:aunts that it became the Anglican Church as we broadly understood ,it. So that if people were to stick to endowments according to</p>
        <p>TRH INTENTION OF TFIS PIOUS ANCESTORS. it was perfectly clear that when the pious ancestor was a Roman Catholic and left his donation to the local church, which was Roman Catholic, it could not belong to the ProtestmA Anglican Church but must belong to the Roman Catholics. (Applause). The Bill proposed to hand over to the living communicants of the Church, as their sole property, unshackled and uncontrolled by anybody outside,the four great Cathedrals of Wales, although they had been built very largely out of public money. It proposed to surrender the right of every parishioner who was not a living communicant, in the parish church and the local religious residencs and to hand those buildings over, without any compensation, to the living communicints of the parish church as their absolute and unfettered property, It proposed to leave to them every endowment since IGO, thus treat- ing the endowments that had been left from the time that there were other &amp;nominations a; being left to the Church as a denomination, insteid of to the Church as trustee for the whole community. It proposed to leave to the Church the whole of the endow. ents, however far back they dated, which were given for the express purpose of maintaining the fabric of the church and the religious residence. It proposed to leave as the absolute property of the Church the whole of the parish furniture. A fact which was often overlooked was that not a single persin now living in Wales who bad a vested interest in the property of the Church would suffer one farthing's loss so long as be lived and served in the Church in Wales. (Applause.) And yet that was called robbery and spoliation. (Laughter.) No, it was a</p>
        <p>MEASURE OF TIIE LARGEST POSSIBLE GENEROSITY to the Anglican community in Wales. The Dill pro- posed to take out of the hands of the Church the tithes and those endowments given—not for the upkeep of the fabric—before 1662. The reason for that was that up to 1662 there was only one recognized religious community—in other words, up to 1662 there were no recognised Nonconformists or Dissenters—no recognised religious community outside the four corners of the Established Church. The Established Church in these days was the religious expression of the whole community. That being so, by what possible right could the small remnant of the whole community who were now the living communicants of the Church say that they were to have the whole of the property, the shares even of those who bad formed other denominations ? The Nonconformists said that during the time the whole commpnity were members of the Cnurch, the money given for It use must ha taken as given for the benefit of the whole community, and that the money held by the Vicar and churchwardens was held by them as trustees for the community. What they proposed to do was to substitute a real trustee— for the Vicar and churchwardens and to devote the monies for the benefit of the whole community, as they were intended. A great deal had been s%id about robbery. He would almost be prepared to forego principle, if the Establishment would DO A DEAL</p>
        <p>with him on the subject of tithe and undertake to restitute to its orginal objects the tithe, as laid down in the Act of Richard 4. They could have all the rest. Chat Act said that owing to tbs abuses which bad taken place and owing to the suff,r.ngs of the poor, tithes wera in future to be divided int) three portions. What was the first portion for—the stipend of the incumbent? No. The upkeep of the fabric? No. The first portion according to that Act, was for the maintenance and sustenance of the poor of the parish. (Applaure.) The second portion was for the upkeep of the fabric and the third for the stipend of the incumbent. If the Established Church wruld undertake to restore to the poor that portion of the tithe which had ben absorbed through the generations in the stipend of the incum- bent, he was prepared to do a deal vt!th them. They knew perfectly well that the tithes had not been used by the incumbents for the mainten- ance of the poor, within the memory of man, although it was a strange survival in the English Church seryice that, when a prieat was being ordained, be pledged himself to this day that be would go and find out the poor of the parish and minister to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>BROKE INTO MASS OF PIMPLES.</head>
        <p>BROKE INTO MASS OF PIMPLES.</p>
        <p>131. Bt ickgarth, E isington-lane, Durham, England.—" My little child aged three years broke into a mass of pimples over one h tlf of her head and one ear, which became very much swollen and then the pimples began to burst aLd all was a mass of running sores. We used five or six (Wien:tut kinds of ointments and powders which had very little or no effect. We thought the child's ear was going to m-roe right off. She could not sleep at nights ; we had to keep a bonnet on her for weeks when she was put to bed.</p>
        <p>" When we heard of Cuticura curing al child which was something similar to ours, we sent for a sample which began to dry it up. We got another tin of Cuticura. Ointment and BCIII9 more Cuticura Soap which made it beautiful and clean and healed it in about three weeks. There has never been a sign of a spot or pimple since," (Signed) Mrs E A Bunt, June 21, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold ky chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32 Th book post-free from nearest (Mint Newbary, IT Charterhouse Square,London ; 11. Towns &amp; Co„ gydney N SW. ; Lennon, Ltd, Cape Town ; Muller, Maclean and Co., Calcutta akd Bombay ; " Outicura," Boston, U S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticurn Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>COMPULSORY TRAINING IN AUSTRALIA, Agitation for a `, Conscience Clause."</head>
        <p>COMPULSORY TRAINING IN AUSTRALIA, Agitation for a `, Conscience Clause."</p>
        <p>Mr Noir Hirdie, ALP, writes from the House of Commons :—An agitation is on foot to secure the insertion of s conscience clause in the Compulsory Military 'Training Act which recently came into force in Australia and New Zmland. The movement seems ti IT meeting with a good deal of acceptance and particularly. in South Australia. The papers to hand from Adelaide inform us that a great public meeting to demand the insertion of a cmscieno3 clause in the Act was held recently in the Town Hall, Adelaide, with the Mayor in the chair. It was a town's meetitig, and the military ptrty, by advertising in the newspapers and otherwise, had nutria special edort to bring up all their supporters. A resolution denouncing the compulsory clauses of the Act and pledging the meeting to work for their repeal was submitted by Mr G F Hills. An arnmadment to the resolution was moved by the mil;tery party supporting the Government and in favour of the Act, but, as the newspaper report shows, it met with very scauty support, and the resolu- tion itself was carried by a very large majority." Mr Hills, who is a Quaker and a Conservative, now writes to me to say that the success of their campaign depends largely upon the amount of funds which they will have at their disposal during the next, few months. A number of well-to-do friends in this country have already sent handsome subscriptions for the specific purpose of conducting a general campaign for an anti- militarist and peace propaganda on religious linos. This money, however, is not available for the work of the new Freedom ',segue, with its specitlo objective of securing an amendment of the Military Training Act by the insertion of a conscience clause. The League is desirous of extending its campaign into other States lied this means money. May I suggest, therefore, that those who feel in sympathy with this movement and are in a position to render financial aid should do so promptly ? The agitation for compulsory military training is being pushed vigorously just now in Great Britain, and the example of Australia is being pointed to as a remelt why the working-class movement here should support the National Service League. If we can assist thoslS who are fighting compulsion in Australia to win sumer.% there, the precedent thus secured will be of great value when we are brought face to face with ectual Farkas mentary proposals for compelsory military service here at home, as I fear we sting be in the not very distant future.</p>
        <p>r.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ILL-NOURISHED CHILDREN AT RUNCORN. The Runcorn Education Committee was informed on Monday that out of the thirty-six patients in attendance at the recently-opened tuberculin dispensary, twelve were children. In several instances the children were so badly nourished that Dr. Annett thought it undesir- able to begin treatment until they obtained strength by better feeding. Mr. Imison said he thought that in some of the cases it was not that the parents were unable to provide food for their children, but that they bought improper food. The Vicar of Runcorn mentioned that he had arranged in 000 case for a boy to go to a house daily four times for tumblers of milk. After some discussion, the committee agreed that it was not possible to do anything at the moment, and the matter was deferred until the adoption of the Notifica- tion of Births Act and the formation of a Children's Care Committee. LICKED BURNING LIME. WIDNES FOREMAN'S COURAGEOUS DEEDS. A serious accident befell a roan named Herbert Ellis, while packing bleaching powder at the Widnes Alkali Works, on Tuesday. Ellis, who is engaged on the mechanical bleaching powder plant, manipulates what is known as the "stop box," through which the powder is transferred to the cylinders for bleaching purposes, and while be was try- ing to open the trapdoor of the stop box something went wrong. The unbleached lime poured in large quantities over his face, instantly blinding his eyes. Help was rendered almost immediately by John Coates, the foreman of the salt cake department, who, seeing the danger of the left eye being eaten away by the burning lime, licked large quantities of the burning material out of the eye. This undoubtly saved Ellis's sight, which, as it is, is very seriously affected. Ellis is twenty-five wears of age, and married, living in Oxford-street, Widnes. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr J Spruce presiding. VAGRANCY. The Cockermouth Union forwarded the following resolution for the endorsement of the Board :—" That in the opinion of this Board the time has arrived when the Local Government Board either by promoting legislation or otherwise should deal with the whole question of vagrancy, and further that any alteration in the existing law should provide for the establishment of labour colonies for habitual vagrants, and the proper treatment of genuine workmen in search of employment. On the motion of Mr Hallows the resolution was carried. He said that workingmen in search of work did not get proper treatment because of the vagrants. They needed putting in some colony. DECREASE IN PAUPERISM. Mr It G Daff reported on the State of pauperism in his district during the past 12 months. The statistics be said confirmed his impression that the year had witnessed a considerable decrease in the number of persons applying to the poor law for relief. Toe figures available for a one day count on the nearest avail +We date to December 31st were : Indoor 22,176 in 1911, and 28,815 in 1910, a decrease of 23 per cant; outdoor 1911, 11,009, as against 11,603, a decrease of 5 per cent. There was thus a large gross decrease in numbers principally due to decrease in the outdoor paupers, the ohms from which, as was shown in the last annual report the pauper old age pensioner is drawn. It seemed pro- bable that the Old Age Pension Act is still exercising such an effect on the statistics as to make it oifficnit to estimate what is the true decrease in public assistance due to the recent increase in trade prosperity. He regretted that over 30 unions in the district still main- tained healthy children of school age in the workhouses. During the year schemes had been adopted or were under considetation by several of the larger unions including Runcorn, for placing children out of the workhouse. Both in the Urban and Rural Unions emplosment had been more plentiful than for several years past. COTTAGE HOMES SUPERINTENDENT AND MATRON. Six ladies and gentlemen appeared before the Board as candidates for the positions of Superintendent and Matron of the Childrens' Home. After much discussion Mr and Mrs Goddard, Hungerford,Kent, were appointed at salaries of .£4O and £25 per year respectively. EXTRA CHRISTMAS RELIEF. Letters had teen received by the Clerk from several Unions sanctioning the payment of additional relief for the Christmas season. RESIGNATIONS. It was reported that Mrs shepOerd Cross and Mrs Barker had resigned their positions as members of the Ladies Visiting Committee. rNrAID CALLS. The CI.EnK reported that the calls of Acton Grange, Alvanley, Latchford Without and several other Parishes bad not been paid. It was decided that the Clerk write the assistant overseer in each case pointing out that the calls were overdue. HALTON MEDiCAL OFFICER. In accordance with notice ot motion given,Mr WOOD- WARD moved that Mr R Le Maxwell of Halton be appointed medical officer for the Halton district at a salary of £25 per annum. Mr T Wright seconded end said that Mr Maxwell had been locum tenons for Dr Carruthers for seveial years. Mr Morris suggested that the salary proposed should be increased from £25 to £3O. Mr Woodward said Le had no power to do so and no disposition to move an increase. He looked at ilia matter from the standpoint of the whole district and in his opinion the amount was a subsidy to a medical man living in Halton. Dr Maxwell had accepted the aPPointment on those terms. OVERSEER'S BONDS, , The Finance Committee reported the receipt of a letter from the Local Government Board with respect to the powers of Board of Guardians respecting the</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>bonds ot assistant overseers. The committee had resolved that each assistant overseer in the Union be requited to give a bond near to the amount of money which may pass through his hands as assistant overseer during any half year. That all assistant overseers and their private bondsmen he notified of the Boards requirmerts and a letter informing them that a bond with an approved society would he found more satisfactory to all concerned That the respeative Councils and chairmen of the parish meetings be informed of the Board's requirements, and that in all cases where the overseers bad ueen previously guaranteed by an approved society or private bonds the overseer's salary be increased by the amount required to meet the new or increased premiums.</p>
        <p>i‘lr Woodward said that the Finance 'Committee had bad its attention called to the insufficiency of many of the bonds now existing throughout the area of the Board. The Board might to be fully secured from all losses, and the committee had been struck by the com- paratively small amount of bond required. It was absolutely nocessaay that a full and sufficient bond should be given to cover any defalcation that might occur in, say, one half year. The Committee realised how difficult it was for private persons who were asked to become bondsmen by friends to refuse. The situa- tion was a very delicate one,and they thought it would be more satisfactory if a general statement was made that bonds for a guaranteed socieiv would be required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>THANKS.</head>
        <p>THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Visiting Committee expressed its thanks to Mrs J W Jones, of Runcorn, for gifts of Christmas cards for the inmates and to a Frodsham lady for gifts of periodicals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>DECREASE.</head>
        <p>DECREASE.</p>
        <p>The customary statietics showed a decrease both in the number ot inmates of the House and in the number of vagrants provided with accommodation as compared to the corresponding period of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Rnneorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton on luesdsy, Mr C E Linaker presiding.</p>
        <p>NEW ASSISTANT SURVEYOR. For the position of A,sistant Surveyor, there were three applies' ions. The successful candidate was Mr A H Leece, of Witt rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MERTON-ROAD rmpnovE:q ENT.</head>
        <p>MERTON-ROAD rmpnovE:q ENT.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Weston Parochial Committee showed that plans 11,c1 been submitted for sewering the new houses being erected on Runcorn Heath. It was resolved that the owners be informed that the Council is prepared to bear one-third of the coat of laying the sewer across the Heath t 9 join up to the Runcorn Urban Council's sewer. The Clerk reported that Mr C Evans had brought forward the fence of property in Post Office-lane belonging to the Orred Estate at a point opposite the chapel and the road which it was intended to make under the provisions of the Private Street Works ACt, 1892, had thereby been narrowed to about 18 feet. It as resolved that the road be made to its present width and that the Orred estate be charged with their due proportion of such frontage as faces the chapel and also down the street. Plans for a new cottage of the Salt Union Ltd were presented and, subject to amenclmwnt in certain particu- lars pointed out by the Surveyor, were recommended for approval. The minutes were approved. APPROVED SOCIETIES' AUDIT. The Local Goverumennt Board wrote stating that many approved societies had no offices of their own and that, for the purposes of a formal audit of account it was undesirable that private or licensed premises should be used. It was suggested that local authorities might allow their premises to be used for this purpose. So far as Boards of Guardians were concerned the Local Government Beard was prepared to give its consent subject, to the Guardians reserving to themselves the power to determine the arrangement at any time if it was inconvenient.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said he did not suppose their rooms would be wanted very much. This being the case no action was takEn in the matter It as decided in view of the holidays to have the next meeting of the Council a month hence and not a fortnight as is usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>BABY DRUNK.</p>
        <p>SPIRITS FOR EIGHTEEN MONTHS OLD CHILD. Theresa Harrison, of 5, Carnarvon-street, Matto Heath, and her husband, Frederick, were charg( d before the St. Helens magistrates on Tuesday with cruelly neglecting their five children. Mr T Garner,who prosecuted for the National Society called Inspector Lull, who said that he had known Mrs Harrison four years, and she was of exceedingly drunken habits ; while the man did not work anything like full time. The five children were aged twelve.ten, eight, four, and Ii years, and they had practically to camp out in a house where there was to furniture, and no pots or domestic utensils. The parents and the children all s'ept OD one bed, which was very filthy. On December lati, when he decided to remove the children to the workhouse, lie found the youngest child of eighteen months, in a comatose condition, it evidently having been given spirits to drink in a house where the woman had been drinking. Sergeant Stewart said that he noticed these children very badly clothed, and looking starved, and followed them home with the object of getting them some relief from tVe police-aided scheme. He then found that the house was in a terrible state and smelling badly. The woman was a most heartless and filthy woman. He had cautioned them, but all they did was to borrow clothes for the children to wear while he made his call. Dr Doran said that when the children were admitted to the Workhouse they were in a shocking state, and the baby of eighteen months was in a comatose state as a result of having been made drunk. It continued in that stat 4, for over twenty-four hours. Both prisoners were sent to goal for six months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>, LAS .../cT E.,nr ;,</head>
        <p>, LAS .../cT E.,nr ;,</p>
        <p>LYM M.</p>
        <p>SCRIPTURE STUDENTS' SUCCESS.—We are pleased to notice that arm ngst the successful candidates in the recently held Primitive Methodist Connexional Scripture Examirations, Lynim is represented by : Alfred Dennis Priestlier, who obtained a first class certificate in the Lower Middle Division; Lois Emily Priestlier, a second class certificate in the Upper Middle Division ; and Ernest Phillips, a second class certificate in the Senior Division. RUMMAGE SALES.—In accordance with the annual custom the Parish Church Rummage Sale was held in the Parochial Schools on Saturday afternoon last. The poor people look enrgerly forward to this sale as a means of securing some useful bargains and there was a full attendance of appreciative buyers on the present occasion though some of the patrons no doubt went to the ‘Vesloyan School where another very successful jumble school was in progress. SALES BY AUCTION.—It is not often that the Lymm district has three auction sates in two days, and yet that has been the case this week. On Tuesday the household furniture and effects of the late Mrs A M Brigg were offered for sate ; whilst on Thursday the residue of how,ehold furniture at Birch Brook House, ifeatley, came under the hammer. Also on Thursday ' afternoon a number of lots consisting of horses, traps, harness, motor, t&amp;c., were disposed of at the Spread Eagle Hotel Yard.</p>
        <p>BREAK-UP AT THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—The scholars of the Grammar School held their "Breaking-up sing song " t.m Saturday evening last, before a crowded audience. The larger part of the programme was taken up by a series of well executed playlets performed by a number of the scholars ; one item being carried on thoughout in the Latin tongue. Miss Hill played the imtermezzo from " Cavaliers Rusticsna ' Eon the violin and also contributed selections of popular songs on the piano. The Heatley Troop of the B.P. Scouts gave an exhibition of handbell ringing, and a pianoforte duet and some vocal items were also added.</p>
        <p>NAVY LEAGUE LECTURE.—Under the auspices of the Navy League, Port of Manchester Branch, Mr Whyman, gave a topical lecture in the Conservative Club en Friday evening last week on "The British Navy." Owing to the very stormy weather there was only a small audience present, but those who had braved the elements were amply rewarded by hearing a well c'elivered lecture and seeing some remarkable lantern Octures thrown on the screen by the skilful hand of Mr Reynolds of Thelwall. Mr W P Rylands whilst introducing the lecturer spoke of the great interest that all Britons took in the Navy and said that it was the object of the Navy League to bring home to the minds of the people of this country the importance of the " Senior Service."</p>
        <p>POSTAL ARRANGEMENTS FOR CITRISTMAS.—WO trust that the following information re the special local arrangements for dealing with the Christmas postal traffic will be of value to our readers. On the 24th of December the night mails for general dispatch will be collected at the village Post Office, Booth's Hill, and other boxes at 7 p.m., after which no dispatches other than for Lymm district will be guaranteed. The Post Offices will he open for other business until 8 p.m. as usual. Persons who wish to have their cards etc.. delivered locally on Christmas morning, must post them between the hours of 5 p.m., December 24th and 4 a.m. December 25th. The Postmaster, Mr Madely, whose strenuous and successful efforts to deal with the very heavy mails of the past two years, informs us that he is increasing his staff to twenty membets, and hopes to get all the mails away on the 24th inst as usual, though, as he points out, they stand a big risk of getting held up at the sorting centres, and therefore strongly advises the posting of any letters or parcels that have to go a distance on the night of the 23rd December.</p>
        <p>lA'ESLEYAN SALE OF WORK.—With the object of raising funds towards clearing off the Chapel debt the local Wesleyan Methodists held a Sale of Work in the schoolroom on Friday of last week. The proceedings were formally opened by Mrs J H Pawlyn, with Mr H S Brown presiding. The room and stalls had been gaily decorated with bunting, etc. by Messrs Finch, and A H Hudson, assisted by members of the school. There was a confectionery gall, on which a choice variety of edibles was Exposed for sale ; a large miscellaneous stall supplied with an assortment of useful and fancy goods, Paper flowers, games, &amp;c. ; a Christmas tree resplendent with its short lived " flowers and fruits" ; and of course the indespensible tea room. Concerts werelaiven by such artistes es the Misses F Rogerson, and Wiustanley and Messrs. Cooke, H Hudson,Margetts A B Pryce, E Waterworth, L Watherworth ; Mrs Frank Brown being the accompanist. The attendance was rather poor but this was no doubt due to the unpro- pitious weather. A plentiful supply of willing worker was found in Mesdano,s H S Brown, F Brown, Finch Hudson, Pawlyn. and Winstantley, and the Misses Furness, Halford, Jones, M Moston, Pawlyn, Pickton, Waterworth and Winstanley.' . . .</p>
        <p>A CHRISTAILS FOREWORD.—Once more the Spirit of Christmas has come upon Lymm ; a faint trace of frost seems to be in the air, voices appfar to be brighter;smiles more plentiful, button-holes of rnisletoe or holly are the order of the day, and the errand boy whistles his liveliest airs,and exudes politeness at every pore. Dining the evenino of the past week the band has been out playing and for quite a month would-be "carol singers" have been soiling many clean front door steps. This Christmas should be one of the happiest Lymm has ever known for besides the usual modes of cocuoation being in a flourishing condition there has been a big demand for labour on the new avenue, and the seuerage scheme. The shop-keepers have no doubt had this in mind, for this year they seem to have surpassed tbeeosel Yes in the art of window dress- ing, Niessrs E Brown and Sons long experience enabling them to make a striking display. As for goods shown in the various shops, one has a selection ranging from, say, a bottle of scent, a box of chocolates, a book, jewellery to a whole lady's or gentleman's complete outfit. Santa Claus should experi- ence no difficulty in obtaining a supply of surprises, and certainly the housewife will be spoiled for choice owing to the large, tempting and excellent quality of goods that have been obtained for her. That the pro- prietors have striven to do their best is revealed on enquiry at MrJ B Egerton's shop, where we were told that he had purchased three first prize heifers and the first prize pen of sheep from the Northwich Christmas fat stock sale, and first prize lambs from the'Norton Arms sale. PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDN'ESDAY.—Before Mr W A Watkin (iu the chair), Major Groves, Hon J E Cross, Messrs W P Rylands, S Davies .and P J_,Ridgway. . . .</p>
        <p>DRIVEFL FlNED.—Bertie Cooper, 372, Knutsford-road, Warrington, was fined 5s including costs, for being the driver of a pony cart and not displaying a red lignt at the rear of the vehicle in Warrington-road, High Legh, at 6.20 p.m. on the 29th November. "GUILTY TO TEMPER."—Emma Wadsworth, Rush Green, Lymm, was summoned for having been drunk and disorderly in Rush Green on the 17th November. When asked if she were guilty defendant replied, " guilty to temper, but not drunk. —From the evidence it appeared that defendant was, at 12.30 a.m. screaming and shouting and tumbling about in the middle of the road in a drunken condition. After creating a disturbance in the neighbourhood she refused to go home.—Defendant said she was in a temper but not drunk. Her husband was attending to another woman who had got drunk.—The Chairman pointed out that in June last the defendant signed the pledge.—Defendant:-1 did take it hut I have not kept it. 1 like telling the truth.—The Chairman : You seem to come here just every twelve months—a sort of annual visit. The magistrates retired and on their return defendant was fined 7s. 6d. including costs. —Defendant will 2s. 6d. do on Saturday and I will pay ss. now ?—The Chairman : Yes. i 1 thitk they will do that.—Defendant : My name is good, I think. —The Clerk : Your voice is.—Defendant : Thank you, good morning. MOTORIST'S OFFENCE.—Reginald Ernest Dickinson, Grove House, Highgate, London war; fined 10s. and costs for having failed to produce his motor licence when requested. DESTRUCTIVE Younis.—Nine youths were summoned for doing &amp;mega to windows,and cupboards and brick- work at a cottage in Booths Hill, the property of Thomas Hewitt, a local property owner.—The damage was estimated by the pros3cutor to be £2 10s. and the date of the alleged offence was November 10th.—The lads were Henry Cadman (19) Booths Hill, Sidney Moston (17) Booths Hill, Percy Taylor (15) West Hyde Jas Taylor (IS), West Hyde, Wm Jackson (15) 5,1 Mostou-street, Acores Fogg (16) 11. Moston-street and William, Henry, and Arthur Read. 20, 19 and 17 years of age respectively 8, Moston-street all of Lymm. In evidence prosecutor said the cottage was rented at 2s.lt had been used as a storage and was occupied up to the 3.td Nov. From time to time he bad given notices warn- ing people against doing damage. In i he present case the windows were broken, the door burst open, plaster knocked off the wall and the place ransacked. He wanted to stop such diseraceful proceeding. The cupboard door ass missring altogether.—By Mr H E Kay (Manchester) who appeared for the youths. He did not know any of the defendants. Mr Kay: I suppose this is only an apology for a house. If you sit at the far side of the room your feet are touching the front door ?—Prosecutor : 1 don't see that it has anything to do with you.—Mr Kay : You say it has been used as a store and I suppose if they are loading potatoes &amp;c. and pitching boxaa into the place things are bound to get damaged 2—That is my business and nothing to do with you.—Margaret Marsh, Booths Hill, Lymm, deposed to seeing all the lads in the cottage when she was going to Sunday school. She knew all of them and they were inside the cottage running about and making a great noise. She saw two stones thrown and she also heard the sound of a window being broken.—Jane Skelborne, The Rookery, Booth's Lymm, was called as a witness for the prosecution Ibut in the box she admitted that she had never seen any lof the boys in the cottage. She knew nothing at all ' about the case, or why she had been asked to go as a witness.—Mr Kay said it was admitted that the boy</p>
        <p>Taylor did send some plaster which went through the window accidentally, otherwise there was no case at all. The witness Marsh, had been trying to make her- self notorious through the case.—The lads all denied having done any damage and the defendant Arthur Reid said he had never been in the cottage. The door had been open for a long time before November 10th. A new lock had been put on during the week.—William Jackson denied having been in the cottage. Each lad was ordered to pay 6s costs each.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED ENLARGEMENT OF THE COUNCIL. INQUIRY BY THE COUNTY COUNCIL . _ . •</p>
        <p>The application of the Urban Council for the in—crease of its membership from ten to eleven, the additional member to be allocated to Statham Ward, formed the subject of an inquiry conducted at the Council offices on Tuesday morning, by Lieut.-Col. Legh, Messrs J W Sidebotham and John Arnold, on behalf of the Cheshire County Council. Lieut -Colonel Legh presided. Mr W Mullard (clerk and collector) appeared on behalf of the Urban Council, and Mr }Ebben represented the County Council. The members of the Council present were Messrs W G Groves (chairman), It II Gotobed, J H Holt, and F Lockyer. There was only a small attend- ance of the pablic,vc hicli led the Chairman to remark that the inquiry did not seem to have raised any great excitement in the neighbourhood. :they hoped that whatever decision they arrived at would be agreeable to everyone ccrucerned.</p>
        <p>Mr MuHard explained that at the present time Statham had only one member, while the other wards had three. Since 1891 the number of houses in the ward had increased by thirty-one,and the rateable value by £1,139. Mr Groves supported the, application, and said that since the representation of the ward was originally fixed there had been a great increase in property. The Council were of opinion that Stathan Ward should have an tidditional member. Mr Gotobed, in supporting the application, said many important questions cropped up in the district, and Statham bad only a voice in local affairs every three years, when they elected their member. Mr G Parker and Mr Latham also expressed the view that the present representation was inadequate. The inquiry closed with a vote of thanks being accorded CO the gentleman who had conducted it.</p>
        <p>:it 7,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD —On Wednesday the members of the guild and many friends also, were entertained for the second time to a musical evening by the choir. This entertainment had been long looked forward to as a , most interesting event and everyone felt repaid in the extreme when the evening came, for the room was crowded with a very representative audience. Regret was expressed at the unavoidable absence of Miss Dutton through slight indisp, sition. 'lhe programme was a long and very varied one aud consisted of piano- forte duets, part songs, songs, duets and two sketches. It is saying little when we say that all acquitted them- selves in a very praiseworthy manner and won much apple use from the audience. The chair was taken by Mr J Jackson, who made a very genial chairman and spoke in kindly strain of the willing services of the Moore choir, Mr Benner, Mrs John Jackson and the Misses Theodore were the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.—A grand concert and cantata entitled " Flags of the nations," was given in the Wesleyan schools on Friday, the 13th inst, by the officials and members of the Penketh Band of Hope, when a fair number of sympathisers with this interest- ing work were present. The cantata was given by a choir, who carried flags and wore badges representing the different nations,and a most creditable performance was presented. The miscellaneous programme was taken part in by Miss Evelyn Booth, Messrs A Plurupton, H Pearson, It Mid wood, S Bailey, B Smith, Gladys, Doris and Hilda Baker, and Ethel and Ruth Davidson. Miss Parker accompanied and Mr Elliott was the conductor.</p>
        <p>WESLiTAN GUILD.—LITERARY SECTION.—In the Wesleyan schools on Wednesday evening the above section of the Wesley Guild surpassed its usual arrange- ments to provide an evening's enjoyment to the Guild members and friends. With that enterprising secretary Mrs Bell, end her enthusiastic and willing helpers, the company's expectations were more than realised and certainly proved that a great deal of talent is in Penketh Jf put into use. The entertainment was largely drawn from Charles Dickens' works.—Mrs P Garnett gave an interesting discourse on Charles Dickens, and exhorted all those who had not already been readers of Dickens' works to start at once, as she was sure they would be abundantly blessed in the realing of his great works. Misses G Bell and Mr Routledge sang beautifully a duet entitled " What are the wild waves saying ?" Then the Bartlett v Pickwick scene was acted. The numerous characters in the scene were admirAly taken by Miss Parker (Mrs Bardell), Mr H Elliott (Mr Pickwick), Mr H Davies (Sergeant Bustim), Mr IA Carn.an (Sam Weller), Messrs W Lexley, H Ambler, A Plurupton, W Ray, C Midwood, E A Murphy, A Richardson, G Carman, Master Garnett, Miss Cooke, Miss Routledge and others. The chair was occupied by Mr P C Garnett, and a vote of thanks was heartily passed at the close, on the motion of the Rev H Middlewood, seconded by Mr C E Parker.</p>
        <p>PENKETH COUNCIL CHOOL.—The annual Council school concert was held on hursday evening in the Wesleyan Sunday Schools. This concert attracts a great deal of attention pwing to its efficiency and the extraordinary talent displayed by the children of the day school and because all the scholars take some part In this grand event. Great patience must have been exercised by the ha do aster, Mr D Griffiths and Mrs Bell, the hcadmistiess of the Infants Department. Dr Murray, one of the managers of the schools, pre- sided, and was supported by his colleagues, with the exception of Mr Withinshaw, who was unable to attend. The school was packed to the doors. The programme was of a variety character, the opening item being a recitation entitled " Welcome " by seven boys (and the audience naturally and enthusiastically welcomed them likewise.) The next item was a song by about 15 girls and boys, and was heartily applauded. Following that the infants took part in an action song, entitled " Christmas Morning" rest come en action song entitled "Ten little mothers" ten girls having a doll each, an&amp; of course dressed very approptiately. Another item was recitation by a little girl and boy of the infant class, namely Florence White and Percy Bailey. These two were about the age of seven and the audience was delighted at the . perfect manner they said their recitations Snow Flakes "was given by the boys and girls in the Infant Department who acquitted themselves well. Miss Isiewbold's class then occupied the platform and had a goed reception. 16 girls with sashes and each a bow decorated with artificial flowers. The piece was called " The Garland Drill " and the di ffeient evolu- tions were earried out extremely well. The Infants were again applauded on appearing on the platform, when they gave an interestang item called. " Bed Time March," which caused roars of laughter Ten little Chinamen then confronted the audience apd performed their allotted task with great skill, and the same may be said for 12 girls in the ribbon action song, the 16 fisher girls, and the dentist's action song. Miss Newbculd's class distinguished itself in a pianoforte song and dance. The wand drill was capitally performed by the elder boys, and " Santa Claus"l laden with</p>
        <p>• r • 9. I.</p>
        <p>presents made a most welcome closing item. Dr Murray spoke strongly in favour of the evening school, and of the cookery classes at the Friends' School, finally expres,ing the thanks of the audience for the evening's admirable entertainment. STOCKTON HEAI`I-1.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS TREE AND SALE OF WORK.—A success ful sale of work in connection with St. Thomas' Church was held in the Church Room on Saturday afternoon. In addition to the Christmas tree, there was a plain work stall, Girls' Friendly Society stall and a refresh- ment stall, all of which were presided over by lady , members of the congregation. The proceeds were in aid of the Society for Propagating the Gospel in Foreign Parts, the Church of England Waifs and Strays Society, and parochial objects. LANTERN EXHIEITION.—A lantern exhibition of local and other views was given by Mr Hallows on Thursday evening on behalf of St. Thomas' Band of Elope. The chair was taken by Mr Whitman and there was a good attendance. Tie views were intersected with songs by Miss M Isherwood who was recalled each time. Mr Fairclough • was the accompanist. Mrs C Hallows gained a good reception by her rendering of" The news boy's debt," while Mr Whitley earned loud applause by his banjo solos. During the evening the Vicar, on behalf of the workers, presented Mr Noden, the boy's secretary, who is removing to Liverpool, with a silver-mounted photo frame on behalf of the workers. Messrs Hallows, \Vl:Menton, Stanuard, and C Hallows also spoke in glowing terms of the good work done by Mr and Mrs Noden and wished them every success in the future. Mr Noden suitably replied, and said he hoped the Band of Hope would go forward and prosper. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>PXDGATE.</head>
        <p>PXDGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—At the Wesley Guild meeting en Monday evening the temperance question was di, cussed from various standpoints. Dr Bennett examined it from the medical point of view, touching on the effects of alcohol on the system generally—especially on the digestive organs and nerves. He quoted the opinions ot Dr Nansen and other Arctic explorers, who averred that as a protection against cold, alcohol was quite useless. Mr C F Morgan spoke on the legal aspect of temperance. The commercial point of view was ' expounded by Mr J Jarman and the mural aspect by Mrs Simpson. WESLEYAN CHURCH CHOIR SERMONS.—The annual sermons in aid of the choir funds were preached in the Wesleyan Church, Padgate, on Sunday, in the presence of good congregations. Special hymns and music were I rendered by the choir. At the morning service the anthem, " Glory to God " (Dr Wesley) was excellently sung and the recitative, " Then shall the eyes," and the air," He shall feed His Rock " (Handel) were beauti- fully rendered by Madame Lily Fairhurst. At the evening service the principal vocalist was Miss Hipkiss, who sang Mendelssohn's ariosa, " 1 will sing of 'Thy great mercies," with much taste and expression. The trio, " Lift thine eyes " (Mendelssolin), was well rendered by Mrs Cottrell, Miss Appleton and Miss Duckworth. The choral, "Sleepers,awake," was beauti- fully sung by the choir. The preacher at the mottling service was the Rev W A Beckett, and in the evening the Rev E Morgan. DEATH OF MR JOHN FAIRHIIRST.—We regret to announce the death of Mr John Fairhurst, late farm bailiff and porter of the Padgate Cottage Homes, which took place at Croft on Saturday. His decease was not unexpected, as he was known to be suffering from a serious complaint. 'The deepest sympathy has been expressed with Mrs Fairhurst in the great loss which she has sustained. The funeral took place at Croft Church on Wednesday afternoon, the Rev H I Wadeson officiating. The Warrington Board of Guardians was represented by Messrs E L Bent (chairman), \V Houghton (the senior Guardian), G Mather and A Bottomley (clerk). Many beautiful floral tributes were received.</p>
        <p>... ~„. .. , . . 011_7 • - ----• .-7.--, .„, . ,:f.: , .•• ..,..,, ~ ILLdeiti ' • lISI . . 4.,..= t.......r., itukho</p>
        <p>Former Warrington Curate for St. Helens</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Parish.</head>
        <p>Parish.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool, on Wednesday inducted the Rev John W Glendenning, M.A., formerly curate of St. Barnabas', Warrington, to the living of St. Thomas's, St. Helens. There was a large congregation, including many of the local clergy, Canon Tyrer, and the Rev Albert Baines (vicar of St. Helens). The Bishop said the service was a call to the clergyman to devote him- self to the shepherding of the people committed to his care. For ten years Mr Glendenning had been diligent and faithful in various curacies. The service was also a call to the people to rally round him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday last, presided over by Mr W Beswick who spoke on " Forgiveness. " Christmas time, he said,was a very appropriate time for exercising this virtue. Mr Hallows, of Stockton Heath, gave the address on " Lessons from Saul previous to being anointed king." Saul, be said, had a good feeling cowards his parents and showed obedience and a loving desire to please them. A young man mani- fested the excellence of his character, by the strong desire to do that which was pleasing to his parents. Mr C Cotton read the lesson, and the chairman led the prayer. One new member received a hearty welcome. The President read a letter from Rev T T James, M.A., who has accepted the cull to Wycliffe Church. Mr Hallons brought greetings from Stockton Heath men's meeting which were reciprocated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Guild.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Guild.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance of members at last Monday's meeting, presided over by Miss Dewar, to hear a paper on "The gmancipation of Woman" by Miss Moore. The speaker, in an admirable manner, sketched out the graaual emancipation of woman from the 17th century, and drew attention to the far wider sphere of activities at the presenttime, due to altered conditions of life end work, spoke of the great influence for good, exercised and extended to all causes for ihe betterment of humanity, and gave illustrations to show how sweating, housing and other social problems would be helped forward by the wider opportunities that would be theirs with the extension of the franchise ( n a fair, just and equitable basis. By this means, women generally would take a deeper and more serious interest in the larger life of the nation, and develop a true patriotism.—The paper was followed with the closest interest, and after an extended discussion, a cordial vote of thanks was awarded Miss Moore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>• " Messiah " at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>The • " Messiah " at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>___------- —.- The rendering of Handel's masterpiece by the Legh- street choir on Monday was most artistic, and in every way a thorough success. Under the training of Mr A Spruce the choir has reached an advanced state of pro- ficiency, and this was amply evidenced in the gratifying effects to which the congregations listened with such rapt attention last Sunday afternoon and even- ing. The soloists were :—Mrs Hoyle, Misses Woodland, A Worthington and M Jones (sopranos), Misses H Spruce and L Plinston (contraltos), Mr Levi Richardson (tenor), and Mr N Cook, jun (bass). The programmes were so well con- ceived that each service was a complete unity of some section of the mission of Jesus Christ. Mr Banner officiated at the organ, and when one considers the state of the instrument one marvels that he gained such strik- ing and accurate effects in some of the more intricate passages. In the afternoon the choir and soloists presented a musical picture of the prophetic declara- tion of the coming of the Messiah, together with its fulfilment on that first Christmas Day. In the evening most of the time was devoted to Passion music. The scene was so realistically presented that the programme became a piece of genuine worship, moving from the reverent contiamplation of the Passion of Christ, to the mighty triumph of christianity over the world. Each of the soloists did very well, and entered into their task with true feeling. It would he difficult to say which of the choruses was most enjoyed, for all were finished and expressive. The Rev W Cooper gave brief addresses, which were in substance expositions of the programme, and enabled the audiences to follow the music with better appreciation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>_Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>_Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev It Rainey Workman, of Rochdale, occupied the pulpit at this chnrch on Sunday. In an address to the children he asked them to keep the flag flying. Whether at play or in school let them play the game. It Was easy to play the fool and easy to go wrong, but if they were to be worthy sons and daughters they must keep the flag flying at whatever cost. The subject of the sermon was Philip's remark to Christ " Lord, show us the Father, and it sufficeth us" (John xiv., 8.) He pointed out that although Philip had been with Christ longer than most of the disciples yet be only knew his Wlaster superficially. There were many in our day who knew Christ in the same way. Philip did not know Him as He meant to be known and as He ought to be known. There were many who had a superficial know- ledge of Christ's history but who did not know Him as a personal living Lord. Faith was necessary to behold Him in His true light. The purpose of Christ's life was to reveal the Father. He broke down the old theories of God as a taskmaster and showed that He was a God of love.</p>
        <p>In the evening, the discourse was based on the text ' The secret things belong unto the Lord our God: but those things which are revealed belong unto us and our children for ever," (Dent. 20.0 29v.). The secrets of to-day, he said, might be the revelation of to-morrow, but there was a point beyond which we could not go. The preacher referred to some of the wonderful revelations of science in regard to the human body, botany and electricity, but even in regard to these there were secrets we could not discover. We did not know what life is ; nor did we know what electricity is. The secret things were God's but what I was revealed belonged to men. There were secret</p>
        <p>things in the personality of Christ that we never understood and never would understand. In regard to the atonement the doctrine was once preached that Christ died to appease an angry God. He had no hesita- tion in saying that that doctrine was not true. It was a libel on the love of God. God was in Christ recon- ciling the world unto Himself; that was a very differ- ent thing from reconciling Himself unto the world.</p>
