      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS TO MR. OWEN TOOLE</head>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS TO MR. OWEN TOOLE</p>
        <p>One of the most enthusiastic gatherings that has ever been witnessed in tl.e United Irish League Hall, Winwick-street. was held on Sunday evening. According to the bills which advertised the event,it was a limelight lecture and presentation. The subject of the lecture as :—" The Story of Ireland : Historical and Political." It was presided over by Mr D Currie the president of the John Maginn Branch, United Irish League. The lecturer was Mr Owen Toole (an ex-councillor of Warrington). Mr Currie in a very well chosen speech, introduced the lecturer, who, on coming forward, received a great ovation. Mr TooLE took the audience back in imagination to 432 AD when St Patrick landed in Ireland to preach the doctrines of Christianity, and gradually led an attentive audience through the history of Ireland to the present day. He showed he was master of his subject, by touching on some of the most important epochs in Ireland's sad yet glorious story. He depicted in telling language the horrible persecution to which the Irish race had been subjected and also described the work of Grattan's Parliament, the meetings presided over by the master-orator of Ireland, Daniel O'Connell. The lecturer said the methods of Irish Nationalists might be different to-day, but they still had the same ideals, the same longing for freedom as in the days of old. He quoted appropriately Parnell's words " No power CAD stop the progress of a nation," at the same time explainin the meaning the great Irish leader meant to convey and ably frustrating the ignorant attempts to interpret them differently. He put forward some very able argu- ments for Irish self-government, and delighted the audience with little touches of genuine humour at the expeme of "King" Carson. He concluded by eloquently appealing to his hearers to stick hard and fast to the U.1.L., and its leaders whose unflinching efforts were shortly to be rewarded by the realisation of their hopes. A very pleasing feature in the lecture was the intro- duction of Thomas Davis's poem "Pontenoy," which was recited with great power and pathos by Mr William McNevin, just as Mr Toole had related the fortunes of the." Wild geese."</p>
        <p>Mr Cuttitv then sale] that he had a very important duty to perform and that was to present Mr and Mrs. Toole with a silver rose bcsl from the members of the U.1.L., John Maginn Branch. The Chairman said he was sure he was voicing the sentiments of everyone present and of everyone who had the interests of the Irish cause at heart in saying that Mr Owen Toole was one of the Irish stalwarts who kept alive the spirit of nationality for many years in the hearts of Irish men and women in Warrington. Wherever the interests of the old land were concerned, there wculd be found Owen Toole, so he hoped that although Mr Toole was going away from them, they would often have the pleasure of seeing him amongst his old colleagues of the Warrington Irish League. (Applause). Mr WAI MCNEVIN proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer for the admirable treat he had given them and said that is was such glimpses of the past that made them better Irishmen and Irishwomen. They had a history to be proud of, though a portion of it was written in blood ancLtears. Ireland was and always would be a nation, possessing its own individuality, patriotism, traditions, music, literature, games and not- withstanding several attempts to exterminate it, its own language. Miss KILGANNON, president of the Ladies' Branch, A.O.H. Warrington,seconded and said that it was a pity they did not know more about the grand history of their country.</p>
        <p>Mr MAI-10:.\T supported. The vote of thanks having been passed with acclama- tion, Mr TOOLE said he could hardly find words adequately to thank them for their kind presentation and a vote of thanks so enthusiastically accorded by his many friends. He assured them that he and his wife would treasure deeply this mark of their esteem and that he would stick to the Irish cause as long as he lived. (Applause.) A most enjoyable concert took place, contributed to- by :—Messrs J Flynn, J McGeer, F Cain, 0 Toole,E Handley and the Misses Hinsdale and Percival. Mr Austin Crowe proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman, referring to Mr Currie's sterling qualities as a Nationalist. Mr Handley seconded. The singing of " God save Ireland" by a most enthusiastic audience brought a most enjoyable evening to a close.</p>
        <p>A presentation to Mr Toole from the Catholic Young Men's Society will be found reported on the next page.</p>
        <p>St. Benedict's Schools. In aid of the recent school extension, a concert was given in St Benedict's School, Orford-lane, the other evening by the Arcadian Glee Party. The artistes were Miss L Hughes (soporano), Miss May Cook (con- tralto), Miss Dorothy Turner (commedienne), Mr R P Wilson (tenor), Mr 11 Barlow (humorist), and Mr Fred Richmond, A.L.O M. (bass and violin). A very bright and pleasing programme was gone through and much enjoyed by a fairly large audience. During the evening the Rev F Jackson, 0.5.8., thanked the con- cert party for cffering their services to help the parishioners of St Benedict's in the heavy expenses which had been incurred by the recent extension to the schools. Mr Richmond, the leader of the concert party, briefly responded and expressed the pleasure it gave them to help the voluntary and denominational school.</p>
