      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>12</p>
        <p>THE BONAR BOTCH. FOOD TAX FOLLY. MR. CROSFIELITS STINGING CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR OF THE " EXAMINEE."3 Sir,—ln more than one of the speeches I have delivered in Warrington this autumn I have dwelt upon decadence amounting at times to posi- tive demoralisation of the modern Tory and Tariff Reform Party. When the leaders of this Party openly encourage rebellion amongst the Orange- men of North-East Ireland, and the "methods of the pot-house brawler" in the House of Com- mons, then indeed degeneration may well have seemed to touch bottom. . • But in point of sheer .ecklessness of policy— not to say negation of policy—combined with hopeless conflict and confusion alike of leaders, press and party, Toryism and Tariff Reform surely approached the very Nadir of their misfor- tunes when Mr. Bonar Law proposed on Monday night that we should leave it to the Colonies to decide whether the Britisher should tax his bread and butter. On Monday morning we were told in the Unionist press that "the only salvation for the Unionist Party lies in rallying like one man round its leader when to-night's speech has been made." And then see how they rally I Mr. Aus- tin Chamberlain speaks the same night as his (professed !) leader and coldly ignores the whole of this new policy, while the next morning the chief organ in tale press of Mr. Law's party— the Times newspaper—writes as follows : "Unionist leaders must not attempt to shift the onus of food taxation on to a Conference of Ministers from oversea. If such taxation is not desirable as a- measure of purely domestic reform, then it is not desirable at all. If the people of this, country will not believe in it as such, they must not be asked ti believe in it at all on the strength of opinion elsewhere. No Dominion statesmen would dream of dealing with a Dominion Tariff on such lines as those. No statesman must dream of it here." To come a bit nearer home there is the Liver- pool Courier, the most influential Conservative paper in the North-West, telling us that 'Mr. Bohar Law is making a serious mistake. If he still follows the course outlined last night, why then we shall have no alternative but to follow him—even tlaough it be with a heavy</p>
        <p>heart." • And already We have Colonial statesmen making it clear that they will be no party to risking Imperial peace and strength by trying to force food taxes down our throats. Small wonder that Mr. Bonar Law received that silent and chilling reception from his own party on his return to the House from Ashton-under-Lyne. Not a cheer, it seems, even from the precious +group of "Con- federates I" Surely this exponent of "the new style," spatch-cocked into the leadership of the Tariff Reformers, is not to be envied; and, he will turn in vain for pity to political opponenfs who watch him in his hopeless plight with an amusement not unmixed with the scornful con- tempt that has been felt for the insolent vul- garity with which he has systematically and per- sistently thrown his -epithets and vituperation .a statesmen whose shoe latchets he is not fit to tie. Time was when Mr. Balfour's fine spun web at least partially concealed from the plain man in the street f6e ugly features of Protection. It is as well therefore we have had Mr. Bonar Law lifting a corner, and more than a corner of the curtain. We see the drama which the Food Taxers and Protectionists are acting on the boards, and we know very well the ugly ending of the play if they are allowed to finish it in their own way. Let every Progressive then see to .it that the men who dare to threaten the humble homes ot I England with these evil and iniquitous taxes on I those things on which our people absolutely depend for health and comfort are hissed and I hooted off the boards.—Yours, etc., A. H. CROSFIELD. 39 Hyde Park Gate, S.W. December 19th, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>TARIFF ASN AN.." OLD MAN</head>
        <p>TARIFF ASN AN.." OLD MAN</p>
        <p>OF THE SEA."</p>
        <p>MR. PARKER AND THE TORY PLIGHT.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>0`;</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>ICBM</p>
        <p>-- Sir—The Daily Mail is of opinion that Mr. Bonar Law's speech at Ashton-under-Lyne "will close the ranks of the Unionist Party.' Instead of closing the ranks it has, and that very natur- ally, further divided them. The difficulty the Leader of the Opposition was faced with was to give a convincing reason why the Referendum Pledge (given so solemnly by Mr. Balfour and endorsed by the other leaders with the hope of sweeping Lancashire) has been withdrawn. Mr. Bonar Law, by an ingenious plan, instead of abolishing the Referendum shifted it from the electors to the Colonies. Mr. Law said that "the question of food taxes will depend upon whether or not the Colonies think them necessary." But surely it ought not to rest with the Colon- ies to say whether or not we tax our food. This ought to be determined by those who have to pay. We have abandoned our-right to tax our Colonies, the insistence of this cost us what are now the United States. Are we going to reverse this and allow the Colonies to determine whether or not we are to tax ourselves? Surely, this is a case of inciting the tail to wag the dog. Be- sides this, the Colonies have declared through their responsible leaders in most explicit terms that they have no intention, and no desire, to interfere in the fiscal policy of the Mother-coun- try. They recognise that it is the right of the people in the country concerned to determine whe- ther their food shall be taxed or not, and it would only create bad blood for them to interfere. They naturally decline to assume a responsibility which could in any sense be interpreted as an interfer- ence with our right in determining our own fiscal policy. To many Conservatives Tariff Reform is at present the "Old Man of the Sea." Many a Lancashire Conservative hoped by means of a Referendum to be able to shake him off —without a serious loss to party credit.. Mr. Bonar Law evidently dislikes the idea of food taxes, or at any rate he thinks the electors dislike them, hence he does not want the odium of putting them on, but he is too much entangled by the Cham- berlain policy to be able to throw this part over- board and so he would shift the responsibility to the Colonies. In other words he wants them to give the initiative for doing a disagreeable thing. The whole business is a huge muddle which gets more entagled with every new move, and the inevitable retribution has come on the party that adopts such a shifting policy, one so full of con- tradictions and so economically unsound. In support of the general theory of Tariff Re- form, interpreted by his party, Mr. Bonar Law makes a contrast of what he affirms to be the trade of Germany and England for the first nine months of 1902 'and 1912. But with the sloppiness characteristic of Tariff Reform statements it is found on examination that the comparison is made between the twelve months of 1902 and the first nine months of 1912. but even were the comparison to be as he states, it would give us very little indica.on of the commercial progress of the two countries. The figures below, compar- ing the years 1902 and 1911 (this being the laht completed year of which we have the imports and exports divided for manufactured goods), give a far safer basis of comparison. INeRFAses BETWEEN 1902-1911.</p>
        <p>Germany's increase of total imports is X192,000,000 Britain's increase of total imports is Germany's increase of total imports is I 168,000,000 Britain's increase of total exports , is Germany's increase of total exported Manuf actnres is Britain's increase of total exported Manufactures is ' 135,000,000 Germany's increase of total imported Manufactures is Britain's increase of total imported Manufactures is , 33,000,000 / Less re exports 11,000,000 £2.2 ,000 ,000 '</p>
        <p>The most significant feature revealed in the above figures is that the imports of manufactured goods, which in spite of the tariff to keep them out, are going into Germany much faster than into Great Britain*.</p>
        <p>Mr. Law said : "In.the United,States and Canada there had been a marked rise in wages during the last five years, Probably larger than in any period of the same extent.' . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Law might have added with equal truth that food prices have risen in protected Countries further than in any similar period. The United States Bureau of Labour shows that real wage' that is wages measured by what they will buy in America, have fallen from a basis of 100 in 1900,t0 98 in 1911. Retail prices of food have risen from 103 to 143. In Germany the rise is from 100 to 142, and wages have not risen in pro- portion. In Great Britain. according to the Board of Trade, food Frices live risen from 100 to 109.3. In dealing with the savings of the two coun- tries Mr. Boner ,Law has again fallen into an oft, repeated and oft corrected mistake of compdr- ing two entirely differe,nt things. The figures Mr. Law gives for Germany refer to public and corporate banks, while for this country he only gives the figures of the Post Office Savings Bank. Before you can institute any fair comparison it is essential that you compare the same things.— Yours, etc., CHARLES E. PARER. • Penketh, Dec. l'rYth, 1912.</p>
        <p>• , lrita is TIRL 1.4 t. Tan .nfL 's -re - • •</p>
