      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS BEWARE I</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS BEWARE I</p>
        <p>TORY CONSPIRACY WIMASKED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Mr. C/ement Edwards' Challenge to Lord Wolrner and Mr., Bonar Law.</head>
        <p>Mr. C/ement Edwards' Challenge to Lord Wolrner and Mr., Bonar Law.</p>
        <p>In accordance with his prcmise at Saturday night's meeting, Mr. Clement Edwards, M.P., has sent us the following letter, stating categorically his reasons for charging Lord Weimer and his friends with being at the head and front Lf a Tory Conspirn.cy to smash the Trade Unions. To LORD WOLMER.</p>
        <p>My Lord,—l received your letter dated December , flth, but after you had sent a copy to the press, and note that you sdy the four specific statements I issued to the Bolton Trade Unionists are all absolutely untrue. This is definite. Let we therefore without waste of words repeat each of these statements and give the proofs of their accuracy. My first statement was that ycu and y. ur Tory colleagues sought by au3ernhuents to the Gsborne Bill, 1. To make Trade Unions subject to Government inqnisition.</p>
        <p>here are the proofs : .(a) Mr. Peto proposed that a ballot for and against political objects should not be takt n at the lodge or office of the Trade Union, but upon employers' premises or if this was impracticable at some place to be appointed by the registrar of Friendly Societies (b) Mr. Ormsby Gore gave notice of an amend- ment proposing that the result of the ballot'abould be notified to the vgistrar of Friendty Societies and that until he grants permission (Nsithin three months) Trade Unions should hot he allowed to give effect to such ballot. Mr. Gore also gave notice of an amend- ment that on complaint received as to the ballot, the registrar might bold an inquiry and receive evidence "in secret ur otherwise,' afterwards altered to " public." (c) You yourself proposed that the ballot was to be taken under the supervision of some person appointed by the registrar of Friendly Societies. (d) You yourself have given notice of a new clause as faovt 8 _</p>
        <p>_ . "The rules of every trade union shall provide for all elections of officers and every cafe of general vote of the members or any part of them to be* by secret ballot and under the same rules and regulations applying to the ballot tor politio*l purposes. (e) You yourself also have on the amendment paper the following clause "The Itealitcur of Friendly. Societies shall audd the colleotioa appropiation and transfer to the political committees or to the benefit fuod (as the , case may be) ot all political contribution." So much then as to you and your c lleagnes' desire to subject trade unions to (I overwment inquisitions : My second statement was, that you ad your Tory - colleagues proposed, 2. Again to make Peaceful Picketing unlawful. My proof of this is to be found in the three sub- sections of Lord Wintertf n's 'proposed new clause hea:.ed " Unlawful Picketing and Penalities." For purpose of clearness and emphasis I print this pro- posal in parallel columns with the existing picketing clause in the 1 rade Disput,s' Act 1906.</p>
        <p>PICKETING CLAUSE LORD WINTERTON'S OF THE TRADE PROPOSED</p>
        <p>DISPErfES ACT Ibo 6.</p>
        <p>NEW CLAUSE.</p>
        <p>It shall be lawful for ore (a) It shall be unlawful or more persons acting on for any person not being their own behalf or on an official of a Trade Union behalf of a Trade Union or whether of masters or of an individual employer or workmen directly affected firm in contemplation or by such strike or lockout, furtherance of a trade die- or a person whose trade, pute, to attend at or near a business or employment house or place where a has been wholly or pefson resides or works or partially stopped in con. carries on business or sequence of such strike or happens to be. if they so lockout,toattend at ornear attend merely for the pur- any house or place where pose of peacefully obtain- any other person resides ing or communicating or works; or carries on information or of peacef illy business or happens to be, persuading any person to with the object of obtain- work or abstain from ing or communicating working, information for the pur- pose of such strike or lockout, or if persuading any person to work or carry on business or abstain from working or carrying on business. (b) It shall be unlaw- ful for more than two persons at the same time to be present at or near any house or place where any other person works or carries en business or happees to be with the object of obtaining or communicating informa- tion for the purpose of obtsiuing or communi- cating information for the purpose of such strike or lockout, or of persuad- ing any person to work or carry on business or abstain from working or carrying on business. (c) It shall be unlaw- ful for any person to attend at or near the house or place where any other person resides with the object of persuading • such other person to work or carry on business or to abstain from work- ing or carrying on business. (2) Any person infring. lug any of the foregoing provisions of this section shell be liable to be arrested without any %%arrant, and on summary conviction to be im- prisoned for a period not exceeding three calendar months with or without hard labour. If this isn't again mak- ing peaceful picketing unlawful the English lan- guage has no meaning. My third statement was that you and your Tory colleagues proposed 3.—To undo the Trade Disputes Act 1906, by making the Funds of the UlllOOB again liable for damages as in the Taff Yale Case.</p>
        <p>Here are the Roofs : The Taff Vale Case decided for the first time, as no doubt you are aware that a Trade Union could he sued in a court of law for the wrongful acts of its agents and as a fact in that crier) the Union had to pay between £lO,OOO and £50,000 by the way of damages and costs for (a) unlawful picketing for (b) procuring breaches of contract (c for interfering in the business of the Taff Vale Railway Company and for conspiracy to injure, I The Trade Disputes Act 1900 reversed all this and provided that what was lawful for one was lawful for many, made picketing lawful, and laid it down that either procurement of breach of contract or mere interference in an employer's business (in connection with a trade dispute) should not be actionable and finally provided by section 4 that " an action against a Trade Union whether of ma,ters or workmen, or agdinst any members or officials thereof on behalf of themselves and all other members of the Trade Union in respect of any tortious act alleged to have been committed by or on behalf of the Trade Union, shall not be entertained by any c,,urts.".</p>
        <p>-Now we have already seen that by Lord Winterton's new clause, Peaceful Picketing is to be an offence and therefore to be an actionable wrong and you yourself by a new clause propose that the procurement of the dismissal of a man in furtherance of a Trade Dispute shall be a misdemeanor and therefore an actionable wrong when done by even one person, so that by the combined operation of yours and Lord Winterton's proposed new clauses you again make actionable those acts which in the Taff Vale Case mulcted the railway servants. Then having done that in such heavy damages and thereby put these acts outside the pale of the statutory objects of the Bill, you boldly propose the following New Clause. " Section 4 of the Trade Disputes Actl9too6rtospa.B 11 not apply to any action in respect of any act ailageri to have been committed in furtherance of any object, other than statutory objects eithjn the meaning of this Act. So as I stated you seek to undo the Trade Disputes Act by making fonds liable as in Taff Vale case, My fourth and last statement was that you and your Tory colleagues proposed (4) To prevent all benefit funds ci trade unions being available for strike pay in time of need.</p>
        <p>The proof of this is again to be found in your Lord- ship's own proposal. In the already quoted clause in which you propose to revive the tight of action for torts against a Trade Union you add a proviso as follows :</p>
        <p>Provided that execution upon any judgment or order by the plaintaff in any such action against any Trade Union shall not extend to the benefit funos of the Union. This would make " Strike" funds liable and benefit funds immune.</p>
        <p>In other words if all funds (benefit and strike) were used as they now are in time of need for strike pay they would he liable to be taken in damages in your lordship's proposed revised actions for torts, RS Y-40,000 was taken from the A.S R,S. and 5.50,000 was taken from the South Wales miners in the Stop Day Action. To avoid this risk they must be ear- marked for benefit purposes and if they are so ear- matictd they cannot be used as strike pay. So much then for the four sp.cific statements con- tained in the poster as to the attitude of yourself and colleagues on the Grand Committee and which I say without hesitation justify the heading Tory Con- spiracy to smash Trade Unionism." But I do not pretend that you and your colleagues stand alone.</p>
        <p>Oa the contrary I believe that what you are doing there, you ate doing as part aud parcel of a general conspiracy by the Tory party against Trade Unionism. Permit me, thereft re to supply you with the grounds of this further belief</p>
        <p>Take the Bill entitled " Trade Disputes Law Amendment Bill " intrcdum,d by Lord Robert Cecil and supported by the following Tory M P.'s : Mr Fletcher, Mr Mills, and Mr Sandys. This Bill propm,es : . _</p>
        <p>• 1: To restore the Civil liability of Prade Unions in respect of tortioua acts committed under the express sanction or rtcognition. This is precisely •to set up Taff Vale again. 2. To limit r icketing to two pickets outside a works and makes it unlawful to picket near a man's residence or where be " h3pperis to be." 3. • .11ppeals clause 4 of the Trade Disputes Act MG which prohibits actions against Tram) Unions. '4. Again makes the procurement of bi each of con- tract except with free IM:touters, actionable. 5. Prooa's to indue Unions to secure part of their funds hem liability for damages by separating them into "Trade" (far dispute purnosea) and "provident " funds but IA hen so separated proposes to make it an Offence, punishable with a fine of till for using provident funds as strike pay whatever the need and suffering in a bitter dispute might be. Again Lord Claud Hamilton, M.P. (chairman of the Great Eastern Railway C).) and a tioqy of leading Conservative MP.' s like Sir Henry Crake, Sir Philip Magnus (your helpful colleague on the Grand Com- mittee), Mr Paget, Mr Bourgoyne and Mr Burdett- Coutts have presented a. Bill which proposes: 1. To revive • the right of action against Trade Un ions.</p>
        <p>2 To make Peaceful Picketing Unlawful (Lord Winterton's proposed unlawful picketing clause in the Osborne Bill is lifted bodily from this Bill of Lord Claud framilton). 3. To repeal the picketing clause of the 'bade Disputes Act. 4. To again make actionable procurements of breach of contract or of unreasonable interference with a business or lidihood in connection with a strike.</p>
        <p>5. To alter Trade Unions, so that members -may enforce payment of benefits in Courts of Law although the Union is not to have the corresponding right to recover contributions.</p>
        <p>• In conclusion let me say that when the leaders of the Conservative Party hare formally repudiated the action of yourself and the Tory members on the Grand Committee, have repudiated Lord Robert Cecil and his Bill, and have repudiated Lord Claud Hamilton and his Bill, and have repudiated Mr Leslie and his letter to the 'Times," I shall begin to believe that His conspiracy to sinish Trade Unionism is con- fined to ooly a portion of the Tory party. I am seiading this letter to the press.—Yours truly, (Signed) CLE MEN T Ell WARDS, 4, Pump Court, Temple. December 10th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Mr. Clement Edwards' Challenge to</head>
        <p>Mr. Clement Edwards' Challenge to</p>
        <p>Mr. Bonar Law.</p>
        <p>The Tory conspiracy to smash trade unionism was the subject of some outspoken comments by Mr Clement Edwards, MP,, at the mass meeting held in the Co-operative Hall, under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association, on Saturday evening. He began by referring to his intervention in the Bolton election and said Lord Wolmer, with the special politeness which characterised the present generation of the Cecilian family, had described his statements as lies. Having promised a reply to Lord Wolmer, through the columns of the "Examiner," Mr Edwards said his charge against the Tory party was not based upon any isolated iustance. A Bill bhd been passing through Grand Committee of the House of Commons which had for its object the reversal of the Osborne judgment, in part, and the restoration to trade unions of the power of carrying on political activities subject to the protection of the rights of the minority. They had sat discussing the bill a great many more days than they ought to have done, because there had been on the part of Lord Wolmer, Lord Winterton, and other members of the Tory party a policy of deliberate obstruction. They had been trying to limit the political powers of trade unions so as to make them futile and ineffective. That was not the worst. They had endeavoured, under the cloak of amendments to the mere political proposals of the bill, to destroy the industrial power of trade unions by getting in clauses having for their object the destruction of the Trade Disputes Act, 1906. One proposal was that peaceful persuasion and picketing should again be made unlawful. Another was that it should be unlawful to picket anywhere near a man's house and, in the phrase of the clause, " where a man happens to be." (Laughter.) Li OW they could do any picketing that was any good ' unless it was in the vicinity of where the man they wanted to picket bappene4 to be, be did not know. Secondly there were only to be two pickets for each works where a strike was in progress. Imagine two pickets at the Crewe works! (Laughter.) And those pickets were to be marked men by being compelled to wear badges Another proposition by Lord Wolmer was that the funds which a trade union used for the purpose of strikes should again be liable tabe taken under an action at law, just as the funds of the railway servants were taken in the great Taff Vale Ca9o of twelve years ago. Lord Weimer also proposed that the strike funds should be divided from the benefit funds, so that the latter could not be used for the purpose of strike pay, however great might be the need and suffering under a prolonged dispute. The power of using all the funds of a trade union indis- criminately in time of need had been as the very life blood of the unions of this country. There was scarcely a union which could carry on a dispute for any length of time if it were deprived of the power cf borrowing frcm the provident funds. Having referred to Bills introduced by various members , of the Tory party with the object of undoing the Trades Disputes Act, Mr Edwards said that if to the Tory members who were backing those Bills, they added the Tories who had been seeking to destroy the Trade Unions Bills in Grand Committee, they had a total of something like 5,5 Tory members who were at that game. Mr Bonar Law was going to Ashton-under-Lyne this week, and it had been announced that he would deal expressly with this trade union question, as it had been found very damaging in the Northern constitu- ences. He (Mr. Edwards) had been denounced for , what he did at Bolton. "When Mr Bonar Law," con- tined Mr. Edwards, "starts out to repeat that my 'statement that there is a Tory conspiracy to smash trade unionism is untrue, I ask him specifically : Does he repudiate the action of Lord Wolmer and the other appointed representatives of the Tory Party on the Grand Committee dealing with the Trade Unions Bill? Does he repudiate the Bill of Lord Robert Cecil for undoing the Act of 1906? Does be repudiate the Bills of Sir Frederick Banbury and Lord Claud Hamilton for destroying the Act of 1906? And does he, above and beyond all, repudiate the threats of Mr. Leslie Scott, a Tory</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>_l,</p>
        <p>member for one of the divisions of. Liverpool, in the Times' that the industrial powers of trade unions must Ile reduced, and the threats of the Daily Mail' and the 'Times' against trade unionism during the past three weeks? If he does not repudiate all this, then the charge stands that the Tory party is up to its eyes in this conspiracy against the trade unions of the country." (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>. . • ; •r • •'• ' V • _ –</head>
        <p>. . • ; •r • •'• ' V • _ -</p>
        <p>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Another Striking Success.</p>
        <p>People's College concert has long been regarded as a model of what the successful school concert should be. The scholars have therefore a unique reputation to maintain and we can accord the concert held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening no higher praise than to state that it was fully in keeping with the tine record on which all concerned justly pride themselves. Nor is this mere fulsome flattery. The cordially expressed .delight of an audience accustomed to expect great things on these occasions, was in itself convincing testimony to the excellence of an entertainment which combined exceptional musical merit, pleasing vatiety and sparkling originality in an altogether admirable way. Despite changes in the public taste, the passing of years seeme but to strengthen the hold of this annual event upon the estimation of the townspeople. The attendance or Thursday evening was again most gratifying, every part of the hall being well tilled, and the representative character of the assembly made it , clear that the marked influence which this school has for so many years exercised on the educational life of Warringteu, is fully rec)gnized and appreciated. The children themselves—perfectly disciplined yet proudly happy—presented a bright and animated picture as they took their places on the platform, and later the ensemble gained in attractiveness from the dashes of colour imparted by groups of characters in fancy costumes. The fresh young voices of the chorus declaimed " Loyal lads and lasses' ((Joleman)--a well-chosen openingiteai— in stirring and vigorous style This was succeeded by the chief choral feature of the evening—the cantata " Walther von der Vogelweid. " Rathbone's b,autiful setting of Longfellow's poetic version of the Minnesinger legend is an ambitious work for the average school choir to attempt, for not only does it test their capabilities as pirt-singers but the tempo is such as to necessitate much painstaking training. However, under Mr Payton's tactful guidance, the children came through their trying task with infinite credit to themselves and their instructor. Generally speaking, the high notes in which the composition abounds were attained with remarkable clearness and accuracy, and the two voice •parts hartnonized well. The expressiveness of the singing called for special commendation and altogether the performance was a revelation of what can be done when something really worthy of supreme endeavour' is attempted in a persevering spirit. The chorus figured prominently and with great credit in the remainder of the programme, their items including "The Island of Dreams" (Stephen Adams), " The Admiral's Yarn " (Paul A. Rubens), which went with a rare swing and was greatly relished by the audience, "The motor car' (Denzl)—the scene of the adven- tures being cnnveniently localised—arid "The Trumpeter ' (J. Airlie Dix.) A touch of realism was given to the last-named by the cornet obligato, perfr-ctly played by Mr W Rutter. Trio concert was brought to a fitting close with the carol "In the field " (Farmer), interpreted in a devotional and finished manner.</p>
        <p>There was no lack cf bright and well-conceived novelties—in fact, one can oaly marvel that the teachers find it possible to devise so much that is new and delighting, alike from the spectacular and musical standpoints, for each successive concert. Their enterprise seems to know no limit. This time the feature which met with the highest degree of acceptance was an original dance medley, prepared by Misses Wallington and Townley. The movements were executed with exquisite grace and skill by a contingent of girls in dainty white dresses. The last dance was the hornpipe which aroused quite a furore of appreciation. From begining to end the entire series of dances quite charmed tie audit nee, and the high state of proficiency which the performers had attained spoke volumes for the thoroughness of their training. Another troupe of girls, smartly attired in red, gave the action song "The girls in red" with natural freedom, the sly digs at the " militant " apparently voicing the sentiments of the listeners who heartily acclaimed the item. The girls had been trained by Miss Brown to whom als., belongs the credit of writing the song. Everybody was charmed with the simplicity of "Good- night" (Mann) sung by eight tiny maids, clad in night- dresses and carrying dolls and candles. With each verse the number of participants gradually diminished until at last only one was left. When she had taken her departure and all the little ones weTe apparently wrapt in slumber, a fairy tripped on to the scene and, at a wave of her magic wand, they again sprang to life and activity. The number was gone through with such an absence of]self-consciousness and a true realization of the child-like spirit of the piece as to ensure its complete success, and Miss Broom- head and Miss White, who were responsible for its preparation, are deserving of hearty congratulations. We were transported to the romantic days of yore by the anion song " Gentlemen of the Road (Clement Lockwood), for which Miss Brown had trained the performers. Bold, masked highwaymen, in traditional " Dick Ttp pin" uniform and equipment, make an attack on a coach, to the great discomfiture of the five ladies in rich brocades, who rush shrieking on to the platform. Their resentment, however, speedily gives way to admiration for the courtliness of the robbers, and the party join in treading the stately minuet. A group of coons, with coal-black countenances and spotlessly white suits, sang "The Dandy-Coloured Coon" (Le Brunn), and comported themselves with smartness and sang froid. Their leader—A Johnson, resplendent in distinctive red uniform, took tbo solo part very effectively. Altogether this novelty was a triumph of "make up" and well-drilled arrange- ment'for which Mr I Bowen merited hearty thanks. Harold Cross, Stanley Spittle, Tho mas Tomlinson, and Ernest Brown described " What a beautiful world this would be" (IV entwrrth), if only each could have his own pet aversion removed. As a laughter- maker "The Dentists' den "(Lewis) took high rank. Tne manipulators of the forceps had expeditious lady assistants and a multitude of wailing clients were relieved of amazingly large molars with a promptitude and dispatch which would have made the average dentist green with envy. Boys and girls took their parts a to the manner born thanks to the direction of their snatural aptitude by Miss Barringer and Miss Peters.</p>
        <p>Pope's College invariably has good soloists at its command and on Thursday two sch,,lars were to the fore in this respect. Ada Knight sang Edward German's delightfully tuneful composition " Who'll buy my lavender?" very sweetly and unaffectedly. Dora Hope is a vocalist of considerable promise and she infused real feeling into her interpretation of the plaintive " Where my Caravan has rested," (Lohr) which afforded an exceptional degree of pleasure to the audience.</p>
        <p>Two humorous recitations added considerably to the lighter side of the programme. Frank Dowling told with the utmost self-possession of the trials of the dutiful husband who wanders through an establish- ment of irritatingly numerous departments, to execute a little commission for his wife. Donald Dunhavand relited "The street watchman's story"—the pathetic record of the trials of the workman doomed to watch a ole in the street "on Christmas Day, only to be deluded with a dazzling dream of feasting and revelry ' He wore the rough clothing of a sou of toil afid recited in a distinct and unstilted way. Throughout the entertainment interest never flagged for a moment and appreciation found its vent in the quick succession of encores; every item, with the exception of the choruses, having to be repeated. Mr E Wallington, the respected headmaster, and his staff, have reason, to be highly gratified at the addition of another conspicuous success to a brilliant list. In particular we would mention the invaluable wprk of Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bac., the popular music master, who must have expended an infinitude of labour on the rehearsals. His methods of handling the forces at his disposal aro the embodiment of tact and patience and at the concert he played the pianoforte accompani- ments with his usual Judgment and good taste. The Parr Hall organ was again in such an unsatisfactory state that it was deemed advisable to leave it severely ' alone. Before the concert concluded, Mr C Jackson Holmes (one of the school managers) cordially acknow- ledged the co-operation of the parents and staff which had been such an important factor in the success of the concert. He paid a special tribute to the work of Mr Payton without whom, he said, there would be no concert. He was a most able musician and his work in connection with that enterprise was undoub- tedly a labour of love. Ile had been so long associated with the school that the concert had become part of his very existence. Continuing Mr Holmes expressed satisfaction that the managers were able to retain the</p>
        <p>11.11111•1111</p>
        <p>...,</p>
