      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-12-21_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>MR. OWEN TOOLE.</head>
        <p>MR. OWEN TOOLE.</p>
        <p>Presentation of 011 Portrait and Address</p>
        <p>A large and enthusiastic gathering of members and friends met in the rooms of the Catholic Young Men's Society on Thursday evening on the occasion of a presentation to Bro Owen Toole. Mr McKinley (vice- president), presided. The MAvon (Councillor Dr Joseph) presented Mr Toole, on behalf of the society, with a portrait in oils of himself (Mr Toole). His Worship spoke in glowing terms of Mr Toole's sterling work in the Council during his six years of office and attested to his high standard of honour and sense of duty ; also the great loss the town had sustained by his removal. Mr TooLF. responded in suitable terms and paid a high trioute to his colleagues on the Council. No question of religion had been broached during his six years of office. The SECRETARY (Mr T Stirrup) then presented an illuminated address on behalf of the society and spoke of Mr Toole's sterling work for the organization. Mr M Cunningham then presented Mr Toole with a clock and vases in green Brazilian onixe marble. He spoke very feelingly as one who, from childhood onwards had been a bosom friend of Mr Toole. He spoke of Mr Toole's grand work in the society and in the Council Chamber. He -asked Mr Toole to accept the timepiece as some little token of esteem and grati- tude from the society. He Faso asked Mr Toole to. accept on behalf of Mrs Toole a gold bracelet pre- sented by the society. Mr A M CROW; in a.capital speech bubbling over with incidents, supported Mr Cunningham. He touched on Mr Toole's public life and especially on his invalu- able work on • behalf of Catholicity during the Educe- , tional crisis of 1906.</p>
        <p>Mr W KILDUFF also supported. Mr TOOLE feelingly responded. What little be bad had the good fortune to do for the society in the past, he would endeavour to more than double in the near future. He spoke of the work of the society and the broad minds of the members, this being shown by the fact that they had present that night representa- tives of both shades of political belief and of all the different religions. A long musical entertainment was rendered by Messrs Boscow, Hannon, Mather, Clay, Kilduff Delaney, Cain, Handley, Flynn, Connolly, Bailey, Anderton, Gleeson Nowns, Toole, Cunningham, Hogan, Williams. Mr E A Sanders gave some splendid comic recitations while Mr Val Vousden's impersonations of Dickens characters were very much appreciated. A presentation to Mr Toole from the United Irish League is reported on the previous page.</p>
        <p>Private Davis, of the 4th Fusiliers (City of London) Regiment, died at Aldershot on Wednesday as the result of an accident during gymnastic practice. Davis was attempting the vaulting-horse exercise when he fell and broke hie back.</p>
        <p>DEATH.</p>
        <p>ECCLES —On the morning of December 16th, at 18, Causeway-avenue, Phebe Ann, the dearly beloved wife of Christopher Eccles, aged 49 years. IN MEMORIAM.</p>
        <p>In loving memory of our dear father, Joirs WAYMAN ROBERTS, who departed this life, December 20th, 1910. Sleep on dear father, your trials are o'er Your weary hands will toil no more On earth there's strife, in Heaven rest, We miss you most, who loved you best. Prom Daughters and Son. In loving memory of my dear wife ANNIE MARIA STARKEY, who passed peacefully away on December 22nd. 1011. "Her love lives on and hath a power to bless, While she who lived is hidden in the grave. Through suffering and sorrow thou bast passed To show God's gift a woman true may be."</p>
