        <p>BLACKBURN BOMBSHELL. Prominent Conservative ott Party's Hopeless Task. Considerable surprise was occasioned at Blackburn on Tuesday by the resignation of Mr. Thomas Marsden, a promLeat solicitor and Anti-Socialist author, from the Conservattve Association through disagreement With the party's fiscal pdlicy. In his letter he declared that the Unionist party had retrogressed considerably since the decline of Mr Joseph Chamberlain, and had returned to the position the party rccupied before Disraeli's leadership. Mr. Marsden adds "I do not think the Unionist party ut.der the present regime can hope to reach the heart of an intelligent enlightened democracy such as exists in these islands at the present day." • GENERAL MACKINNON AND THE TERRITORIALS. General Sir Henry Mackinnon, the general cancer Commanding the Western command, was the guest en Saturday at the annual dinner of the Cheshire and North Wales Volunteer and Long Service Medalli sts' ' Association. He said the special reser% es had not been taken up much by the counties and ware certainly not BO popular as the old militia. Still, he had hopes t bat When the country got accustomed to the new name and conditions of service they would take them up and fill their ranks, and make them what they cught to b' most valuable asset for the regular army. The Territorial Force was going fairly strong, and he was not at all disappointed with what had taken place lest Year. Two difficulties were the strikes and the bad Weather, which made camping last summer unpleas tnt lie could not speak top highly of the very fine spirit shown by the win,le of the Territorials under most un?omfortable and depress;ng surroundings in the</p>
